      "id": "bbc-2026-11-24-fa-chief-signs-nda-to-view-fifa-sell-off-plot-documents",
      "title": "FA chief signs NDA to view Fifa sell-off plot documents",
      "summary": "Debbie Hewitt is among several football chiefs to view documents made available by Fifa, but cannot speak about them.",
      "body": "Football Association chair Debbie Hewitt has signed a non-disclosure agreement (NDA) as a condition of viewing documents related to Fifa president Gianni Infantino's failed World Cup sell-off plot.Hewitt was granted access to the information at the world governing body's headquarters in Zurich.Earlier this month, Fifa said \"key documents\" on the abandoned plan to sell stakes in its tournaments to private investors would be made available to members of its council, but only in person and under \"strict review protocols to preserve confidentiality\".Uefa had demanded Fifa release details about the controversial Fifa Forward Enterprise (FFE) proposal, and answer questions relating to its decision-making.Hewitt was among a group of council members who wrote to Fifa to say they were \"concerned that any review would seemingly be limited to certain 'key documents'… which in turn would only be available for review under unknown 'strict review protocols'\".They also asked for clarity on what the protocols were, and \"an explanation as to why Fifa considers such restrictions necessary since it is not clear why our rights are being limited in such a way\".The NDA means Hewitt cannot reveal what she has read, or comment about it publicly.Fair Square, which campaigns for greater accountability in sport, told BBC Sport: \"Debbie Hewitt and the FA didn't need to see the details of FFE to know that it was a gross abuse of presidential power.\"As a member association, the FA is entitled to hold the Fifa president accountable for his actions, and as a Fifa Council vice-president, Hewitt should have seen these plans as soon as they were hatched.\"They had the right to demand to see these documents, and should have exercised that right forcefully and publicly, rather than signing a gagging clause that puts Fifa and Infantino back in control.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
