      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-24-ex-nvidia-partner-megaspeed-hit-with-u-s-commerce-department",
      "title": "Ex-Nvidia partner Megaspeed hit with U.S. Commerce Department export ban",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.upi.com/Top_News/US/2026/11/24/NVIDIA-partner-Megaspeed/1701795520063/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50630245\nPoints: 9\n# Comments: 2",
      "body": "Megaspeed International, the Singaporean firm at the center of the NVIDIA-Jensen Huang controversy, has been placed on a U.S. blacklist of firms who American businesses are prohibited from trading with without special authorization. Photo by How Hwee Young/EPA\nNov. 24 (UPI) -- Megaspeed International, the Singaporean firm at the center of the NVIDIA-Jensen Huang controversy, has been placed on a U.S. blacklist of firms that American businesses are prohibited from trading with without special authorization.\nThe Commerce Department notice, which is scheduled to be published in the Federal Register on Wednesday, means Megaspeed, together with related entities in Thailand and Malaysia, will no longer be able to purchase U.S. exports without a license from the department -- which would likely be refused.\nThe notice said the seven entities were being added to the blacklist because the government had determined they were \"acting contrary to the national interest or foreign policy interests of the United States.\"\nNVIDIA partner Megaspeed, already the focus of a U.S. export controls probe into whether it transferred U.S.-made microchips to China since 2024, was catapulted into the global spotlight after it was alleged NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang had an extramarital affair with Alice Huang, a Chinese-born senior executive of the firm who allegedly was a spy for Beijing.\nMegaspeed has previously said it was a law-abiding and legitimate business that always complied with U.S. export control rules and that Alice Huang had not worked at the company since August 2025.\nMegaspeed is being investigated separately by authorities in Singapore, with Prime Minister Lawrence Wong telling journalists Monday night that the probe was to discover if the firm had broken any of the city-state's laws but that an earlier investigation of Alice Huang had turned up nothing to warrant any action against her.\nThe ban comes 11 days after Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent complained that he and others in the Trump administration had been pushing for export restrictions to be placed on Megaspeed, deeming it a national security threat, and questioned the delay in doing so by the Commerce Department.\nU.S. Sen. Pete Ricketts, R-Neb., on Tuesday demanded the Commerce Department provide Congress with answers as to why it took so long to act.\nPresident Donald Trump makes comments as he holds a press briefing at the White House on Friday. Trump was joined by Vice President JD Vance and Secretary of State Marco Rubio. Photo by Yuri Gripas/UPI | License Photo\t\t \t\tRead More\nStock markets climb with news of Huang's return to Nvidia\nNvidia reinstates Jensen Huang as CEO, president, board director\nTrump: Jensen Huang had 'no idea' woman was Chinese spy\nNvidia launches probe into reported Chinese spy effort targeting CEO\nFormer NVIDIA boss Huang, Commerce Secretary Lutnick summoned by Congress",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "nephihaha",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T16:08:29.000Z",
