      "id": "theverge-2026-11-24-wyze-s-new-window-feeder-records-birds-from-a-camera-inside-",
      "title": "Wyze’s new window feeder records birds from a camera inside your home",
      "summary": "A year ago, Wyze introduced a $35 security camera that stuck to the inside of a window so you could keep an eye on what was happening outside your home without a complicated installation. Today the company announced its new Window Bird Cam, which expands on that idea by pairing a similar window-mounted camera with a feeder that attaches to the outside of the glass. Alongside the kit, Wyze is also ",
      "body": "A year ago, Wyze introduced a $35 security camera that stuck to the inside of a window so you could keep an eye on what was happening outside your home without a complicated installation. Today the company announced its new Window Bird Cam, which expands on that idea by pairing a similar window-mounted camera with a feeder that attaches to the outside of the glass. Alongside the kit, Wyze is also launching a new subscription service called Bird Watch, which adds identification capabilities and features that will benefit busy yards.\nThe Wyze Window Bird Cam is available today through the company’s online store for $69.98 and includes the outdoor feeder with suction cups for mounting, a small tray for holding seeds, and a cover for protection from the elements. Wyze is also selling an optional $14.98 accessory called the Window Bird Cam Perch that gives birds a ”natural landing spot right in the camera’s portrait sweet spot,“ plus a fruit fork, suet holder, and a jelly cup to attract more visitors.\n[Image: The optional perch accessory adds more room for birds to land plus holders for fruit and suet to attract more visitors. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/11/wyzebird2.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nBy installing inside your home and plugging into a USB power source (using an included 10-foot cable) the Bird Cam doesn’t need a battery, doesn’t need to be weatherproof, and will be well in range of your Wi-Fi network. Instead of the wide-angle view offered by most security cameras, the lens on the Bird Cam is designed to focus at a distance of 4 to 8 inches so birds look crisp while the rest of your yard is softly blurred.\n[Image: Without concerns for battery life, the Bird Cam records videos of visitors at 30fps. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/11/wyzebird3.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nOut of the box, the Bird Cam will detect motion and provide instant bird arrival notifications while 30fps video is recorded locally to a microSD card up to 512GB. You can also watch and share a livestream from the camera through a mobile device. The arrival notifications do have a five-minute cooldown so if you want to ensure you don’t miss any visitors, you’ll need to upgrade to Bird Watch for $6.99 per month, or $69.99 per year. The subscription adds species identification with a confidence score and access to an interactive bird wiki, full-length videos of bird visits stored in the cloud for 60 days, and no cooldown period between notifications.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Cameras",
        "Gadgets",
        "News",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Andrew Liszewski",
