      "id": "bbc-2026-11-24-man-baffled-after-accidentally-travelling-to-spain-on-wife-s",
      "title": "Man baffled after accidentally travelling to Spain on wife's passport",
      "summary": "Ben Moore says he is still baffled over how he was able to make the journey without being stopped.",
      "body": "A man who mistakenly travelled to Spain using his wife's passport has said he is still baffled about how he was able to make the trip without being stopped in the UK.Ben Moore, from Talke, in Staffordshire, noticed there was a problem soon after getting off his Ryanair flight while he was queuing to get his passport checked at Alicante airport.\"I opened up the passport there and, to my horror, it was my wife's photo on the passport,\" he said. \"The lads who I was with basically said the colour drained out of me immediately.\"Third-party provider Swissport, which carries out Ryanair's checks at Manchester Airport where Moore flew from, said it was now reinforcing mandatory boarding procedures.Moore, who was travelling to Alicante on a stag party with friends, told BBC Radio Stoke he initially thought he must have picked up his wife's passport in addition to his own.He said he approached Spanish police who - along with the language barrier - appeared to be as confused as he was about how he had turned up there without his own passport.\"I searched through all my luggage about eight times thinking there's no way I could have got to Alicante without my own passport,\" he said.\"They could not believe that I'd got to Spain on my wife's passport.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T14:04:14.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/7d20/live/bb56d2f0-e777-11f1-8f1d-19326d289ceb.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmzx9ygq1x2wo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-11-24-bambu-s-five-nozzle-p3c-isn-t-quite-the-3d-printer-i-expecte",
      "title": "Bambu’s five-nozzle P3C isn’t quite the 3D printer I expected",
      "summary": "The Bambu Lab P3C. | Image: Bambu Lab\t\n\nThree weeks ago, Bambu Lab introduced the state of the art in home printing tech at $2,499 - now, you can get many of its fancy new features for less than half the price. \nLike that bigger H3C, the new $1,149 Bambu P3C can also swap between materials on the fly, mix different colors to produce much of the rainbow, mix soft and hard materials, even mix differ",
      "body": "Three weeks ago, Bambu Lab introduced the state of the art in home printing tech at $2,499 — now, you can get many of its fancy new features for less than half the price.\nLike that bigger H3C, the new $1,149 Bambu P3C can also swap between materials on the fly, mix different colors to produce much of the rainbow, mix soft and hard materials, even mix different sizes of nozzle and different diameters of filament so you can switch between high speed and fine detail in a single print.\nBut Bambu cut more corners than I expected to hit the lower $1,149 / £999 / €1,149 launch price today, and I think they made more of a tinkerer’s 3D printer than one suited for either beginners or pros.\n[Image: A size comparison between the H3C, P3C, and P2S in my garage. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/11/bambu-p3c-IMG_20261123_202905_045-1.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nSome of the changes were completely expected, like its smaller 256 x 256 x 260mm print area (much like Bambu’s other P-series printers), and that it supports seven automatically swapping tools instead of nine. (Seven is still quite a lot when affordable toolchangers like the $849 Snapmaker U1 typically offer just four.)\nBut unlike the H3C, the P3C doesn’t come fully loaded, and it doesn’t load itself. While it has slots for seven rolls of filament and up to seven independent tools (including thicker-than-usual 2.85mm filament, or pens, cutting blades, and debossing and foil transfer tools if you want it to double as a Cricut), it only comes with five 0.4mm print nozzles by default.\n[Image: You can get seven, but it doesn’t come with seven, and you have to load the filament by hand. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/11/AMS_3P.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nAnd while five is still one more color than you’d get with an $800 Bambu P2S Combo, the 3D printer I’d buy for myself, the Bambu P3C’s automated material system (AMS) is the first Bambu has shipped without any motors to automatically load your filament.\nDon’t get me wrong: You can still keep five colors of filament ready to go at a moment’s notice, one more than the P2S, by keeping them loaded into the P3C’s five independent nozzles. You can also buy and fairly easily install two more printheads as well, though they cost up to $100 each.\nBut loading those nozzles is a slow and surprisingly manual process where you have to shove a string of filament down a tube, quite a long distance by hand, after waiting for the machine to preheat each one, and it feels like a step backwards after years using the earlier motorized versions of Bambu’s AMS. Then again, the P3C doesn’t have to spend time and filament purging between colors like the P2S does.\nI expect many 3D printing enthusiasts will see $1,149 for the Bambu P3C as a pretty good deal, assuming they haven’t written off the company’s gadgets after Bambu’s open source snafus. Even five nozzles is enough to load all five rolls of Bambu’s “PLA Palette” cyan, magenta, yellow, black, and white (CMYKW) filaments for color mixing to produce beautiful creations like the company’s new hummingbird test print, which came out almost flawlessly on my first try.\nBut you should know that color mixing isn’t necessarily a foolproof way to produce some colors, like the brown tree branch that’s supposed to be the hummingbird’s perch, and I’m not looking forward to manually pulling and pushing a roll of brown filament to change that. Bambu says I should probably try a feature called “mixed color sublayer” for better results, and I also suppose I could daisy-chain a normal motorized AMS to one of the P3C’s printheads, as that is a supported workflow. But that’s why I’m suggesting we’re in tinkerer territory.\nI asked Bambu about the decision to not include filament-loading motors in the P3C’s AMS system, and here’s part of the statement I got back from spokesperson Karlie Hou: “The AMS 3P’s design deliberately omits the auto‑loading feature as part of the effort to offer premium multi‑head performance at roughly half the price of the flagship H3C system, allowing a far broader audience — hobbyists, small studios, and educational institutions — to enjoy premium multi‑head, multi‑color printing experience.”\nThe company says it should take around five minutes to manually reload five nozzles. “In the next [software] update, we aim to shrink the total hands‑on time for loading five spools to under one minute,” the company writes, though that seems like a tall order.\nMaybe I’ll put the P3C to a year-long test like I did with the P2S, and see how often I actually need something CMYKW can’t do for me, and whether the filament stays dry when left in the printheads for a long time. The question is: What printers should I test against? Are there any upcoming ones I should wait for?",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Gadgets",
        "Hands-on",
        "Reviews",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Sean Hollister",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T14:00:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/11/KV.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=11.782117505974,17.840926115167,81.791662183126,62.982664592544",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/1041034/bambu-p3c-3d-printer-price-release-date",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
