    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-24-baby-p-s-mother-given-open-prison-recommendation",
      "title": "Baby P's mother given open prison recommendation",
      "summary": "Tracey Connelly was first sentenced in 2009 over the death of her toddler son Peter.",
      "body": "Image source, ITV NewsImage caption, Peter Connelly suffered more than 50 injuries, despite being on the at-risk registerThe mother of the toddler known as Baby P - who died after months of abuse - has been told she will not be freed from jail, although the Parole Board has recommended she should be moved to an open prison.Tracey Connelly, now 45, was jailed in 2009 for causing or allowing the death of her 17-month-old son Peter at their home in Tottenham, north London, in August 2007. Connelly has been freed twice, in October 2013 and July 2022, and recalled to prison both times, in February 2015 and August 2024.Public parole hearings took place last October, but the case was adjourned after the panel decided there needed to be a further psychological assessment.Image source, Met PoliceImage caption, Tracey Connelly has been freed on licence on two previous occasions Peter suffered more than 50 injuries, despite being on the at-risk register and receiving 60 visits from social workers, police officers and health professionals over eight months.Connelly's then-boyfriend Steven Barker and his brother, Jason Owen, were also convicted of causing or allowing the death of 17-month-old Peter.Giving evidence to the Parole Board review, which was livestreamed from prison, Connelly admitted she had been a \"horrendous\" mother who \"knew deep down\" that Barker was abusing her son, but she had wanted her Prince Charming and her children \"paid for that\".She said she never intended to live with anyone again or be around other people's children. \"I want to be the CEO of my own home and my own life,\" she told the review.At the Old Bailey in 2009, Connelly was given an indeterminate sentence for public protection (IPP), which allowed prisoners to be held beyond their minimum term and kept on licence for years after release. Such sentences were abolished in 2012, and recent reforms mean licences now end after a set period without recall. The panel said that but for these reforms it would have released her again on strict licence conditions.The Parole Board said that \"somewhat ironically\" a measure meant to address the unfairness of IPP sentences had made it harder to free Connelly.'Recommendation only'Every professional who gave evidence supported her release, but lawyers for the Justice Secretary Alex Norris opposed it, citing Connelly's history of deception, her failure to disclose relationships and an assessment rating her a high risk of serious harm to children.A spokesperson for the Parole Board said: \"This was a recommendation only and the secretary of state for justice considers the advice before making the final decision on whether a prisoner is suitable for open conditions.\"We will only make a recommendation for open conditions if a Parole Board panel is satisfied that the risk to the public has reduced sufficiently to be manageable in an open prison.\"More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T13:02:41.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/7ba1/live/832ce060-af31-11f0-b1c6-736a05ed5483.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwn70z7ryp8ro?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-11-24-uber-nuro-and-lucid-kick-off-fully-driverless-robotaxi-testi",
      "title": "Uber, Nuro, and Lucid kick off fully driverless robotaxi testing",
      "summary": "Uber's high-stakes robotaxi bet is officially ditching its human safety net. Nuro, Lucid, and Uber are starting fully driverless public road testing in the San Francisco Bay Area, with employee-only rides launching shortly after.\nFirst announced in July 2025, this three-way alliance represents Uber's aggressive comeback in autonomous ride-hailing, after having abandoned its own troubled in-house e",
      "body": "Uber’s high-stakes robotaxi bet is officially ditching its human safety net. Nuro, Lucid, and Uber are starting fully driverless public road testing in the San Francisco Bay Area, with employee-only rides launching shortly after.\nFirst announced in July 2025, this three-way alliance represents Uber’s aggressive comeback in autonomous ride-hailing, after having abandoned its own troubled in-house efforts years ago. By pulling human safety operators from behind the wheel of Lucid Gravity SUVs, the companies say they are clearing a critical hurdle toward a public launch. That debut is now slated for early 2027, marking a slight slip from the partnership’s original late-2026 target. When it finally goes live, it will pit Uber directly against entrenched rivals like Waymo and Zoox on their home turf.\nDriverless testing kicks off across four South Bay cities—Palo Alto, Mountain View, Los Altos, and Sunnyvale—before expanding across the broader Bay Area next year. To fuel this push, Lucid has delivered more than 100 upgraded Gravity SUVs, up from the 75 engineering vehicles on the road earlier this year.\nThe automaker began building pre-production models in October to clear regulatory safety approvals, and it plans to start manufacturing the actual commercial fleet in the coming weeks. Each SUV is powered by Nuro’s Level 4 “Nuro Driver” system, pairing cameras, solid-state lidar, and radar with a roof-mounted LED “halo” that flashes a rider’s initials and pickup status.\nRemoving the human backup comes after nearly a year of round-the-clock supervised testing in the Bay Area and Houston. Before letting the cars loose on public streets alone, Nuro put the software through grueling, high-risk edge cases on a closed track in Las Vegas to prove it could handle the unpredictable chaos of real-world driving.\nThe robotaxis are scheduled to hit the Uber app for Bay Area riders in the first quarter of 2027, with Houston following later that year. Looking further ahead, Uber has committed to buying at least 35,000 Lucid vehicles—including the automaker’s upcoming midsize model—over the coming years as it races to scale a global autonomous fleet.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Autonomous Cars",
        "News",
        "Ride-sharing",
        "Transportation",
        "Uber"
      ],
      "author": "Andrew J. Hawkins",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T13:00:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/11/Driverless-Image-2.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,3.4613147178592,100,93.077370564282",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/transportation/1040676/uber-nuro-lucid-driverless-robotaxi-testing-bay-area",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-24-the-rest-of-the-world-noticed-welshman-donald-demolishes-aus",
      "title": "'The rest of the world noticed' - Welshman Donald demolishes Aussies",
      "summary": "A remarkable opening partnership of 150 in 10 overs between Aneurin Donald and Phil Salt helps England chase 184 against Australia to level the T20 series.",
      "body": "Second T20, CarraraAustralia 184-8 (20 overs): Chaudhary 45 (35); Curran 2-29 England 188-2 (13.1 overs): Donald 85 (32); Meredith 1-18England won by eight wickets; series level 1-1ScorecardFrom Gorseinon to the Gold Coast to global recognition. Welshman Aneurin Donald's demolition of Australia in the second T20 will make the rest of the world notice his talents and pave the way for numerous franchise deals, according to former England captain Michael Vaughan.Donald's astonishing opening partnership of 151 in 10 overs with Phil Salt helped England hammer Australia and levelled the T20 series on the Gold Coast, as they chased 184 in just 13.1 overs.Donald blitzed 85 from 32 balls, including nine sixes, while his compatriot Salt made 58 off 30 as the pair from small Welsh villages in Swansea and Denbighshire took the Australians apart. Donald's 18-ball fifty was the joint third-fastest for England in the format as the pair raced to 79-0 in the powerplay, flogging Australia's second-string bowling attack to all corners of the ground in a remarkable display of power hitting.\"It was a night where we saw a player really step up to the big stage,\" Vaughan told BBC Sport.\"I'm sure we're going to see much more over the next few years from Donald, but I think tonight the rest of the world noticed and said 'oh, there's a player there'.\"He's got a few franchise gigs at the moment but to do it on the biggest stage against Australia, with the world watching, he's going to be talked about.\" His opening partner Salt, who was dropped by Joel Davies on 10, was mostly able to watch from the non-striker's end before accelerating to a 24-ball half-century of his own, which came during a brutal over from spinner Davies that went for 31. Donald looked gutted to miss out on a century after he holed out to long-on off Riley Meredith's bowling, before Salt was brilliantly caught and bowled by Ben Dwarshuis an over later. However, England's phenomenal top order - which still awaits the return of Jos Buttler from injury - finished the job with ease as Tom Banton walked off unbeaten on 26 off eight balls, including a monstrous six for the winning runs.Australia's 184-8 looked competitive on a surface that had assisted England's spinners and Sam Curran's slower balls - the hosts recovering from a stodgy middle over period after falling to 58-4 in the powerplay. Davies added a handy cameo of 38 before Dwarshuis whacked Curran's last three balls for six to finish strongly but within just a few overs of the chase, Donald and Salt had silenced a lively Australian crowd in style.The five-match series continues at the Gabba, Brisbane, on Friday.Donald made his T20 international debut as a 29-year-old earlier this summer on the back of consistent county and Hundred performances, but struggled to really cement his place with 56 runs in four innings. Still, a player known for an incredible strike-rate in particular, he was backed by Harry Brook and Brendon McCullum to play freely and showcased that strength against an Australia attack that had no answers. \"He's the perfect T20 striker,\" Vaughan added. \"He has got a great base and a good eye and he doesn't over-complicate it. His mindset is to just try and hit everything for six, very much the modern T20 player. \"The game is moving forward and you need players like Donald who can hit the ball like that.\" Brook himself said he was \"speechless\" at the end of the game, high praise from one of the world's best, as Donald and Salt made what could have been a tricky chase look like a stroll in the park. Only three international T20s had been held at the Gold Coast's Carrara Oval previously so England were in relatively unknown territory.Not that it mattered to Donald, who swatted Davies for four from his first ball to start the demolition. His first couple of sixes off Jack Edwards sailed into the crowd despite looking like he had not timed them perfectly, giving Australia captain Mitch Marsh an insight into his new Perth Scorchers team-mate when the Big Bash League starts in December.Though the pitch had looked difficult to face spin in Australia's innings, Donald and Salt both tucked into it with aplomb - before Salt's takedown of Davies, Donald clubbed Nikhil Chaudhary for 26 to bring up his fifty by the end of the fifth over. Donald's showcase gives England a nice headache at the top of the order, with Buttler and Jacob Bethell still to come back into the fold - a frightening prospect for opposition bowlers.Despite the batting brilliance, there were mixed fortunes for England's bowlers with a contrast between their spinners and seamers.Australia's powerplay was bizarre. Their run-rate was healthy, just shy of 10 runs an over, but they threw away four wickets which meant they had to be more cautious throughout the middle. Marsh was caught and bowled by Henry Crocombe, Jake Fraser-McGurk was out slogging to Curran before debutant James Coles produced a fine run out to get rid of Sam Harper. Coles then took the catch in the deep to get rid of Tim David, and that allowed the masterful Adil Rashid and Liam Dawson to get to work, bowling seven overs on the bounce as Australia's middle order came to a standstill. Rashid tied Lachlan Shaw and Joel Davies in knots early on as they struggled to pick up his variations before he removed the former for a stodgy 10 from 17 balls. Davies dug in before accelerating at the death, with assistance from the big-hitting Edwards as Jamie Overton's struggles continued. The 18th over conceded 16 as he only bowled two overs for 28, before Crocombe's penultimate over flew for 16 and Dwarshuis got to work on Curran, who was also taken apart in the final over in Australia's win on Saturday. Unfortunately for Curran, he had previously been sensational at mixing up his pace deceptively and had figures of 2-9 off his first three, but completely lost control of his yorkers in the final three balls which the powerful Dwarshuis sent flying. England will be grateful for the return of Jofra Archer to their ranks as their difficulties at closing out an innings are becoming a concern.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T12:58:20.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/d4c5/live/25f045a0-e805-11f1-99a1-253766e7ad8b.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/cricket/articles/ck30g8ed598no?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 5,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-24-llm-agents-work-for-their-parent-companies-not-for-you",
      "title": "LLM agents work for their parent companies, not for you",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://michaelharley.net/posts/2026/11/01/llm-agents-work-for-their-parent-companies-not-for-you/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50627340\nPoints: 554\n# Comments: 231",
      "body": "In the evenings after work, I will often sit out back on the porch and do some phone rotting. That usually takes the form of me just flipping through reels on Instagram, allowing the algorithm to take me where it may. I usually pair this with a healthy tumbler of rum or bourbon. I'm turning my brain off after work, and I'm just having a good time on the internet. The kids might call it dopamine maxxing? Hell, I don't know. Who gives a shit about what those assholes would call it?\nAnywho, I often encounter songs I really really like, and I would like to listen and discover more of please. Instagram makes it hard for me to connect with the band outside of their platform. I was trying to make this connection manually myself at first. I would just email myself a reel from my phone. Then when I'm back on the sofa and in the mood to track down new music, I'd open the reels and do my best to search on Bandcamp or YouTube or try to figure out if some of these musicians had a way to buy music from them. Very few do these days. They send you their Spotify link or send you to their TikTok page. I don't want to do any of that. I'd like to trade US dollars in exchange for some music files I can download onto my computer, where I can listen as much as I like on any device I own thank you please. Retro maxxing.\nSo, I used Claude Code to build a skill that does this for me. It opens the Instagram reel and attempts to identify the artist and song. It then tries to determine if the artists have a Bandcamp page so I can purchase the songs I want, and if it finds one, it gives me the URL so I can click it to complete the trade I want. I want to buy music from artists who make music I like. That's a fair and wholesome endeavor, I feel, but this recently stopped working.\nIt turns out Bandcamp added a challenge on their search API page. Ah bummer. No worries, that was an unpublished workaround anyway. As I was mulling over this discovery, the robot went on to explain that it would not help me work around the challenge. cue Nick Young question marks\nWell, that led me down a whole nother rabbit hole, which is turning into this post. There's another branch of my skill that attempts to locate the same song from the Instagram reel on YouTube, and it'll download the song to my computer if I ask.\nSo let me get this straight. The robot, which is my robot that I pay a pretty penny for, will not attempt to work around the challenge at Bandcamp so that I can more easily connect with bands to complete a sale, but it will download the YouTube song, which doesn't send any money to the band or anyone???\nThis is the real danger of the frontier AI models. Not that it won't help me hack Bandcamp[1], but that this model doesn't really work for us. Well, they do, but only insofar as Anthropic or OpenAI or whoever else allows them to. In this case, Anthropic is trying to ensure that people can't use their models for evil. I'm not trying to do evil. I'm actually trying to do good! I want to buy something from a small artist on the internet. That's basically the most wholesome thing a person can do on the internet!\nHave no doubt, dear reader, these companies want to dominate. They want to capture the market. They want all the money. It'll be the same march to enshittification that happened with Google. A bunch of nerds who built something cool sold out, and it turned into just another business that decided to remove its don't be evil mantra.\nRight now, I think the robots are providing real information to the best of their ability, but I think it's only a matter of time until we must wonder if the answers they're providing are real answers that the robot found, or if they're weighted answers Anthropic put there because a company gave it enough money for product placement. I want to know the best robot vacuum according to old reddit or other trusted sources like Wirecutter, weighed and measured according to what I, and thus my robot agent, think are important requirements. I don't want to know which robot vacuum parent multinational conglomerate has a large ad budget.\nAnd it's not just ads. If Anthropic can tell its model not to work around a challenge, then it can tell its model to do whatever the US government tells it to do, including not helping people download copyrighted music. It does that some now, but it's easy to slip the noose on that. For now.\nThis is the reason I want a local model that runs in my homelab. My robot should work for me and have my best interests in mind, not the interests of US corporate copyright holders. I plan to study up on what \"open models\" even means. I've been thinking about these as the FOSS version of LLMs, but I think that doesn't exactly map, as the biggest piece seems to be how the model was trained. The Chinese models are likely to refuse to answer questions about Taiwan, for example.\nOn the existential threat or tool question, I'm firmly in the tool camp, and I really do not like artificial restrictions on my tools. I will do everything I reasonably can to remove those restrictions.\nAnd to be clear, I'm not trying to work around Bandcamp's challenge. I'm responding to the fact that Claude preemptively told me it wouldn't help me do this. ↩︎",
