      "id": "bbc-2026-11-24-from-dancing-on-the-tube-to-being-sectioned-sabrina-bahsoon-",
      "title": "From dancing on the Tube to being sectioned: Sabrina Bahsoon on her psychosis misdiagnosis",
      "summary": "The content creator's self-shot videos of her dancing on the London Underground blew up online in 2023.",
      "body": "Bahsoon first had \"psychotic symptoms\" in March this year after filming a video on the Tube with girl group Flo.She says that evening she began to scream, cry and her conversations became \"very erratic\".The next day she broke up with her boyfriend, which prompted her sisters to check on her, as it was so unexpected.Bahsoon says her condition got worse, becoming violent towards herself and posting videos which didn't make sense, before her sisters called 999 for an ambulance.\"I'm normally a very relaxed person, maybe anxious at times, but the way I was speaking about harming myself and the way I was screaming and being violent was super out of character for me,\" she says.A&E doctors initially thought her symptoms could have been provoked by stress and a lack of sleep, discharging her with sleeping tablets. She then began destroying things at home and spoke about having hallucinations, which led to police bringing her to hospital in handcuffs.Bahsoon was sectioned and moved to a psychiatric ward where she was treated with antipsychotic medication, which she says made her \"docile\".\"I went from being a total firecracker to being completely catatonic,\" she says. \"I stopped eating, drinking. I could not speak properly.\"Bahsoon has \"very faint memories\" of this period, she says, but her family has since shown her clips of her having breakdowns which she finds \"heartbreaking\".After two months on the psychiatric ward, Bahsoon was diagnosed with an autoimmune disease called anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis.It was caused by tumours in her ovaries, known as teratomas, producing antibodies which attacked her body.She had surgery to remove the tumours in June, which she says brought on a \"drastic\" change in her behaviour from \"freaking out\" to having more understanding of what was happening around her.Bahsoon has since been recovering back in her home country of Malaysia and is having therapy to \"deal with all the trauma\".But she says she feels \"very lucky\" as doctors have told her she is part of a small percentage of people who they expect to make a full recovery.The content creator first revealed what happened in a TikTok video in October, and says she is sharing her story as she wants to raise awareness of the disease.She also wants to \"push people to advocate for themselves\" if they feel they're not getting the \"treatment they deserve\".\"I do want reform in the way that hospitals deal with psychotic patients or patients that present with psychotic symptoms,\" she says.\"I hope I'm sharing my story so that nobody else has to go through this.\"She plans to return to her content creation in the new year and is looking forward to \"enjoying life with a whole new perspective and appreciation for what I do and what I now can help people with\".Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis is more typical in women and mainly affects young people, with about 40% of cases aged under 18, according to the charity Encephalitis International (EI), external.Dr Ava Easton MBE, chief executive of EI, says a \"frightening\" aspect is how quickly someone's behaviour and personality can appear to change.\"A person may suddenly seem confused, distressed or [to be] experiencing psychosis when the underlying problem is actually inflammation of their brain,\" she says.She says there's a \"real risk\" of it going undiagnosed and encourages people to look out for the warning signs, external.According to the NHS, external, cases of all types of encephalitis are rare, and EI says the condition affects about 6,000 people in the UK each year.A spokesperson for Imperial College Healthcare NHS Trust, which was the first to treat Bahsoon, said they were \"very sorry\" for what she experienced.They said an internal review found some aspects of her initial assessment \"were not followed up as they should have been\" and an investigation had been started.Dr Ed Beveridge, divisional medical director at Central and North West London NHS Foundation Trust, also confirmed they'd be part of that review.He said anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis was a \"rare autoimmune condition\" that is not \"routinely screened for nationally\".\"We are pleased that her condition has now been identified and treated.\"If you have been affected by the issues raised in this article, help and support is available via BBC Action Line.",
