    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-24-2035-came-early",
      "title": "2035 came early",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://fzakaria.com/2026/11/23/2035-came-early\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50624497\nPoints: 705\n# Comments: 261",
      "body": "tl;dr trynix.dev boots GUI programs now. Try solitaire, GL gears, xeyes, a voice, the weather over curl, or Firefox.\nI remember watching the talk The Birth & Death of JavaScript by Gary Bernhardt and falling in love with the whimsical yet dreadfully serious unfolding of the story of how JavaScript will come to dominate all software.\nOthers have noted the inevitability of JavaScript’s dominance, so perhaps it was not surprising that Atwood’s Law holds true.\nAtwood’s LawAny application that can be written in JavaScript, will eventually be written in JavaScript.\nI highly recommend watching the talk if you have not already seen it, but the short and skinny is a lookback from 2035 and how JavaScript came to be the defacto language for all software.\nIn Bernhardt’s retelling of events, the date is worked back from 2035, however unsurprisingly, large language models have rapidly sped up the timeline. What was once perceived to be the end-state at 2035 has arrived years earlier and you can witness it unfolding at trynix.dev.\nC and C++ compile to it\nOne of the most impressive creations I’ve managed to build leveraging nixpkgs-multiverse is https://trynix.dev.\nIt still baffles my mind that I can run any Nix package, over 300,000 distinct package versions, straight from my browser. Truly insane capability.\nI was however a little dismayed at the speed of the QEMU emulation in the browser. It was slow enough that the creation tipped more towards novelty than practicality.\nI woke on November 20th and saw Domen Kožar sharing a single message in our TacoSprint Discord channel: unkernel.dev\nIt was the fast browser Linux experience I had sought to achieve, but with Nix.\nIt is Linux compiled to WebAssembly, allowing the kernel and system components to run at wasm speed while only user programs are emulated. It was published by Pooya Parsa on November 19th, 2026.\nIn an AI induced manic frenzy, I adopted the new engine along with a slough of long-wished features almost immediately.\na strip that says what is loading\nGUI windows on the page\nunkernel ships a Wayland desktop\ntrynix boots on unkernel by default\nThe proof is in the pudding: unkernel delivers remarkable speed improvements over qemu-wasm,11With a very healthy set of patches applied to make it even faster.  especially on first runs where kernel work dominates. The new engine is able to deliver 2x to 32x faster performance depending on the workload.22The benchmark page tracks both engines over time, measured through the real page and shell.\nseconds, first run (log scale)\nAmazingly not only does the kernel boot quickly, it also enables networking, full-fledged graphical and audio experiences directly in the browser.\nWindows. A program’s Wayland surfaces are composited in the page by wland, and they float over the terminal you started them from.\nGL. The VM includes a minimal Mesa build with only the virgl driver, compiled to WebAssembly, rendering through your browser’s WebGL2.\nSound. The VM has an ALSA sound card, and can play audio through your browser.\nNetwork. curl, git clone and pip install work. Each TCP connection goes over one WebSocket to a Wisp relay.33Thank you to @_pi0_ for adding trynix.dev to his Wisp relay allow-list.\nThe best joke in Bernhardt’s talk is a browser running inside another browser: the Windows GIMP under Wine, in Chrome, in Firefox. Of course, I had to try it with trynix. 😈\nBernhardt's 2035, drawn in 2014\nHere is Firefox in trynix, and it loading trynix.dev, and then loading Firefox again inside itself.44Do not be too overjoyed. It took more than an hour for this to render.\nSit and bask in that insanity. That is Firefox, on Linux compiled to WebAssembly, in a Firefox tab, loading the site and then loading Firefox again inside itself. 🤯",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "setheron",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T06:30:33.000Z",
      "imageUrl": null,
