      "id": "bbc-2026-11-24-the-shake-up-facing-renters-as-a-new-kind-of-landlord-emerge",
      "title": "The shake-up facing renters as a new kind of landlord emerges",
      "summary": "Companies building flats that they then rent out could usher in big changes for renters.",
      "body": "According to the latest government survey of private landlords in England, 83% of English landlords own four properties or fewer, representing 51% of tenancies.And according to 2024 research by real estate agency Savills, around 40% rental homes in the US and Germany are owned by large institutions - be they private firms, listed companies, pension funds, or co-operatives. For the UK, Savills calculated that figure was just 3%.But the rental market is changing. The trend is most visible in England, where BTR has boomed in large cities and where regulations like the Renters' Rights Act apply, but there are BTR developments in Scotland, Wales, and Northern Ireland too and the devolved nations are also seeing increased regulation on landlords, such as with Scotland's rent controls.Lexi is pleased with the idea of a sector with stricter rules for landlords and more professional institutions getting involved.\"Anything that takes away from landlords having personal preferences [around choosing who they are or not prepared to rent to]… is a good thing,\" she says, adding that she believes large, corporate landlords would be less likely to act that way.Not everyone is convinced BTR landlords are better, though.Clint Stevenson, a 44-year-old magician for weddings and events, has been renting his one-bedroom flat in Ramsgate for £550 a month for two years. He says he has \"the most amazing landlady\" - an older woman who he regularly chats to. He sometimes walks her dog and does her shopping as well.He says he had considered renting from a BTR development, but found the accommodation to be \"very industrial and very unpersonal\". He also found what was on offer to be expensive compared to what he is used to paying.\"They just look like big, money-grabbing deals,\" he says.In Cardiff, Grainger, the UK's largest listed BTR developer, is currently offering a fully-furnished studio flat on the 20th floor of The Copper Works in the city centre with views over the bay for £1,265 a month.It's a lot more than what is currently on offer on Rightmove, a 15 minute walk away from the city centre - a fully-furnished, ground-floor, one-bedroom flat with a garden for £800 a month.But tenants in The Copper Works have free access to a gym, a cinema room, a roof terrace, and a games room. There's also free WiFi, a concierge service with a helpline, and other amenities. In addition, the studio flat has an EPC B rating as opposed to an EPC C rating for the one-bedroom flat down the road.\"You have to look at what you're getting for your rent,\" says Helen Gordon, Grainger's chief executive. She says BTR landlords can offer these services because of their scale. As she puts it: \"If you run 11,000 homes, you can afford to invest in systems, processes, people, helplines – that sort of thing – that smaller landlords can't.\"Gordon strongly pushes back against suggestions that there is a lack of human connection within BTR, noting that tenants are able to speak with staff whenever they need and that the firm regularly organises events for socialising with other tenants to create a sense of community.It's a pattern across the BTR sector: a higher price point in exchange for what is marketed as high-quality accommodation and a suite of services - some in the sector even refer to 'customers' rather than 'tenants'.The number of BTR homes has tripled between 2019 and 2025, and while it represents just 3% of the overall rental market at the moment, Gordon believes it could reach \"at least 10%\" over the next ten years. \"And I think it could go higher,\" she adds.\"If you've got major institutions that include pension funds, financial institutions, and FTSE 250 companies running the stock, it's easier to hold people to account than lots of disparate people, so you can see why it's attractive [to government].\"A 2012 government-commissioned report concluded that more BTR properties could help increase housing supply and drive economic growth as well as providing tenants with more choice and better quality housing. It recommended central government and councils support the development of BTR through favourable planning decisions and releasing public land.There is a lot of evidence governments and local authorities listened. Local leaders in big cities – London and Manchester, in particular – have partnered with developers to deliver large housing projects involving, in many cases, the release of public land. Kings Cross in London is a prime example of this and current London Mayor Sadiq Khan's push for BTR development near Transport for London (TFL) land, mostly Tube stations, is another example. As part of that, TfL formed a joint venture with Grainger.Lucian Cook, head of residential research at Savills, says it's not a matter of looking at one policy, government, or council in particular but more the \"policy environment\" which has been around since the 2010s.He points to a series of tax changes, including mortgage interest tax relief changes and higher stamp duty rates for second homes and buy-to-let properties introduced by the Conservatives around 10 years ago, which made the sector less favourable for smaller landlords.The Renters' Rights Act and more stringent energy efficiency requirements were later introduced. And the last two Budgets have raised taxes on rental income from next year. The tax change applies to individuals who are landlords, not to BTR companies which pay corporation tax, further encouraging professionalisation of the sector.Yet, while many welcome these changes, others say they are pushing smaller landlords out of the sector. As Cook puts it, there has been more \"stick\" for small landlords than \"carrot\" for the BTR sector.Between 2000 and 2016, a net 2.74 million homes were added to the English rental market in total, according to an analysis of government data by the Joseph Rowntree Foundation. In the 10 years since, a net of just 200,000 have. For renters, it is now less likely that your property is owned by a smaller landlord.Kevin Barber started off with one rental home. He has since grown this portfolio to 40 properties with his business partner but resents what he sees as the clout of the BTR sector. \"The big property companies have got so much financial power and influence with the government,\" he says.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T00:28:48.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/8db7/live/089748f0-e7e7-11f1-99b6-a35a62fa4264.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6n90g2l82d1o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 5,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-24-bbc-secret-filming-shows-dog-breeder-selling-sick-puppies-fo",
      "title": "BBC secret filming shows dog breeder selling sick puppies for £2k each",
      "summary": "The breeder's rating was downgraded but he continued to advertise as five-star, investigation finds.",
      "body": "When Katie Thornton paid £2,000 for a nine-week-old puppy for her son, she knew it was a premium price - but was happy to be doing business with a \"legit\", council-approved breeder.But Ozzy the Dobermann pup started coughing in the car on the way home, and within 48 hours he was in a veterinary hospital receiving urgent treatment for pneumonia.Since then, his ongoing health problems mean Katie has racked up more than £5,000 in vet bills.A BBC investigation has found the breeder had been stripped of his five-star rating two months before selling several sick puppies, but continued to advertise as five-star.We have also found at least 15 other breeders in England advertising puppies alongside claims their star rating is higher than it really is.An expert reviewing our findings - including undercover footage we recorded at the breeder's home - said the investigation exposes a nationwide \"wild west\" of dog licensing, making it \"easy for consumers to be scammed\".The breeder has not responded to our request for comment.In England, local councils grant breeders in their area a licence and star rating dependent on welfare standards.Vet reports for North Yorkshire Council from August and December 2025, seen by the BBC, cited dozens of concerns with Estill's business, including:\"Poor\" living conditions \"akin to a prison\", with dogs kept in cages, stacked on top of each other, that were too small for them to turn around inDeteriorating health and welfare of \"sad\" dogsPuppies for sale not matching descriptions - \"in other words it is a scam\"Prospective buyers \"very vulnerable to unsuspectingly purchasing unhealthy puppies\"The business was \"more or less a puppy farm masquerading as an honest and conscientious breeder. This must stop\"Estill \"bullied\" a local vet to perform an \"unnecessary\" Caesarean section on one of his dogs, to reduce the time he would have to spend monitoring herHis \"demanding\" behaviour led the practice to give him several doses of a powerful stimulant which he gave to one of his dogs at home - which the vet considered to be \"negligent\"He had a three-year-old dog euthanised because he suspected her to have a neurological condition, despite the vet encouraging him to get diagnostic tests and specialist treatment first.Following the reports, which concluded that the business was \"functioning outside the law\", the council renewed Estill's licence in January but downgraded his rating from five-star (the highest standards) to one-star (minor failings).Dr Caroline Allen, founder of Pet Lighthouse and former RSPCA chief veterinary officer, said she was \"surprised and shocked\" that the business kept its licence.A North Yorkshire Council spokesperson said the threshold for revoking a licence \"was not considered to have been met on the evidence available\".They added: \"We take animal welfare very seriously and investigate concerns brought to our attention.\"Following the veterinary inspection reports, we have continued to work with the licence holder to address the identified concerns and are satisfied that standards have significantly improved, resulting in a higher level of animal welfare at the premises.\"The BBC has evidence that Estill continued to sell puppies while claiming on his website and in person that he was five-star rated.While Estill has since updated his website homepage, he has not updated the metadata, which means when his business is searched for online the five-star claim still appears alongside a link to his site.There is no official list of licensed breeders and their star ratings, and not all councils publish them, so Katie took his word for it.\"If I would've known he was a one-star breeder, I would've never even gone to the property. I wouldn't have bought a dog off a one-star breeder,\" she said, adding that she wanted to see Estill's licence revoked.Kat Durrant's dog Bentley died aged three in 2022 of a heart condition known as dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM).Within days of bringing him home from Mikjulora Dobermanns, Kat says Bentley was \"really poorly\".\"The vet asked me if I had got the dog from a puppy farm because of his condition,\" she added.Three years later, Kat had Bentley put to sleep as his weak heart was \"giving up\" and he was struggling to breathe.\"Bentley suffered his whole life because of everything he'd been through when he was young,\" she said.Kat, from Manchester, set up a Facebook group where other people who had bought dogs from Estill could share their experiences.Including Kat, owners of 17 dogs mentioned in the group said the pets had died before the age of eight, including 10 below the age of four.Several others claimed their dogs were sick at a young age but survived.The vet report from December 2025 said Estill was not correctly testing his dogs for DCM despite his website saying that he did test.The report said the fact that Estill did \"nothing\" when customers told him about DCM diagnoses was \"truly the hallmark of a breeder who does not care about the welfare or improving the breed\".Michelle Paton, who lives in Edinburgh, bought her puppy Cruz a week before Katie bought Ozzy.He had a cough \"as soon as he came through the front door\" and was diagnosed with pneumonia and possible heart failure a few weeks later.Michelle was frustrated to discover Kat's Facebook group and felt Estill's licence should have been revoked \"years ago\".\"If somebody was actually doing their job properly then they would have put an end to him breeding a long time ago,\" she said.Caroline Housam, from York, bought her puppy Roxy the day before Katie bought Ozzy.Roxy started coughing in the car on the journey home.When Caroline took Roxy to the vet she said she was told there were problems with her glands, and later they found she had a limp and ulcers in her eyes.She is having ongoing treatment and tests.\"I'm actually angry because I look at my dog and I'm thinking, 'I don't know what lies ahead',\" said Caroline.\"When you listen to what other people have said [on Facebook] who have had [dogs] bred from him, her chances of living until two are slim.\"To corroborate the claims made, the BBC sent undercover reporters to film at Estill's home, posing as potential puppy buyers.Estill refused to show us where the puppies and their mother slept.He said he had \"another 15 Dobermanns\" in cages in a back room and worried that the neighbours would complain if we went in and they started barking.He offered to show us the space another day and said he had \"nothing to hide\".Reviewing our footage, Allen said this refusal was the \"most serious concern\" and breached regulations.She added that the eyes of two of the puppies for sale were \" not all normal\", citing \"a number of abnormalities\" such as discoloration and the shape of the eye socket.Looking at a photo of a male pup taken by the BBC, she said: \"That is not a healthy dog.\"Estill's licence certificate and star rating were also not visibly on display anywhere, despite government rules saying both should be \"clearly and prominently displayed\" for customers.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
