      "id": "bbc-2026-11-24-coldest-spell-of-autumn-continues-with-risk-of-hill-snow-ice",
      "title": "Coldest spell of autumn continues with risk of hill snow, ice and heavy rain",
      "summary": "Colder weather continues for much of the UK with some ice and hill snow but milder and wetter in the south and west, as Simon King explains",
      "body": "Image source, Getty ImagesBySimon King Lead Weather PresenterAs the coldest spell of the autumn so far continues, parts of the UK could see some hill snow, ice or heavy rain this week.With Arctic air to the north and east of the UK, Scotland, northern and eastern England will experience temperatures below the average with some hill snow, freezing rain and ice.Meanwhile, milder air to the west of the UK will bring heavy rain to parts of Northern Ireland, Wales and south-west England with temperatures around average.The battle between colder and milder conditions is likely to continue through the week.Freezing rain riskIt will be a cold and frosty start to the day across Scotland, northern and eastern areas of England with temperatures in the morning starting below freezing.There will be some rain, however, moving in from the south-west early on Tuesday that will mix with colder air and bring some wintry hazards.Over the higher ground of the North Pennines, Cheviots, Southern Uplands and Grampians, there may be a spell of freezing rain.Freezing rain occurs when precipitation starts off as snow coming out of cloud before it hits a milder layer of air where it melts into liquid rain.It then passes through another colder layer of air - below freezing - close to the ground.  On hitting surfaces such as the road, pavement, cars or power lines, the liquid rain freezes instantly to create a layer of ice.In prolonged periods, it can be one of the most hazardous types of winter weather, especially in built up and populated areas.For the rest of Tuesday, there could be a few centimetres of snow over the higher ground of the Grampians.Heavy rain to bring localised flood riskWhile wintry hazards will affect more northern and eastern areas of the UK, in the south and west it will be milder and wetter.Heavy rain will persist across south-west England, Wales and, for a time, Northern Ireland.The Met Office has issued a yellow weather warning for rain covering Cornwall and the Isles of Scilly beginning at 10:00 GMT on Tuesday and lasting until 23.59 GMT.Image source, BBC WeatherAround 30-40mm of rain are forecast widely in the warning area, but as much as 60-90mm are possible where the heavy rain persists, particularly in western Cornwall.Localised flooding is possible, leading to difficult driving conditions and road closures in some places.Strong winds will develop around western areas of the UK throughout Tuesday too.Temperatures will hold up at around 10 to 13C, which is around, if not slightly above, average for the end of November.Cold dominates the rest of the weekImage caption, Colder air will dominate across the UK for much of the week before milder air tries to move in later in the weekFor the rest of the week the colder air will push back westward.On Wednesday morning there will be some fog or freezing fog patches, especially in Northern Ireland which may be stubborn to clear.Otherwise it will be a largely dry day with some bright or sunny spells at times.Colder but mostly dry weather will continue into Friday before another weather system tries to make inroads to the west or south-west of the UK.Rain will move slowly eastward and temporarily turn to snow over the hills of northern England.More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T00:03:36.000Z",
