    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-23-pip-assessments-need-fundamental-overhaul-report-to-say",
      "title": "Pip assessments need 'fundamental overhaul', report to say",
      "summary": "Disability Minister Sir Stephen Timms says he will recommend major reforms to the personal independence payment.",
      "body": "A review into the personal independence payment (Pip) will call for a \"fundamental overhaul\" of the assessment process to \"better recognise people's varying costs and needs\", the disability minister has said.Sir Stephen Timms has been looking into disability benefits in England and Wales for the past year and found the set up was \"no longer fit for purpose\".Writing in the Times, external, he said his review, to be published on Tuesday, would recommend people with the most severe conditions have a \"fast track\" process which is \"less burdensome\".It comes after a separate review into young people not in education, employment or training (Neets) recommended Pip be scrapped and replaced for under-25s.Sir Stephen, who produced his report alongside disability groups, carers, clinicians and MPs, said that while Pip had the \"right aims\" when it was created, there have been \"marked shifts in the nature of disability and ill health\" in the 13 years since.He said there had been \"substantial growth in claims for mental health and neurodiverse conditions as well as changes to wider society and the workplace\".\"For those with the most severe conditions and end of life care, the review recommends a fast track which is less burdensome, with assessment capacity freed up for other cases.\"Where claimants do not have additional costs or care needs, we should be signposting them to services and support.\"He added: \"Someone with a lifelong or degenerative illness should not need regular reassessments which feel adversarial and unnecessary.\"Disabled people must have the chance to participate fully in everyday life, including in work, and need a more personalised, flexible system that supports them to do so, rather than itself being a barrier.\"The Timms review was commissioned after a U-turn from former Prime Minister Sir Keir Starmer's government on a plan for major benefit reform, which attempted to reduce the welfare bill by £5bn a year.More than 130 MPs, including 120 from Labour, signed an amendment to reject plans to cut disability and sickness-related benefit payments in 2025.The rebels said they opposed the plans because of the number of people expected to be pushed into relative poverty and a lack of consultation at the time.Sir Stephen's long-awaited review comes only a week after a former Labour Health Secretary Alan Milburn handed down his proposed reforms to tackle youth unemployment.The government welcomed the Milburn review, which included a recommendation to move those aged 18 to 24 off Pip and the health element of universal credit, and onto an entirely new benefits system.Prime Minister Andy Burnham said he thought the \"same philosophy\" could be applied \"right through the age range\".At this stage it is unclear how both of the reviews will complement one another.No 10 has said the government would consider the Timms review \"alongside Milburn's and respond in the coming weeks\".Downing Street has not ruled out the possibility that some people who currently receive Pip could have a reduced entitlement in the future and would not say if the review's recommendations would lead to a reduced welfare bill.The cost of Pip is expected to rise to more than £41bn by 2030, up from £15bn in 2020. It was being claimed by 4m people in the UK as of 30 April 2026, according to the Department for Work and Pensions.Though reforming the benefit has frequently been looked at as a way to reduce the Treasury's welfare bill, disability charities have described it as a lifeline for many. Ahead of the Timms review being handed down, disability equality charity Scope said ministers must ensure Pip is not weakened.\"Life costs more when you're disabled, whether you work or not,\" executive director James Taylor said, adding that Pip was \"vital financial support\".\"We know there is real fear among disabled people that changes could leave them unable to afford the essentials.\"The disability benefit system is devolved in Scotland and will not change in line with England and Wales; however the Timms Review could impact the funding the Scottish government receives. Disability benefits are also devolved in Northern Ireland, but in practice it reflects any changes made to the system in England and Wales.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
