      "id": "bbc-2026-11-23-faisal-islam-why-jensen-huang-proved-too-big-to-fire",
      "title": "Faisal Islam: Why Jensen Huang proved too big to fire",
      "summary": "Huang's role at the centre of his company's journey to lynchpin of AI proved crucial.",
      "body": "There have been three jaw-dropping moments in the past few hours around Jensen Huang's reinstatement as Nvidia chief executive after he admitted an affair with a woman who was alleged to be a Chinese spy.The shock announcement of his return to the chipmaker was followed by the addition of one trillion dollars of stock market value to Nvidia.Then it was the moment President Trump admitted the obvious: that it was his \"You're unfired\" message direct to the board that changed the decision.The nature of the U-turn is clear from what Nvidia was saying just last week. The board was clear that Huang was not returning. The interim management had also suggested some shifts in strategy away from the Huang era.All of that is now in the dustbin of corporate history. This is a comeback that exceeds the return of Sam Altman to OpenAI or Steve Jobs to Apple, in terms of sheer numbers.LIVE: Nvidia shares soar after Jensen Huang returns as CEOThe rise and fall and rise again of Jensen HuangHow important are Nvidia and Jensen Huang to the US economy?The reason for the turnaround was pretty clear in the first statement. The US government had said there were no national security concerns with his reinstatement. Such a reassurance could only have come from the White House.And an hour later President Trump revealed that he had spoken directly to the Nvidia board.This is where I go back to my own conversation with Jensen Huang last September, where he openly advocated for allowing Chinese access to advanced Nvidia microchips. He was one of the few people in the world who could credibly say he knew the minds of both President Trump and President Xi.Reports in the US say there were splits in the Trump administration between China hawks wanting to restrict exports, and others who backed Huang's argument. There is still an independent investigation into the company's compliance with export controls.Nvidia's chips are critical to the development of frontier artificial intelligence systems. Jensen Huang's influence over this pipeline meant he was more important than many world leaders.This is a move of importance not just to the flagging US tech stock boom, but the future of the world economy, of US politics, and of course the geopolitical race between the US and China.The recent doubts about Nvidia struck at the heart of the AI boom. This is after all a company actually generating phenomenal amounts of real sales - over $400bn (£305bn) this year, compared with $27bn four years ago.Nvidia's stock market valuation has been used as a critical form of financing for other companies to construct data centres. A complex web of loans, guarantees, and shareholdings helped spread and sustain the AI boom. Jensen Huang's exit had raised doubts about the fragility of this system, and the interim management had suggested a more \"disciplined and selective\" approach.The concerns about so-called \"circular financing\" still raise concerns from some analysts. The public backlash against datacentres and AI evident at the US midterm elections also point to a changed environment. The Democrats in Congress have vowed to investigate both the President and Mr Huang, and he is due to appear before them next week. Jensen Huang is at the centre of it all - the technology, the financing, the US politics and the geopolitics.Today has proven Jensen Huang was in fact, too big to fire.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T17:11:58.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/8d27/live/d9940160-e7d2-11f1-939f-471cde339c0e.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ckr5g8edl94eo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-23-israeli-attacks-have-turned-gaza-into-toxic-wasteland-oxfam-",
      "title": "Israeli attacks have turned Gaza into 'toxic wasteland', Oxfam says",
      "summary": "The report says there are signs of possible contamination across almost all of Gaza as a result of Israel's actions.",
      "body": "Image source, Hamza Z. H. Qraiqea / Anadolu via Getty ImagesImage caption, The UN estimates that Israeli forces dropped more than 200,000 tonnes of explosives on Gaza in almost two years (file photo)ByYolande KnellMiddle East correspondentReporting fromJerusalemGaza has been turned into \"a toxic wasteland\", with more than 99% of the Palestinian territory showing indicators of environmental contamination from Israeli military attacks, a new report from the charity Oxfam says, external.Heavy metals, explosive residues, asbestos from destroyed buildings, and dioxins and biological contaminants are threats to Palestinians now and potentially for decades to come, it says.Oxfam International's executive director, Amitabh Behar, said the impact on Gaza \"will have fundamentally degraded the environmental conditions necessary for human safety and survival\".In response, the Israeli embassy in the UK said the report \"falls short of professional standards\". Behar also accused Israel of \"environmental war crimes\" as part of an \"ongoing genocide in Gaza\".Israel has rejected genocide accusations in an ongoing case at the International Court of Justice as baseless and distorted. It maintains that its military campaign in Gaza is an act of self-defence against Hamas in response to the group's deadly attack on Israel on 7 October 2023, and that it takes steps to mitigate civilian harm.Image source, Anadolu via Getty ImagesImage caption, The report says 99% of Gaza shows indicators of potential environmental contaminationOxfam and the UK-based Conflict and Environment Observatory (CEOBS) used satellite imagery, remote sensing, spatial analysis and other open-source data to draw their conclusions in the most comprehensive report to date on the severe environmental impacts caused by three years of intense war in Gaza.The report details how munitions used in Israel's intense bombardment, as well as the debris from destroyed buildings and infrastructure, all have an impact on the environment. In February, a UN special rapporteur stated that more than 200,000 tonnes of explosives were dropped on Gaza in almost two years - \"the equivalent of the total energy of approximately 13 Hiroshima-type nuclear bombs\".Last year, the UN Environment Programme (UNEP) estimated that Israel's military operations destroying homes and infrastructure had generated more than 61 million tonnes of debris across the territory, with about 15% potentially carrying a high risk of contamination from hazardous materials.\"There are going to be chronic health impacts that are long term because a lot of these contaminants are persistent in the environment,\" Oxfam report author Lama Abdul Samad told the BBC.She says that now and in future Gazans are likely to suffer from increased respiratory diseases, cancers, organ damage and a rise in birth defects and stillbirths.Image source, AFP via Getty ImagesImage caption, Only a small fraction of the more than 60 million tonnes of debris in Gaza has been clearedWith all six wastewater treatment plants out of operation in Gaza, untreated sewage currently makes its way into groundwater and the Mediterranean Sea. The health impact has recently been exacerbated by heavy winter storms causing rainwater to mix with wastewater and flow through streets and tent sites housing displaced people.Testing types and levels of contamination is often not possible because Gazan laboratories have been destroyed and there are Israeli restrictions on the entry of specialist testing equipment and scientists. The BBC has approached the Israeli military for comment.UNEP said security concerns also prevented its expert staff from previously travelling to Gaza.The Oxfam report calls for a full, independent environmental assessment to be carried out in Gaza to identify contamination risks ahead of a clean-up. It says special attention must be given to the protection of workers involved.The Israeli embassy sharply criticised the report, arguing that it relied on satellite imagery \"and unverified assumptions, and its methodology lacks the academic rigour needed to withstand serious scrutiny\".It said the report drew conclusions about Gaza's soil and groundwater without considering the excavation of hundreds of kilometres of tunnels by Hamas, rendering it fundamentally flawed.Image source, ReutersImage caption, Recent winter storms caused rainwater to mix with wastewater and flow through streets and tent campsAlready, Gazan doctors have been reporting increased acute respiratory infections caused by dust and pollution from destroyed buildings and displaced people burning plastic, rags and cardboard to keep warm and cook food. Gastrointestinal diseases are linked to unsafe water and poor sanitation.A Palestinian dermatologist, Dr Alaa Abu Hajar, told the BBC he had seen a five-fold increase in chronic skin diseases since the start of the war, putting this down to environmental pollution and overcrowded, unhygienic living conditions in tents and displacement camps.\"There are rodents, rubbish and sewage that's spread through the streets,\" he says. \"Unfortunately, the age group most affected is children.\"Image caption, Dr Alaa Abu Hajar has seen a five-fold increase in chronic skin diseases since the start of the warFarmers and those working in fishing say that pollution of the soil and sea mean they are unable to produce food that is safe.\"Sewage and wastewater are discharged into the sea without treatment and this is a great disaster,\" complains fisherman Abu al-Abed Sultan. \"There is also rubble from destroyed houses that's dumped into the sea. When we lower our nets, we catch rubble and garbage. This harms the marine environment; the fish are affected and so are the fishermen.\"At least 74,746 Palestinians have been killed since the start of the war in Gaza, according to the Hamas-run health ministry, whose figures are considered reliable by the UN. The UN says that humanitarian conditions remain dire.Israel has conditioned large-scale Gaza reconstruction on the full disarmament of Hamas and other Palestinian armed groups. In July, Hamas accepted a plan put forward by the US-led Board of Peace to give up its weapons in exchange for an Israeli troop withdrawal. However, the Israeli government said the proposal was insufficient.More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
