      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-23-ladybird-is-now-in-alpha",
      "title": "Ladybird is now in alpha",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://ladybird.org/posts/alpha/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50614249\nPoints: 2299\n# Comments: 561",
      "body": "Hello friends! Ladybird is now in alpha. You can download it for macOS and Linux.\nI’ve been looking forward to writing this post for a long time. :^)\nLadybird is a browser with a new engine. The engine is our own, built from the ground up, and not a fork. The browser around it is modest, but functional.\nWe want security nerds to hack it! A new browser gets hacked, over and over, and that’s part of the process, so we make no promises about security today. When you pwn us, please tell us how so we can harden Ladybird!\nThe alpha is a rolling channel, and we’ll be pushing out automatic updates regularly. This is the part I’m most excited about. Ladybird improves quickly, and until now you had to build it from source to see that.\nIf you build websites, try yours in Ladybird and tell us what breaks. It supports WebDriver for automated testing, as well as the Firefox DevTools protocol.\nThank you to our sponsors and donors, and to everyone who has tested, reported bugs, and written code over the years. There’s still a mountain of work ahead of us, but I’m very happy that you can finally try Ladybird for yourself!",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "janvdberg",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T16:13:12.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "/assets/img/logo-new.webp",
      "originalUrl": "https://ladybird.org/posts/alpha/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-23-how-important-are-nvidia-and-jensen-huang-to-the-us-economy",
      "title": "How important are Nvidia and Jensen Huang to the US economy?",
      "summary": "The Nvidia chief executive has been at the centre of the artificial intelligence boom.",
      "body": "Nvidia and its co-founder Jensen Huang have been at the centre of the artificial intelligence (AI) spending boom.No company or chief executive has arguably been more consequential to the development of AI in recent years, with the US microchip manufacturer under Huang positioning itself as a critical cog in the development of the world-changing technology.That central position prompted questions, in the aftermath of Huang's departure over revelations of extramarital affairs, over how important he was to the wider AI industry and US economy.The fact he has been handed his job back after just 11 days in a U-turn by Nvidia's board implies the top brass believe Huang is irreplaceable.Shares in the company have surged on the news of Huang's shock return, following a period of sharp declines.While Nvidia is not as much of a household name as other Big Tech players such as Apple, Amazon and Microsoft, the company has been just as integral to the booming US stock market of recent years.The billions of dollars being funnelled into AI and the construction of data centres has bolstered the American economy, with Nvidia being one of the biggest spenders.Originally focusing on chips which process graphics for computer games, it pivoted to making the chips used in AI systems and now dominates the market. It is now one the most valuable firms in the world and its rapid growth is poised to continue as the firm's quarterly sales doubled from a year earlier.Huang played a major role in the company, leading its shift towards AI, and maintained a firm grip on it, with up to 200 people directly reporting to him. Some suggested his departure, which was sparked by the emergence that Huang had an affair with a woman alleged to have been a spy for China, posed a risk to the wider AI industry.That was because it was unclear whether Nvidia would change strategy and potentially rein in spending - a move could have had wider implications for the US economy, but which looks unlikely now Huang has returned. The fears were sparked due to Nvidia being a key source of finance for many firms, as well as being a microchip supplier. On its journey to the top of the AI industry, it has, through Huang, created a spider's web of deals through what is known as \"circular financing\".Not only does it sell microchips to its customers, which include the likes of Meta, SpaceX and OpenAI, it has also provided funds for them and other firms in the supply chain by either handing out loans or investing in them directly.The practice has prompted concerns among some investors, who worry what might happen if Nvidia were to turn off the spending taps. If investment dried up, would other firms in the supply chain cut spending or jobs themselves? Would that spread to the wider US economy?That risk was perhaps why tech bosses including Elon Musk and Tim Sweeney, the CEO of Fortnite maker Epic Games, had called for Huang's reinstatement. Musk claimed that \"critical deals\" involving Nvidia's technology would be lost following Huang's resignation, and backed a campaign, along with shareholders and supporters, to \"Bring Jensen Back\". Following the announcement of Huang's return, Musk posted: \"The mistake has been corrected.\"Kathleen Brooks, research director at investment platform XTB, said Huang's return suggested he was \"the godfather\" of AI and has the status to shift \"the dial for corporate finance\". \"Is Jensen Huang the most valuable man on the planet? His return suggests more of the same when it comes to circular financing and the rapid AI infrastructure build out, and the market loves it,\" she added.However, Huang's return to Nvidia does not solve the wider issues rattling the tech stock markets in recent weeks.Investors had taken fright at Democrat gains in the US midterm elections, with many having campaigned for tougher regulation of AI firms. Questions also remain about whether the huge sums being invested in AI will actually pay off. However, investment in AI is still expected to exceed $1 trillion in 2026, according to investment bank Goldman Sachs, who said the technology would remain the \"dominant driver of business investment growth\" next year.The value of the shares in Big Tech companies have cushioned the US economy from global economic shocks, including tariffs, buoying investments and pensions for people worldwide.President Donald Trump hailed Huang as a \"great patriot and national treasure\" after his reappointment and pointed to the chief executive's wider impact.\"The country needs Jensen's leadership at Nvidia in order to ensure the success and prosperity of not only the company, but the American economy,\" he wrote on Truth Social.Nvidia said its leadership had received input from \"senior levels of the United States government and was \"confident that Mr Huang's reinstatement poses no national security risk\".",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T16:12:35.000Z",
