    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-23-welfare-cuts-what-s-been-happening-with-pip-and-universal-cr",
      "title": "Welfare cuts: What's been happening with Pip and universal credit?",
      "summary": "The government commissioned a review into Pip last year after it was forced to water down planned cuts to benefits.",
      "body": "In March 2025, the government, under former Prime Minister Sir Keir Starmer, announced plans to tighten daily living assessments for both current and future Pip claimants. However, after more than 120 Labour MPs threatened to vote against the legislation, the government said those already receiving Pip would not be affected. The original proposals said that people with the highest levels of a permanent condition or disability would no longer have to be reassessed at all. The assessments involve questions about everyday tasks, with each scored from zero, for no difficulty, to 12, for most difficulty.For example, needing help to wash your hair, or your body below the waist scores two points, but needing help to wash between the shoulders and waist is worth four points. The government said originally that anyone claiming Pip for the first time after November 2026 would have to score at least four points for a single activity, rather than across a range of different ones.However, this change was delayed until the wider Timms review of Pip. The final report - which will include recommendations - is due to be published on Tuesday.The cost of Pip is forecast to rise to more than £41bn by 2030. The cuts originally proposed by the government aimed to save about £5.5bn a year by the end of the decade.However, the Institute for Fiscal Studies (IFS) and Resolution Foundation said the concessions made by the government meant it would make no \"net savings\" by 2029-30.Sir Timms's review is expected to recommend people with the most severe conditions have a \"fast track\" process which is \"less burdensome\".His long-awaited review comes after another review by former Labour Health Secretary Alan Milburn, who proposed reforms that involved changing benefits to tackle the number of young people who are not in education, employment or training (Neet).The government welcomed the Milburn review, which included a recommendation to move those aged 18 to 24 off Pip and the health element of universal credit, and onto an entirely new benefits system.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T13:52:40.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/fa84/live/7e5f5ec0-03c7-11f0-9bb2-ab6d3a01d537.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cj924xvzrr2o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-23-nariq-bf16-level-qwen3-8-27b-in-a-10-gb-file",
      "title": "NariQ: BF16-level Qwen3.8-27B in a 10 GB file",
      "summary": "We built NariQ: 3-bit-or-lower quantizations of Qwen3.8-27B that retain near-BF16 quality across agentic tasks, math, multilingual reasoning, and image understanding.\nTo our knowledge, this is the first 3-bit-or-lower quantization that retains BF16 lvl performance on long-horizon agentic tasks.\nBenchmarks (BF16 / 3-bit / 2.5-bit / 2-bit):\n- DeepSWE: 39.8 / 41.0 / 41.9 / 28.6\n- Terminal-Bench 2.1: ",
      "body": "NariQ: Qwen3.8-27B in a 10 GB file, BF16-level on long-horizon agentic tasks\n(Nov 20, 2026) We are releasing NariQ v1 GGUFs of Qwen3.8-27B at 3, 2.5 and 2 bits per weight. Run them on our\nThe 3-bit and 2.5-bit files match BF16 on long-horizon agent tasks, math and image understanding. The 2-bit file\nNariQ is a trellis-coded post-training quantization (PTQ), calibrated on real coding-agent traces and stored in a\nlayout that runs on a single GPU.\nMore details are in the tech report.\nDeepSWE v1.1113 tasks × 3, mean pass@1\nTerminal-Bench 2.182 tasks × 3\nEach cell gives a percentage and a count in parentheses. For DeepSWE, the count is the mean number of fully solved tasks per round. Across the same three rounds, the mean share of each task's new tests that passed was 83.1% (BF16), 82.9% (3-bit), 83.2% (2.5-bit) and 77.2% (2-bit). One BF16 verifier timeout in round 3 counts as zero (338 of 339 trials graded). Each NariQ variant had all 339 trials graded. Terminal-Bench counts solved trials, and the remaining columns count correct answers. The agent runs use the Claude Code CLI (2.1.309, thinking on, effort max) driving the local model, with a 262,144-token context and the model's thinking-mode sampling (temperature 1.0, top-p 0.95, top-k 20).\nKL divergence from the BF16 model on held-out coding-agent traces, against file size. Every point was measured by us with the same code. Lower is better.\nDeepSWE fully solved tasks out of 113, against file size. Our NariQ points and solid BF16 line average three rounds with Claude Code. Our GSQ-RCO points use one round. The hollow diamonds show approximate averages from Unsloth's published Dynamic V3.1 chart (mini-swe-agent). Compare each point with the BF16 line for its harness. Higher is better.\nTerminal-Bench 2.1 score over 82 tasks × 3 attempts, against file size. Filled circles are our runs (Claude Code); hollow diamonds are Unsloth's reported results (mini-swe-agent), read from its published Dynamic V3.1 chart (hence ≈; its scores are averages over several passes). The two harnesses differ, so compare each point with the BF16 line of its own harness (solid: ours, dashed: Unsloth's). Higher is better.\nless memory; BF16-level on agent tasks\nthe smallest; weaker on math and agent tasks\nthe vision projector, for all three\nOne command on Linux with an NVIDIA GPU and driver 570 or newer (downloads the fork's binaries and the files, checks them, and fits the context to your GPU):\nbash run.sh 3.0   # or 2.5, 2.0\nOr download and serve yourself, with llama-server from the fork:\nllama-server -m Qwen3.8-27B-NariQ-3.0bpw.gguf --mmproj Qwen3.8-27B-NariQ-mmproj-BF16.gguf \\\n-ngl 99 -c 131072 --jinja --port 8080 \\\n--temp 1.0 --top-p 0.95 --top-k 20 --min-p 0\nThe sampling flags are the thinking-mode settings our scores used. -c 131072 fits a 24 GB GPU with little to spare;\nlower it if a display shares the GPU, or let run.sh pick the largest context that fits. Use -c 262144 (the model's\nfull context) on 32 GB or more. GPU memory with the vision projector:\nContext that fits on a 24 GB GPU\nGPU memory at the full 262K context\nPrebuilt binaries, the Docker image, supported GPUs and build steps: the fork's\nDecode speed on one NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 (Blackwell), single session, 512 greedy output tokens, tokens/s:\nNariQ on our llama.cpp fork with -fa on -ctk bf16 -ctv bf16 -t 1 -tb 16 --backend-sampling --samplers temperature (greedy sampling on the GPU, BF16 KV cache). UD-IQ3_XXS is Unsloth's file on llama.cpp, measured earlier on the same GPU type.\nKnown limits and future work\nRequires a NariQ-aware runtime: for now, our llama.cpp fork. Stock llama.cpp, Ollama and LM Studio can't load\nthe new tensor types yet; we hope to merge support into upstream llama.cpp. Our internal SGLang plugin and our\ninference engine built on Resin are a work in progress for potential public release.\nThe llama.cpp fork needs more work across hardware. The current fork is focused on SM120 performance (e.g. RTX\nPRO 6000). Ampere, Ada and Hopper GPUs and Apple M-series GPUs are slower than comparable GGUFs for now; we would\nlove open-source contributions for speedups, as this has been a solo effort. GPUs older than Ampere fall back to slow CPU execution, and AMD and\nIntel GPUs are not supported yet. GeForce cards have not been tested yet; tested GPUs are RTX PRO 6000, H100, L4\nApple M-series (Metal): build from source; on an M3 Pro (18 GB) decode is about 3.8 tok/s with the 3.0bpw file\n(4.9 with 2.0bpw), far slower than CUDA. Other Macs are untested.\nThe 2-bit file is weaker overall; use the 2.5-bit or 3-bit for higher overall performance.\nThe vision tower stays in BF16; only the language model is quantized. The MTP (multi-token prediction)\nmodule is not included yet; it is still to be quantized.\nKV-cache and activation quantization are next, so the files fit long contexts on 16 GB consumer GPUs and run\nfaster. Today the KV cache and activations stay in BF16.\nFor technical consulting, compute sponsorships, or partnership inquiries, please contact founders@narilabs.com.\nThese files inherit the base model's Apache-2.0 license; see NOTICE.\nThanks to the Qwen team for the amazing Qwen3.8-27B, and to the\nGSQ-RCO (IST Austria DASLab) and GGML teams, whose\nopen-source work this release builds on. NariQ's trellis codes build on QTIP\nIf you use NariQ, please cite the technical report:\ntitle       = {NariQ: Sub-3-Bit Quantization for Long-Horizon Agentic Tasks},\nauthor      = {Doyeob (Toby) Kim},\nFollowing the widespread community adoption of the Qwen3.5 and Qwen3.6 series, we are pleased to introduce Qwen3.8, the most capable generation in the Qwen open-model family to date.\nBuilt on the architectural foundation of Qwen3.5, Qwen3.8 delivers substantial gains across coding, professional work, research, and long-horizon agentic tasks. Qwen3.8-27B brings these advances to a compact, deployment-friendly dense model: a native vision-language model that understands images and videos, with flexible thinking control, designed to carry complex, multi-step tasks through to completion with greater reliability.\nQwen3.8-27B features the following enhancements:\nCore Capabilities: Comprehensive improvements across coding, professional work, research, and long-horizon agentic tasks.\nAgent Execution: Stronger autonomous planning and better handling of environment feedback, leading to more reliable end-to-end task completion.\nDownstream Compatibility: Broader support for popular harnesses and development tools, making it easier to integrate into your existing stack.\nFlexible Thinking Control: Thinking mode is on by default and can be disabled per request; reasoning depth can be tuned with reasoning_effort, and reasoning context from historical messages is retained via preserve_thinking.\nVision-Language Understanding: Native support for image and video understanding, from STEM diagrams and documents to hour-scale videos.\nType: Causal Language Model with Vision Encoder\nTraining Stage: Pre-training & Post-training\nHidden Layout: 16 × (3 × (Gated DeltaNet → FFN) → 1 × (Gated Attention → FFN))\nNumber of Linear Attention Heads: 48 for V and 16 for QK\nNumber of Attention Heads: 24 for Q and 4 for KV\nRotary Position Embedding Dimension: 64\nMTP (Multi-Token Prediction): trained with multiple steps\nContext Length: 262,144 natively and extensible up to 1,000,000 tokens.\nTo achieve optimal performance, we recommend the following settings:\nSampling Parameters: We suggest using the following sets of sampling parameters:\nThinking Mode: temperature=1.0, top_p=0.95, top_k=20, min_p=0.0, presence_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0\nInstruct (or non-thinking) mode: temperature=0.7, top_p=0.80, top_k=20, min_p=0.0, presence_penalty=1.5, repetition_penalty=1.0\nFor supported frameworks, you can adjust the presence_penalty parameter between 0 and 2 to reduce endless repetition. However, using a higher value may occasionally result in language mixing and a slight decrease in model performance.\nAdequate Output Length: To optimize performance on agentic tasks, we recommend allocating sufficient output length to allow the model to generate detailed and comprehensive responses. For frameworks that support separate token limits for internal reasoning and final outputs, we suggest the following configuration within the 1M context length:\nReasoning Content: Set the maximum output length to 262,144 tokens.\nFinal Response: Set the maximum output length to 131,072 tokens.\nThese settings provide the necessary capacity for complex reasoning while ensuring ample space for high-quality final deliverables.\nProcessing Ultra-Long Texts: Qwen3.8-27B natively supports context lengths of up to 262,144 tokens. For long-horizon tasks where the total length (including both input and output) exceeds this limit, we recommend using RoPE scaling techniques to handle long texts effectively, e.g., YaRN.\nLong Video Understanding: To optimize inference efficiency for plain text and images, the size parameter in the released video_preprocessor_config.json is conservatively configured. It is recommended to set the longest_edge parameter in the video_preprocessor_config file to 469,762,048 (corresponding to 224k video tokens) to enable higher frame-rate sampling for hour-scale videos and thereby achieve superior performance. For example,\nAlternatively, override the default values via engine startup parameters. For implementation details, refer to: vLLM / SGLang.\ntitle = {{Qwen3.8-Max}: A New Bar for Coding and Cowork},",
