    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-23-the-next-circle-of-security-hell",
      "title": "The next circle of security hell",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://blogs.gentoo.org/mgorny/2026/10/27/the-next-circle-of-security-hell/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50610334\nPoints: 143\n# Comments: 74",
      "body": "A few years back I wrote “The modern packager’s security nightmare”. Have things improved since? On the contrary, I feel like we’re slipping down a well-oiled slope with no end in sight. Vulnerabilities are reported at unprecedented rates. A lot of people are doing everything they can to patch them up, which doesn’t necessarily mean they’re not making things even worse. Complex software involves complex trees of dependencies, and every branch on that tree needs tending to. When they stop being reliable, people further down the tree start taking more weight. And there’s a limit of how much a single person can do, so what started as an effort to resolve an issue upstream may quickly become a weakness.\nIs software less secure these days? It’s tempting to say yes. I’d love to blame the corporate sector for egotistically dumping low quality software on us. I’d love to blame marketing-oriented people developing stuff with the sole goal of making a profit. I’d love to blame Rust for getting people obsessed about memory safety, and ignoring the kinds of vulnerabilities that aren’t caught by the compiler. Finally, I’d really love to blame slop. But the truth is, I don’t know. That’s really beside the point.\nThe point is that nowadays everyone and their chatbot became security researchers. Projects are being flooded with vulnerability reports. Even though pretty much every bug bounty was discontinued because of slop, people aren’t stopping. And even if we disregard truly invalid junk reports, there’s a lot left, and you have to deal with them somehow. You have to figure out how important they are, and how to fix them (or more likely, whether the fixes submitted are truly valid and don’t introduce regressions), or if you should reject them and why.\nIn the end, security is tricky. We’re seeing lots of bugs like REDoS, or other kinds of “performance bugs”. Are they really vulnerabilities? Well, it depends on the attack vector. And that in turn depends on how the software is used. Some scenarios are really contrived, and feel like “shell can execute dangerous commands if you type them in”. But then, you also have really serious issues such as “timing attack can be used to guess user’s secret”. You need to know a lot to make that kind of judgment, and sometimes your judgment doesn’t really match users’ expectations.\nIn the end, what can project maintainers do? Even if they have the expertise and the energy to judge, if they can “know better”, there’s likely a CVE assigned already. It may have absurdly high score. All security scanners will complain unless you fix it, and users will come asking or complaining, or attacking, or forking. I mean, technology can’t handle nuances. More than once I’ve been doing unnecessary security stabilizations over vulnerabilities that didn’t affect Gentoo (for example, because we already unbundled the vulnerable dependencies), to avoid false positives from security scanners. I’ve been also doing meaningless version bumps (with no real changes or no changes that applied to Gentoo) so that our packages don’t get reported as being “outdated”. It’s a hassle and it sucks, but it saves people from worrying.\nI’ve said this before and I’ll say it again: most projects aren’t really prepared to handle security issues. And it’s not just about triage. It’s about having LTS or security branches, it’s about making proper security releases with minimal changes. Unfortunately, it’s quite common for security fixes to be combined with new features, deprecations, API changes. In extreme cases, people are caught with their metaphorical pants down, and suddenly need to make a security release in the middle of a major rewrite, or along with barely tested new code. And then, people downstream discover that they need to quickly deal with major API changes to upgrade past a vulnerable version. These things keep piling up.\nWhat happens next? The community reaches a breaking point. People can’t keep up. You discover you can’t rely on others, so you work around them. You start bundling more stuff. Soon enough your workload goes beyond your ability. Software development becomes “every chatbot for itself”. The nightmare becomes worse, and the wheel keeps turning.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "qtqtqt",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T11:34:16.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://s0.wp.com/i/blank.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://blogs.gentoo.org/mgorny/2026/10/27/the-next-circle-of-security-hell/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-23-nospeaker-make-your-macbook-s-trackpad-talk-using-its-haptic",
      "title": "Nospeaker ─ Make your MacBook's trackpad talk using its haptic motor",
      "summary": "https://x.com/codycollier/status/2124505859883716918\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50610318\nPoints: 289\n# Comments: 165",
      "body": "_   __ ____      _____  ____   ______ ___     __ __  ______ ____\n/ | / // __ \\    / ___/ / __ \\ / ____//   |   / //_/ / ____// __ \\\n/  |/ // / / /    \\__ \\ / /_/ // __/  / /| |  / ,<   / __/  / /_/ /\n/ /|  // /_/ /    ___/ // ____// /___ / ___ | / /| | / /___ / _, _/\n/_/ |_/ \\____/    /____//_/    /_____//_/  |_|/_/ |_|/_____//_/ |_|\n─  haptic voice synthesis  ─────────────────────────────  trackpad  ─\nMute your Mac. Your trackpad will still talk.\nThere's no speaker in a MacBook trackpad, just a small haptic motor that taps the glass so you feel a click.\nNO SPEAKER drives that motor up to 4,000 times a second, fast enough to make sound you can hear, and turns it\ninto a voice. It talks, sings, raps, and becomes an instrument under your finger.\nOne command. About a minute and a half. No speakers used.\nWatch the demo on X, with the sound on.\n01 / before you start\n[ mute your mac ]        it still talks; that's the point\n[ put it on a table ]    on your lap it's much quieter\n[ hands off the glass ]  until it asks you to touch it\nSpace skips a part. q or Ctrl-C goes to the end.\ncurl -fsSL a90.com/nospeaker | sh\nDownloads the latest release to ~/.local/bin/nospeaker and starts the show. a90.com/nospeaker points\nto play.sh in this repo, so you can read what it runs first.\nGrab nospeaker.zip from the latest release,\nunzip it and run ./nospeaker in Terminal. Signed with a Developer ID and notarized by Apple.\nNeeds Xcode or the Command Line Tools (xcode-select --install). Builds one program for Apple Silicon\nand Intel Macs. Built with only the Command Line Tools, the Intel part needs macOS 13 or later.\n03 / play with it\n./nospeaker \"hello there\"            say anything\n./nospeaker --voice Daniel \"hello\"   in another macOS voice\n./nospeaker --sweep                  a rising tone, 100 Hz to 4 kHz\n--plain                              no visuals, plain text\n--external                           play through an external Magic Trackpad\nA Force Touch trackpad (built into MacBooks from 2015 on, or a Magic Trackpad 2 or newer), and macOS 12 or\nlater. Without a Force Touch trackpad, it says so and exits.\nMacBook Pro (Mac17,10)         M5 Max   macOS 27.2.1   everything works\nMacBook Pro (MacBookPro15,1)   Intel    macOS 15.7.4   works, built from source\nNot tested yet: a Magic Trackpad, and versions of macOS older than 15. MultitouchSupport is private, so\nolder systems may lack the functions it uses, and loudness differs between trackpads. If you try it on another\nMac, open an issue with your model, your macOS version and how it sounded.\n05 / how it works\nsay ──▶ formants ──▶ two tones ──▶ motor ──▶ glass\nper 10 ms    F1 + F2       haptics   sound\nWhy you can hear it. The trackpad has no speaker, but the motor moves the glass. Driven back and forth\nhundreds or thousands of times a second, the glass vibrates fast enough to make sound you can hear. It's quiet\nbecause the motor is built for short taps, not for sound.\nThe motor. MultitouchSupport is the private framework macOS uses for the trackpad. Its function\nMTActuationCreateFromDictionary takes a description of a vibration, a list of sine tones with a frequency,\na strength and a length each, and MTActuationActuate plays it on the motor. Apple's public haptics API only\nThe range. Measured with the MacBook's own microphone, every semitone from 98 Hz to 3,951 Hz: how loud it\nis over a quiet room, and how much of the sound is the note itself.\n│              .       ++++#+#+   .+++#####    # #.# #\n│   ++        +. . #..+++++#+#++++.+++#####+ #####.###          .#\n│   +++#.     +...+#..+++++#+#++++.+++#####+ #####.####+        .#\n131         262         523         1047        2093    3951\npublic notes stop at 600 Hz ─┘\nacross: every semitone, in Hz · up: loudness, +16 to +34 dB\n# clean note   + fair   . mostly buzz\nIt's loud from about 100 Hz to 4 kHz, with a few dead spots where the glass resonates. Its loudest, cleanest\nnotes are around 1 kHz, which covers the most important part of a voice.\nThe voice. macOS's own voice speaks the line into memory. Every 10 ms, linear prediction finds the voice's\ntwo strongest resonances (formants), its loudness and its pitch. The motor plays those two resonances as tones,\nrestarted on every beat of the pitch, so they buzz like a voice instead of whistling. Hiss sounds (s, f, t)\nbecome quick bursts of high tones. Two resonances beat three: in a blind test they were clearer, and louder,\nbecause the motor's strength is split fewer ways.\nIt sounds robotic, and it's much easier to follow when you can read along, which is why the show types every\nThe code. One Swift file, nospeaker.swift, with no dependencies.\nIs it safe? It never drives the motor past its normal maximum strength. Going past it is possible and\nlouder, but the driver starts to stall, so nospeaker doesn't.\nMultitouchSupport is private and undocumented, so a macOS update could break this.\nThe MultitouchSupport function names were learned from the open-source Tactile project. Everything else was\n──────────────────────  thanks for listening  ───────────────────────\nMade by Cody Collier · a90.com · MIT license",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "yakkomajuri",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T11:31:54.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://opengraph.githubassets.com/5d3b3b2c677f6f6b07edccbd832fee74023b297872104fa2fee8f40dc5af027d/os6/nospeaker",
      "originalUrl": "https://github.com/os6/nospeaker",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
