      "id": "bbc-2026-11-23-capita-shares-plunge-70-as-boss-quits-after-civil-service-pe",
      "title": "Capita shares plunge 70% as boss quits after civil service pensions failure",
      "summary": "Public sector workers have been hit by delays to pension payments, in a scheme overseen by Capita since last year.",
      "body": "Image source, Getty ImagesShares in outsourcing business Capita have sunk 70% after its boss stepped down as the saga over public sector pensions drags on.Chief executive Adolfo Hernandez has agreed with the firm's board to leave with \"immediate effect\", admitting that the company had \"not delivered\" for members of the UK's Civil Service pension scheme.The scheme, which manages the pensions of 1.7 million public sector workers in the UK, has been mired in controversy, with tens of thousands of retiring civil servants having faced delays receiving their pension payments.Capita has cut its profit forecast, partly due to the problems surrounding the scheme, and says it plans to raise £190m to bolster its finances.Capita took over the administration of the Civil Service Pension Scheme in December last year.One Cabinet Office minister last month labelled the company's progress in improving how it runs the scheme as \"abysmal\".Hernandez, who will be replaced by current chief financial officer Pablo Andres, said: \"I am proud of the groundwork we have laid and the progress we have made under my leadership.\"Of course, within that I recognise that we have not delivered the service that members of the Civil Service pension scheme deserve.\"Capita said it was now expecting its underlying operating profit for 2027 to be hit by between £85m and £95m, having previously expected a reduction of up to £40m.This comes from the extra cost of the recovery plan, as well the loss of two other \"major\" contracts in the defence and pension solutions divisions.Capita said it was planning an equity raise of £190m in the first half of next year, in order for the company to \"move forward from a more stable foundation\".The backlog of payments from the civil service pension scheme has been described as \"hell\" by people who have failed to receive money, some of whom retired early owing to ill health.BBC Radio 4's Money Box programme has been hearing from people caught up in the saga, and left in financial hardship.Sir Geoffrey Clifton-Brown, who chairs the Public Accounts Committee of MPs, said: \"The mishandling of the civil service pension scheme has caused severe hardship for some scheme members, particularly considerably delayed bereavement payments.\"The PAC will continue to pursue this dreadful matter and we can only hope that the new acting Chief Executive, Pablo Andres, will be able to get a grip on this whole affair.\"'Capita needs to steady the ship'Until the end of November 2025, the scheme was administered by a company called MyCSP.The contract was taken over by Capita on 1 December, in a seven-year deal worth £239m.\"Capita inherited an enormous backlog of cases which needed addressing and failed to deliver on the promised automation, AI and digital tools needed to manage the 1.7 million members,\" said Russ Mould, investment director at investment platform AJ Bell.\"Efforts to fix the problems failed and government deadlines came and went, leading to the withholding of milestone payments, with the National Audit Office probing the contract.\"Capita needs to steady the ship quickly, and hope that the modest recent improvements in service levels on the civil service scheme can be built upon.\"Get in touchHave you been affected by the problems with the civil service pension scheme?",
