      "id": "bbc-2026-11-23-househunter-swaps-australia-for-wales-most-affordable-town-w",
      "title": "Househunter swaps Australia for Wales' most affordable town - where locals are struggling to buy",
      "summary": "The area has attracted investors and even a buyer from Australia, but locals struggle to afford homes.",
      "body": "Househunter swaps Australia for Wales' most affordable town - where locals are struggling to buyImage caption, James Brown bought his house in Tylorstown for £80k after seeing it online while living in AustraliaByStephen Fairclough and Tony BrownBBC WalesReporting fromFerndale, Rhondda Cynon TafA house-hunter has moved 10,000 miles (16,000km) from Australia to Wales' most affordable town because the properties are so cheap.James Brown, 56, spotted the three-bed house in Tylorstown, near Ferndale, Rhondda Cynon Taf, online for £80,000 and put an offer in.\"It's ideal if you're commuting, and everything's virtually on your doorstep, so a really affordable area to come to,\" he said.But some people who live in Ferndale - dubbed Wales' most affordable town with an average property price of £116,200 - say they are still struggling to buy homes.Zoopla says a typical home in Ferndale costs \"less than twice the annual wages of two full-time workers\".The property selling website said its analysis shows this is the most affordable town in Wales.Residents have described how they have had to travel to work in England to afford a home in the area, while an estate agent said the low prices attract investors and buy-to-rent landlords from outside south Wales.Originally from Gloucester, when Brown moved back from Australia he decided to move to the south Wales valleys because of the property prices.He said the equivalent property in his home region would have cost him £200,000.Brown said: \"It's definitely a lot of outside buyers buying properties in Wales which I suppose in some ways is not helping the local people get on the ladder, but like I said, the affordability is just unreal in south Wales.\"Image caption, Emma Weston, 36, had to invest savings in her business rather than buy a homeEmma Weston, 36, who runs a café in Ferndale said she had to make the choice between using savings to invest in her business or buying a house.\"Obviously, I chose to keep my job and put the money here,\" she said.My partner, he's an electrician, a good trade, I obviously manage a business.\"We still have money but there's nothing left in the pot whereby I can say we can save X-amount to put down to a deposit.\"Weston knows property is more expensive elsewhere, but says Ferndale still does not feel affordable.\"I've lived outside of the valleys, therefore I can see the comparisons,\" she said.\"However, if you look at the cost of living crisis, which we're all facing, everything is just costing an absolute bomb.\"Image caption, Marcus Thomas, 31, is a homeowner and views it as an investment for his childrenCafé customer Marcus, 31, took a job nearly 100 miles (165km) away in Somerset so he could afford a family home.\"Moving to my new job gave that opportunity to buy the house,\" he said.Thomas said he liked the security of having his own home, and sees it as an investment for his children.But he said: \"I think if you were living in London, Reading... Birmingham... I'd say it is the easiest place to get a property because the house value here and there is completely different.\"For us, it's hard. For people coming into Wales is easy and is affordable.\"Image caption, Estate Agent David Ricketts says \"buy to let\" investors are attracted to areas like FerndaleEstate agent David Ricketts said the south Wales valleys are attractive to people outside the area.\"You're getting more for your money, there's less stress, there's a lower mortgage to pay and it's an area which I think will get better and better, it'll grow and you'd get a lovely welcome in any valleys town,\" he said.Ricketts said some buyers were not necessarily looking to live there themselves.\"What you've got now is a situation, not only just to buy, you have investors coming in because they've realised that the rental yield is quite reasonable and that will only grow,\" he said.\"These are very attractive areas to live and also to be a landlord.\"Image caption, Donna Thomas, 41, rents because she cannot afford a mortgageDonna Thomas, 41, rents in Maerdy with her partner and their three children.They rent because a mortgage is too expensive.\"If we're happy where we are, why change that? Why go into an even more stressful situation rather than just stay where we are and make do with what we have?\"It would be more stress, and everything would be harder, the money wouldn't stretch as far and the thing is we want to give the girls the best life that we possibly can with what we have,\" she said.Thomas said she was happy for investors to buy properties in the area and rent them back to locals.She said: \"It means that when you can't afford to get a mortgage on a house that you really like, if somebody else did buy it, and then rented it out to you, I would say that would be really good.\"I think I can't afford to get a mortgage on this house, but I really like this house, and now I'm renting it.\"Image caption, Ellie Thomas, 25, is saving for a deposit to buy a houseChiropractor Ellie Thomas, 25, lives with her parents in Ferndale.She is saving up for a deposit on a house near a city.\"I don't think I would want to live here,\" she said.\"I just feel like there's not much going on here. Most of the shops are shut. There's like one café, a few hairdressers, there's not really much.\"However, she understands it was likely to be more expensive and harder to buy elsewhere.\"Obviously, it's a bit hard to find somewhere nice in the area you want for an affordable price,\" she said.\"It would be easier for me to live here.\"It's a lovely community, everyone knows each other, but it's just a bit far out of the way, and petrol is expensive now too.\"More top stories",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
