    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-23-the-new-docker-built-to-run-on-trust",
      "title": "The New Docker: Built to Run on Trust",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.docker.com/blog/meet-the-new-docker-built-to-run-on-trust/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50607194\nPoints: 11\n# Comments: 11",
      "body": "Evolving an iconic brand for the agentic era\nToday, Docker is officially launching its new brand, and I’m excited to tell you what it’s all about and why we think now is the time for change.\nLet’s start with the big picture. We’re living through a transformative moment: for Docker, for developers, for the industry, and quite possibly, for humanity itself. AI is creating possibilities that would have sounded like science fiction not long ago.\nBut it’s also forcing the industry to confront an almost existential question: how do we harness all that power and intelligence without compromising security, governance, or control? Or, put more simply: how do we get to a point where we can actually trust agents?\nThat one seemingly simple yet infinitely complex question is why we’re here today, introducing you to a new Docker.\nA new Docker, but a familiar story.\nOk, not exactly here, but once upon a time, a decade or so ago, the tech industry was at a similar inflection point. Digital transformation was the term du jour, promising a new way of engaging customers and powering the global economy. There was one big constraint, though: teams needed a better way to build, ship, and run software consistently across any environment.\nEnter a company you might have heard of: Docker.\nTogether with all of you, Docker built the standard trusted by millions of developers. For over a decade, developers have relied on us to make software portable, open, reproducible, and secure. Docker turned the frustration of “Well, it works on my machine!” into the assurance that “Yes, it works everywhere.”\nToday, of course, the challenge confronting us is new and evolving, and we all need to evolve with it. We cannot simply continue to do what we’ve always done and expect to succeed in this new era. That applies to business leaders, developers, tech companies, everyone. It certainly applies to Docker. That’s what this rebrand is about: evolving to meet the moment for our customers.\nLet me be unequivocal: this is not about leaving Docker’s legacy or heritage behind. We’ve spent more than a decade earning your trust. This is about building on that foundation, not replacing it.\nThe qualities you value us for aren’t changing. We’re still committed to openness and industry collaboration. We’re still, and always will be, developer-first. We’re still fanatical about simplicity, and about showing up everywhere you need us.\nOn the product front, the containers you know and love aren’t going anywhere. Containers still play a huge role in the software development lifecycle. As long as that’s the case, Docker will continue leading the way.\nContainers weren’t designed for agents\nAgents have introduced a new problem. Containers isolate workloads. But agents aren’t workloads. Agents are actors.\nAgents make their own decisions about what to run, what to access, and what to change. Developers and enterprises need solutions built specifically to isolate and govern them, so they can give those agents real autonomy without giving up control.\nDocker’s next chapter is about delivering exactly that: security, isolation, and governance, so we can all trust agents to do meaningful work without feeling like we’re always one step from catastrophe.\nIn case I haven’t made it blindingly obvious yet, the new Docker is all about trust.\nTrust Docker because our track record speaks for itself. Trust Docker because we’re proven, neutral, and secure. Trust Docker because it’s pretty darn clear you can’t trust the agents to manage themselves.\nTrust us because we have a software supply chain that is already the standard for building securely. Trust us because nobody isolates agents where they run better than we do. Trust us because we’re taking a comprehensive approach to improving governance and minimizing risk across the entire enterprise.\nTrust Docker because we’re not egotistical enough to think we can solve a challenge this big alone. Trust us because everything about our history, our heritage, and our actions over the past year tells you we’ll partner with the smartest and build alongside the best, and not create vendor lock-in.\nMost of all, trust Docker because we’re not just talking the talk. We’ve got the goods to back it up: trusted supply chain for your agents to build from, trusted runtime to isolate your agents, and a trusted ecosystem to ensure all the layers of trust are covered, whether made by Docker or not.\nA whale of an evolution\nAs our product team innovated to meet the moment and our messaging in the market began to shift, one thing became clear: the speed and scale of evolution happening inside Docker called for an equally momentous evolution on the outside. Something that makes it unmistakably clear that it’s a new day for Docker. Something that says and shows, loud and clear, for all the world to see, that Docker is not just about containers anymore.\nSomething, dare I say, whale sized.\nI’m talking, of course, about our beloved Moby.\nIf you know Docker, then you know Moby, and if Docker is evolving, Moby should too. The only question was to how and to what extent? We looked at a lot of whales, folks. (I may or may not have been seeing whales in my sleep.) Ultimately, with the help of our friends at the strategic brand transformation company, Motto, we landed on a new Moby for the new Docker.\nIn many ways, Moby is a microcosm for Docker and the changes we’re making: the Moby you love and trust, evolved for a new era. Just as the new Docker is no longer singularly defined by containers, neither is the new Moby.\nCome take a look around\nWe’ve got lots to show you. Poke around our new website. Find and engage with the new Docker brand on social media. And if you want to see what trust in the agentic era looks like in practice, try Docker Sandboxes and Docker AI Governance. We’d love to hear what you think.\nWherever this new era takes us, Docker will always honor and cherish the trust you continue to place in us. Now, let’s go build what’s next.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "peer2pay",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T03:34:29.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://www.docker.com/app/uploads/brand/social/docker-og-default.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.docker.com/blog/meet-the-new-docker-built-to-run-on-trust/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 5,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-11-23-kodak-s-next-collectible-is-a-tiny-digital-photo-frame-that-",
      "title": "Kodak’s next collectible is a tiny digital photo frame that doubles as a tracker",
      "summary": "After expanding its Charmera collection with a Millennium Edition featuring Y2K-inspired designs earlier this year, Reto's follow-up to its tiny Kodak camera is an equally small digital photo frame you can use to display your shots. But while the Charmera's usefulness stopped at snapping grainy photos and terrible videos, the Kodak SlideTag goes one step further. It also functions as a Bluetooth t",
      "body": "After expanding its Charmera collection with a Millennium Edition featuring Y2K-inspired designs earlier this year, Reto’s follow-up to its tiny Kodak camera is an equally small digital photo frame you can use to display your shots. But while the Charmera’s usefulness stopped at snapping grainy photos and terrible videos, the Kodak SlideTag goes one step further. It also functions as a Bluetooth tracker that’s compatible with either Apple’s Find My or Google’s Find Hub tracking networks.\nThe Kodak SlideTag will be available for $34.99 each and, like the Charmera, will feature a series of retro designs distributed in mystery boxes so you don’t know which one you’re going to get. Kodak also sells a complete set of all eight vintage designs for $279.92 if you don’t like mysteries; however, there’s a ninth “secret edition” only available in the individual boxes with a sleek metallic finish.\n[Image: The SlideTag is a bit smaller than a classic Kodak film slide, but far more functional and versatile. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/11/kodak2.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nFor those who grew up in an era where projectors are small enough to integrate into phones, the SlideTag is designed to look like a modernized version of the film slides that were used to project photos decades ago. Instead of film, the SlideTag features a 1.54-inch color E Ink screen that mixes six colored inks to display photos at a resolution of 240 x 240.\n[Image: Illustrations with bold, defined colors will translate best to the SlideTag’s color E Ink screen. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/11/kodak3.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nThe color reproduction won’t be as accurate as an LCD or OLED screen, but the SlideTag can hold and cycle up to 12 photos transferred to the device over Bluetooth from Kodak’s Memory Link app running on a mobile device. You can also use it to display a short to-do list, contact details, a countdown timer, or a QR code linking to wherever you want.\nAs a tracker it’s not quite as functional as an Apple AirTag or Google’s Pixel Tag, which use features like ultra wideband (UWB) and Channel Sounding to make them easier to find. The SlideTag sticks to basic Bluetooth with a range of up to 30 meters out in the open and a 75-decibel buzzer to help locate it when you’re close. Battery life will vary depending on how frequently you use tracking features or refresh the displayed image, but the SlideTag uses a common CR2032 coin cell battery that’s easy to replace.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Gadgets",
        "News",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Andrew Liszewski",
