    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-22-price-wins-grand-slam-to-end-wait-for-major-title",
      "title": "Price wins Grand Slam to end wait for major title",
      "summary": "Gerwyn Price ends his four-year wait for a major PDC title as he powers to a brilliant 16-11 victory over Gian van Veen in the Grand Slam of Darts final.",
      "body": "Gerwyn Price ended his four-year wait for a major PDC title as he powered to a brilliant 16-11 victory over Gian van Veen in the Grand Slam of Darts final. The Welshman last won a major title when he claimed the World Series in 2022, but finally got over the line after defeats in three finals in 2026. The 41-year-old was the runner-up at the World Matchplay, World Grand Prix and World Series, as well as suffering semi-final exits in the Premier League, World Masters, UK Open and European Championships. This title has been long overdue and Price won it in spectacular fashion with a mid-match burst. Despite six breaks of throw in the opening 14 legs - including brilliant 150 and 145 checkouts from Price - the game was level at 7-7.But Price claimed a crucial lead in a scrappy 15th leg when he finally took out double two at the sixth attempt. That flicked a switch after a mini-interval and Price won the next five legs to open up a six-leg advantage. Van Veen broke back after another interval, but Price was able to hold off any hopes of a comeback from the Dutchman and seal his fourth Grand Slam title. \"It's amazing - I've had some close calls this year when I probably should have won games and tournaments, but when it finally comes around it's nice,\" Price, who moves to world number three, told Sky Sports. \"I was probably more nervous in this game than ever. Just to keep the media off my back, I mean I do mind and I don't mind, but just to keep them off my back.\"Now they can leave me alone!\"The triumph, which sees Price earn £200,000 and the Eric Bristow Trophy, is his first in a PDC ranking and TV major since he won this event in 2021.The pair will now switch their focus to the Players Championship Finals from Friday, where they could meet in the second round. That event will determine Price's place in the PDC order of merit and his seeding for the World Championships which begin on 11 December. The draw takes place on 30 November. Across the 19 editions of the Grand Slam, seven of the winners have gone on to triumph at the World Championship in the weeks that follow.It has happened in each of the past four years, with Luke Littler (twice), Luke Humphries and Michael Smith doubling up.The past 10 years have delivered five winners of both in the same season, while another two Grand Slam winners have reached the Worlds semi-finals and one the quarter-finals.Therefore a Grand Slam winner usually has a pretty decent run at the Worlds, although with Van Veen as second seed and Price either third or fourth, that would hardly be a huge surprise.Of the 19 World Championship winners since the Grand Slam's inception, 11 have reached at least the semi-finals in Wolverhampton - or Coventry, which staged the 2020 event (seven winners, two runners-ups and two semi-finalists).The good news for Littler and Humphries, who were both beaten in the last 16 this week, is that five winners of the Worlds have also been knocked out at the same stage of the Slam.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T21:06:47.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/e2ae/live/3b964520-e6bc-11f1-99a1-253766e7ad8b.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/darts/articles/cklye10j4z1yo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-22-a-beginner-s-guide-to-local-ai-with-inkling-qwen-and-unsloth",
      "title": "A Beginner's Guide to Local AI with Inkling, Qwen, and Unsloth",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.gpupartner.com/blog/beginners-guide-local-ai-inkling-qwen-unsloth\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50603845\nPoints: 668\n# Comments: 270",
      "body": "We have AI at home! Set up your own free personal ChatGPT on your PC with Unsloth and Inkling or Qwen. It's private, and the whole house can use it.With the launch of open-weight models like Qwen, cloud-based LLM providers seem like they're more of infrastructure or appliance businesses when it comes to most routine tasks that an AI model can handle.OpenAI and Anthropic have displayed a pattern of dropping model quality in the 'latest' version and controlling exactly what you're allowed to run on their models. Add to this ballooning costs and privacy concerns, and we get a pretty good case for moving away from this 'convenient' cloud experience.That's basically where I was too. I wanted to offload certain token-heavy research, data collection, or coding tasks to an AI model without staring at usage limits all day. So, as any reasonable human being would do, I decided to set up my own local AI: a Claude and ChatGPT replacement that runs at home on my own hardware.Since this was my first attempt, my primary focus was keeping it simple and hosting it on Windows. If you're a beginner, you can follow along to get one going on your home network too.Once you have the hardware sorted, it takes about 10-15 minutes (excluding download times).The short versionInstall Unsloth Desktop, download a quantized AI model that fits your GPU's VRAM, set your context size, and turn on LAN access so everyone at home can use it.What Do You Need to Run a Local AI at Home?Not much (other than the hardware). Here's a short list:A Windows PC with a graphics card (GPU). The more video memory (VRAM) it has, the bigger the model it can run. I'm using an RTX 5090 with 32GB VRAM for this setup.Unsloth Desktop. A free app that 'serves' the AI model to you through a chat interface. Get it from Unsloth.An AI model sized to fit your GPU. We'll pick one in the next section.That's it. No terminal commands, Python environments, or subscriptions required.Which AI Model Can Your GPU Run?It depends on how much VRAM your graphics card has. As a rough guide, 8-12GB of VRAM handles smaller 9B-12B models, while 32GB comfortably runs 27B-35B models with plenty of room for long conversations.On consumer-grade hardware, we'll need smaller models. The full-size Qwen model sits at around 56GB and won't really fit on a consumer graphics card unless you're running a Mac with unified memory. That's where quantization comes in.What Is Quantization?Quantization shrinks a model by compressing weights and moving to a lower precision. You basically trade some accuracy for a model that actually fits on your GPU and runs faster.The same photo stored at 8, 4 and 2 bits per pixel. A model's weights lose detail the same way going from Q8 to Q4 to Q2.If you're wondering whether this tradeoff is worth it, well, it depends. You'll find that certain types of workloads don't really care and give similar outputs, while others may be nearly unusable.Which Model Should You Run for Your VRAM?For this guide, we'll be going with Qwen3.8-27B with 4-bit quantization on my system with an RTX 5090. Going up a tier is an option thanks to its 32GB of VRAM, but I wanted to allow for a larger context window (how much of the conversation the model can keep in mind at once) rather than cramming in the largest model that fits.Qwen3.8-27B at each precision with a 128K context, from our VRAM calculator. At 4-bit, the model and the context fit a 32 GB card with room to spare.If you want to run a model of this caliber with excellent performance, a hilariously overpriced RTX 5090 (remember when $1,999 felt too high?) is unfortunately still not a terrible 'deal' these days. The other option is way more expensive enterprise-grade GPUs.If you're on a GPU with less VRAM, you can still get something very usable going right away. The table below gives you a rough idea of where you can start.SizeVRAMModels to tryVersion (quant)Context sizeCards at this sizeSmall8-12 GBQwen3.5-9B, Gemma 4 12BUD-Q4_K_S or Q4_K_M8K-16KRTX 5060 8GB, RTX 3060 12GB, RTX 5070 12GBMedium16 GBQwen3-14B, gpt-oss-20b (MoE), Gemma 4 26B (MoE), Qwen3-Coder-30B (MoE), Qwen3.6-35B (MoE), Inkling 2 Mini (MoE)Q4_K_M or Q5_K_M16K-32KRTX 5060 Ti 16GB, RTX 5070 Ti, RTX 5080, RTX PRO 2000Large24 GBGemma 4 31B, Qwen3.8-27B, Inkling 2 MiniUD-Q4_K_M or Q4_K_S16K-32KRTX 3090, RTX 4090, RX 7900 XTX, RTX PRO 4000Extra Large32 GB+Qwen3.8-27B, Inkling 2 MiniUD-Q4_K_XL or Q6_K64K-128K+RTX 5090 32GB, RTX PRO 4500 32GB, RTX PRO 5000 48GB, RTX PRO 6000 96GBModels marked MoE (mixture of experts) only run a few billion of their parameters for each token. They keep a decent speed on a smaller card with the rest held in system RAM, which is why a 35B model sits in the 16 GB row. On an RTX 5060 Ti 16GB, our calculator puts Qwen3.6-35B at about 47 tokens per second this way.Feel free to experiment with several models until you find one that fits your needs. You can also use GPUPartner's VRAM Calculator to get an estimate.Can You Run Inkling 2 on an RTX 5090?Inkling 2 Mini, yes. Inkling 2 Small, no.Inkling 2 Mini is the newest model in this size class. Thinking Machines Lab released it on November 17, 2026: 26.4B parameters, Apache 2.0, vision support and a 1M-token context. Unsloth's recommended file, UD-Q4_K_XL, is 17.6 GB. That is within 0.1 GB of the Qwen3.8-27B file in this guide, so it sits on a 32GB card the same way and leaves about 14GB for context.Nothing else in this guide changes. Update Unsloth Desktop first, because Inkling 2 needs a recent build, then search for Inkling 2 Mini in the same drop-down in Step 2.Inkling 2 MiniMemory neededFits entirely on2-bit10-11 GB12 GB card3-bit13-15 GB16 GB card4-bit (recommended)17-18 GB24 GB card8-bit (lossless)24 GB32 GB cardThose figures are Unsloth's. They count RAM and VRAM together and cover the model alone, so a long context needs more. Inkling 2 Mini is a mixture-of-experts model with 5.4B parameters active, so the 4-bit file also runs on a 16 GB card with the remainder in system RAM, a little slower.Inkling 2 Small is small in name only. It is a 594B model, and the smallest file Unsloth publishes is 171.7 GB. That is unified-memory territory: a 256GB Mac Studio or a DGX Station, not a graphics card.Can You Run a Local AI on a Mac?Yes. You'll find good alternatives in Apple's Mac lineup thanks to its unified memory. Macs are pretty good at running larger models, even if they don't offer the best performance. Just make sure you grab the MLX flavor that is custom-built for Apple Silicon.The same trade applies to unified-memory PCs like the MSI PRO MAX EDGE AI+ 11M and NVIDIA's DGX Spark.How Do You Set Up a Local AI with Unsloth Desktop?It takes three steps: install Unsloth Desktop, download your model, and set the context size. I'll go over these steps in more detail below so you can follow along.Step 1: Install Unsloth DesktopFirst, we'll need a solution that 'serves' the AI model to you. My pursuit of simplicity narrowed my choices down to LM Studio or Unsloth Desktop. Both abstract away a ton of terminal commands and handle Python environments and dependencies behind a nice, simple UI, which makes them excellent picks for a first home setup.I landed on Unsloth Desktop mostly because, well, I liked the name better. Also, it didn't try to push the very thing I was trying to escape when I opened its website: a subscription.The community seems to like it overall too, so that's another point in its favor.Once you've clicked through the installer and launched Unsloth, you'll see a window like this one.Unsloth Desktop on first launch, before any model is loaded.Step 2: Download a Quantized ModelOn the top left of this main window, you can pick the model that you want to use from the drop down. We'll be using Qwen3.8-27B.The model picker. Qwen3.8-27B is first on the Recommended list.You should see a submenu like the one below when you click on a model. I'll be using Unsloth's UD-Q4_K_XL version. The M and S versions are smaller, but the XL version behaves much closer to the full-size model without filling up our VRAM, so it seemed like a good pick for my GPU.Every quantization of Qwen3.8-27B with its download size. The gear next to the downloaded one holds the context setting.Step 3: Set the Context SizeOnce the download completes, you should see a gear icon to the right of the model as shown in the screenshot above. Here, you can set your context size manually or leave it on Auto if you aren't sure how much you need.I'll be setting mine to 128K (unquantized) and might reassess after I've run the model through a few of my routine tasks for a few weeks. Again, you can use this VRAM calculator to help you with these numbers for your specific GPU.At this point, our first local AI is up and running, and you can start chatting with it right inside Unsloth.Remember, your mileage may vary depending on your setup.How Do You Share a Local AI Across Your Home Network?Short answer: Unsloth Desktop has LAN sharing built in. Set a Remote password, start LAN access, and share the address it gives you with anyone on your home network.Once Unsloth Desktop is set up with a model, you might want to give family members or friends access too. For now, we'll only serve it over our local network, so my family can use it without needing physical access to this PC.Head into Unsloth Desktop settings (gear icon on bottom left), and look for the 'Remote & LAN' tab.Settings, Remote & LAN. LAN access is the second card. The Remote access card above it is for a later part.Most of this section will be greyed out until you set a Remote password. Once you've set a password, you'll see a 'Start' button for LAN access. Click that and you should see an address that anyone on your home network can open in a browser to use a local AI model within a chat interface. They'll sign in as 'unsloth' with the Remote password you set.Just keep in mind that the PC needs to stay on with Unsloth running for anyone to reach it.Can You Access Your Local AI Across the Internet? Soon™Not with this setup alone. Everything so far is limited to your local home network.If you just need a chat interface like ChatGPT, Gemini, etc. to work within your home network, you're basically done at this point! You and anyone on your network can start talking to an AI model hosted on your machine right away.In the next parts, we'll cover performance, tunneling, as well as setting up an agent that runs locally and uses only the web tools you allow it to use. This will let you automate more complex tasks like coding, research, and so much more without being tied to a subscription or usage limits and censorship.As always, my focus remains simplicity that allows my family to use it without too much guidance or help.PS. If you have any recommendations for agentic harnesses or even models to try, feel free to let me know and I'll give them a go!SourcesSourceDetailsUnsloth Desktop documentationFree, open-source app for macOS, Windows and Linux; runs GGUF and MLX modelsUnsloth Desktop: LAN accessRemote & LAN settings, LAN access start and port behaviourQwen3.8-27B model cardOfficial 16-bit weights (~56 GB) and parameter countUnsloth Qwen3.8-27B GGUFQuantized files and sizes, including UD-Q4_K_XL (~18 GB)Unsloth: Inkling 2 Mini run-locally guide26.4B parameters, Apache 2.0, 1M context, UD-Q4_K_XL at 17.6 GB, memory by precisionUnsloth: Inkling 2 Small run-locally guide594B parameters, smallest file 171.7 GBThinking Machines Lab: Inkling-2-Mini model cardOfficial weights, released November 17, 2026NVIDIA GeForce RTX 509032 GB GDDR7 consumer GPU used for this build",
