      "id": "bbc-2026-11-22-centrica-boss-says-he-is-hugely-concerned-over-impact-of-soa",
      "title": "Centrica boss says he is 'hugely concerned' over impact of soaring energy bills",
      "summary": "Chris O'Shea says the number of people unable or unwilling to pay their energy bills will increase when prices jump in the new year.",
      "body": "The boss of British Gas owner Centrica says he is \"hugely concerned\" about the impact the expected jump in energy bills in January will have on consumers.Chris O'Shea told the BBC he thought the number of people unable or unwilling to pay their energy bills would rise when prices go up in the new year.The new energy price cap to take effect from January will be announced on Wednesday and consultancy Cornwall Insight has forecast it will jump by £443 a year for a typical household.The government has said some households will get £100 to help with bills, but one Citizens Advice adviser said it was a \"drop in the ocean\" while a pressure group called for action on \"inflated energy firm profits\".Speaking to the Sunday with Laura Kuenssberg programme, O'Shea said the predictions for a \"substantial increase\" in bills from January were \"materially correct\", adding that the first quarter of the year was the time when most energy was consumed.\"That's right at the point where people will be paying for Christmas and the like. So I am hugely concerned,\" he said.O'Shea added there was a \"massive problem\" with people not paying their bills in the UK, with some not able to pay their bills and an \"increasing number of people that are choosing not to pay their bills\".He said the collective impact of that was that \"every single electricity consumer, is paying about £1.50, £1.60 a week to cover the cost of people that are not paying their bills, and it's just going to get worse when bills go up\".The amount owed to energy suppliers by households hit £5bn in the April to June period, according to energy regulator Ofgem.Wholesale energy prices paid by suppliers have been rising this year as a result of the conflict in the Middle East, which has pushed up bills for households.Cornwall Insight has predicted Ofgem's energy price cap will rise by 26% to £2,166 a year for a typical household from 1 January.The price cap affects about 20 million households in England, Scotland and Wales and sets a maximum price for each unit of gas and electricity, so the actual level of bills will vary according to energy usage.The government has said some households will get a £100 payment to help with energy bills, a policy which was announced in the Budget.The government has also temporarily cut 5% VAT from electricity bills until the end of June next year, which is expected to save the typical household about £45 a year.But consumer groups have argued the £100 payment announced in the Budget will not be enough for some.One Citizens Advice adviser said it was a \"drop in the ocean\" and that we would see \"cold-related deaths\" this winter.\"For someone in a lot of debt, who can't afford their bills, it's not going to touch the sides.\"The Fuel Poverty Action Group said the government and Ofgem needed to take stronger action against energy companies.\"Our inflated energy prices are driven by inflated energy firm profits,\" the group said.\"Government and Ofgem must take stronger action against this blatant profiteering whilst we suffer in cold homes, or are forced into debt.\"Energy firms operating in the UK are subject to a windfall tax that was introduced in 2022.When the windfall tax is added to other taxes levied, it means the overall tax rate on profits earned by oil and gas companies operating in the UK is 78%.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
