      "id": "bbc-2026-11-22-move-on-former-pms-pile-in-with-advice-for-andy-burnham-but-",
      "title": "'Move on!' Former PMs pile in with advice for Andy Burnham, but will he listen?",
      "summary": "Theresa May, David Cameron and Tony Blair have weighed in on Burnham's plans on moving closer to the EU, but the prime minister's message itself is not very precise.",
      "body": "Then of course, there is the politics at home. When cornered on this subject the prime minister and his cabinet often say the \"British people will be the boss\" - going on to cite polling that clearly displays disappointment with Brexit and a discernible interest in going back in.But that's not the same as polling suggesting the public is demanding the government puts it at the top of the list.As one senior Labour figure puts it, there has \"certainly been a shift - and an openness to have the conversation\" about rejoining, but the PM \"has to be really careful about locking himself into a manifesto commitment that alienates a lot of people\" alongside plenty of voters who might welcome a move.What of May's warnings about getting tangled up in EU talks at the exclusion of all else?One government insider from the time asks: \"Can you even remember anything else that happened between 2017 and 2020? It's extremely naïve to think he can do all the other things that are extremely pressing, like defence, or Neets, while trying to play out a future with the EU - it won't happen.\"It's worth noting, as some ministers do privately, that Burnham himself was not working in Westminster during the most intense struggles over leaving the EU - an advantage perhaps, that he doesn't carry that baggage, or a gap in his experience of how bad it was.And yet, the prime minister - who, crucially, is confident in his ability to communicate with the public - is plainly resolved on exploring going back into the European Union, and choosing to take that argument on.After what the government considers to have been a successful summit, that adventure looks more tempting to the many in Labour's tribe who think he is right to do exactly that.After the last 72 hours, opportunity is knocking - but the warnings from prime ministers past ring out too. Under May, Brexit affected nearly everyone and everything - trying to go back in under Burnham could perhaps affect everyone and everything too.",
