      "id": "bbc-2026-11-22-bbc-apologises-for-any-offence-caused-by-strictly-noise-disr",
      "title": "BBC apologises for 'any offence caused' by Strictly noise disruption",
      "summary": "Lancashire police says a mobile phone has been seized and it is assessing whether any criminal offences may have taken place.",
      "body": "The BBC has apologised \"for any offence caused\" after lewd audio was heard during the live broadcast of Strictly Come Dancing.A recording of sex noises appeared to be playing from the audience in Blackpool's Tower Ballroom as judge Motsi Mabuse gave feedback early in Saturday's episode. She paused before joking: \"It's not me.\"At the end of the programme, presenter Emma Willis apologised to viewers \"if you heard any audio disruptions\". A BBC spokesperson also issued an apology and said it was \"investigating how this happened\".Lancashire police said it was assessing whether any criminal offences may have taken place, after receiving a formal complaint and a mobile phone being seized.The moment was streamed on social media by content creator Daniel Jarvis, whose videos include pranks. He appeared to place a phone call before the audio began playing in the ballroom, and suggested he was trying to target judge Craig Revel Horwood for his \"bullying\".Jarvis has previously claimed he was responsible for sex noises disrupting Match of the Day in 2023.Willis told viewers: \"Before we see the judges' leaderboard, we'd like to apologise if you heard any audio disruptions earlier in tonight's show and for any offence caused.\"",
