      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-22-leonida-pc-recompilation-of-the-xbox-version-of-gta-6",
      "title": "Leonida – PC Recompilation of the Xbox Version of GTA 6",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://github.com/ItsNotRexed/Leonida\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50595168\nPoints: 77\n# Comments: 42",
      "body": "Grand Theft Auto VI (Xbox Series) running natively on Windows, built on your PC from your own copy.\nLeonida is a static recompilation project. The Xbox Series CPU is x86-64 like a PC, so the game's code is loaded\nand run natively, and the console's system software is reimplemented. The console's precompiled RDNA2 GPU programs\nare translated to SPIR-V for Vulkan. The game's models, textures and audio are read from your package. All of this\nhappens on your PC, from your own copy of the game.\nImportantThis repository does not contain the game, keys or an executable, and it does not explain how to get them. You\nneed your own copy, with its files already readable on disk. Nothing here decrypts or works around any protection.\nEverything the build creates (the generated shaders, extracted assets, the runtime folder and your saves) comes\nfrom your game and stays on your PC. Never share those files.\nEarly. The game does not boot yet. Everything that can be done without the game's executable is in place, so\nwork on booting can start as soon as one is available to a developer.\nFinding and checking your copy (Xbox Series 1.0.2.1)\nShaders: carve, unpack native RDNA2 code, disassemble\nShaders: RDNA2 → SPIR-V translation\n✅ All 26,503 (disassembled with LLVM 21; LLVM 23 leaves 1,471 rejected for now, #644): 2,236 VS, 12,101 PS, 2,689 CS, 9,282 mesh, 195 geometry. All pass spirv-val, with a per-shader metadata index for pipeline creation. ⚠️ 21,545 are wave64: exact on 64-wide subgroups (AMD). On 32-wide subgroups (NVIDIA, Intel), compute and mesh shaders are exact with wave64 emulation (--wave64-emulate, on by default there); cross-half wave ops in vertex, pixel and geometry shaders are still approximate\nShader translator checked on a real GPU\n⚠️ The hand-written GPU tests (vertex fetch, texture sampling, exports, ALU ops, partial-register ops, barycentrics, wave-uniform branches, compute LDS/barriers/buffer stores, wave64 masks) have been run on more than 30 GPU and driver combinations, with results in #166: NVIDIA Turing, Ampere, Ada and Blackwell (Windows and Linux); AMD GCN3, RDNA1, RDNA2, RDNA3 and RDNA4 (Windows driver and RADV); Intel Gen9 (ANV); Apple M1 (MoltenVK, no geometry or mesh stages); and Mesa llvmpipe. Wave64 tests need 64-wide subgroups (AMD) or LEONIDA_W64_EMULATE=1. These are small test shaders: the game's own shaders are checked by spirv-val and by driver pipeline creation (vk_pipeline_check), not by their output\nModels (OBJ with real names, UVs, material references)\n✅ Exported. Not all layouts are decoded yet\nTexture dictionaries and world texture pools (DDS)\n✅ Exported. Pool entries stored as BCPack are still skipped by the export; a clean-room decoder (tools/bcpack.py) rebuilds BC1, BC3, BC4 and BC5, and is not wired into rpf9dump yet (notes)\nAudio banks (AWC: Opus, XMA2)\n✅ Exported whole. Opus repackaged losslessly as Ogg Opus; PCM (codec 0/2) as WAV; XMA2 decoded with ffmpeg. --wav adds 16-bit PCM WAV (notes)\n? Measured. 1,589 audio-config records with exact durations and AWC stream ids; station grouping and content-id lists; no per-track start offsets (notes)\nPE loader and import shims\n⚠️ Runs a stand-in executable end to end (gta6rt --run: CRT startup, statics, heap, files, threads, GDK imports through the shims, exit code back). Untested against the real executable\n⚠️ In progress. A Vulkan-backed device behind the Xbox-ABI proxy creates queues, command lists, fences, descriptor heaps, committed and reserved resources, render-target and depth views, texture views and samplers (which go into the bindless set), and query heaps. Barriers, clears, uploads, copies (BC included), colour resolves and occlusion, timestamp and pipeline-statistics queries run on Vulkan, checked on one GPU. No buffer views, root signatures, pipelines, draws or swapchain yet (plan)\nBooting, story mode, anything playable\nThe port isn't downloaded ready-made. It's built on your PC from your own copy.\nDownload this project with its submodule: git clone --recursive https://github.com/ItsNotRexed/Leonida. A\n\"Download ZIP\" from GitHub leaves out third_party/rpf9-explorer. The build fetches it if git is installed,\nand otherwise tells you what to do.\nIt finds your copy of the game by itself: it looks in Downloads, Desktop, Documents, Games, your home folder and\nthe top of every drive, two folders deep. It recognises the version from a few file sizes, without reading\nanything large. If you have more than one copy it asks which one, and if it finds none it asks you to paste the\nfolder. Before starting it checks that everything below is installed and that there is enough free space, and it\nlists anything missing in one go. The result goes to Documents\\Leonida. A stopped build continues where it left\nYour game folder is the one that holds MicrosoftGame.Config and the .rpf archives, as plain files.\nYour own copy of the Xbox Series package, version 1.0.2.1.\nWindows 10/11 x64, with about 350 GB free for work files. They can go on another drive with --work.\nPython 3.12+ (tick \"Add python.exe to PATH\").\nVulkan SDK, for spirv-as / spirv-val.\nLLVM 21+ with the AMDGPU target for llvm-mc. The official\nWindows build has no AMDGPU target. On Windows, scripts\\build_llvm_mc.ps1 builds a native llvm-mc.exe with it\n(no WSL; needs git and the Visual Studio C++ tools below) and sets LLVM_MC. Or install llvm-21 in WSL and set\nLLVM_MC to the whole command, LLVM_MC=\"wsl -d Ubuntu -e /usr/lib/llvm-21/bin/llvm-mc\" (see\ndocs/design.md). On Linux, llvm-mc on PATH is enough.\nVisual Studio 2022 with Desktop development with C++ and the Windows SDK 10.0.26100 or newer, for the\nruntime stage (the runtime uses version 1.2 root signatures, which older SDKs don't declare).\nffmpeg on PATH (optional: decodes the XMA2 streams, and required for --wav).\nFrom a terminal, with the same behaviour and more control:\npython -I make_game.py --find                          # list the copies found on this PC\npython -I make_game.py --check                         # check your copy, build nothing\npython -I make_game.py --game \"D:\\path\\to\\your\\package\" --out \"D:\\Games\\Leonida\" --work \"E:\\leonida-work\"\npython -I make_game.py --stages shaders,assets         # only some stages\npython -I make_game.py --game \"D:\\path\\to\\your\\package\" --wav   # also decode Opus to PCM WAV (large)\npython -I make_game.py --wave64-emulate off            # auto (default), on or off; see below\n--wave64-emulate decides whether wave64 compute and mesh shaders are translated to be exact on any subgroup size\n(see \"Wave64 emulation\" in docs/design.md). NVIDIA and Intel GPUs have 32-wide subgroups and need\nit. The default, auto, asks vulkaninfo (part of the Vulkan SDK) and turns it on unless every GPU reports 64-wide\nsubgroups, or when it can't tell; it also turns it on when the GPU the runtime would use can't be made to run\ncompute and mesh shaders 64-wide. The mode is saved next to the translated shaders, and changing it translates them\nagain. With auto, a work folder that previously used emulation keeps it for later translations and retries, even if\nthe current GPU supports native wave64. Use --wave64-emulate off to translate it natively.\nThe build records which translator sources produced the shaders. A change to those files reruns translation using the\nsaved disassembly. Older work folders need one translation after upgrading to record this information.\nStages run in order: check, shaders, assets, runtime, pipelines (creates every translated shader on your\nGPU driver and saves a pipeline cache, so the first boot doesn't compile them). The shader translation is the longest step: it runs\non --jobs processes (half the CPU threads by default).\n--wav is off by default because 16-bit PCM WAV is several times larger than Ogg Opus. It decodes the repackaged Ogg\nOpus streams to work\\assets\\audio\\wav after the audio bank step; existing WAV files are skipped when a build resumes.\nWith CMake 3.24+, a C++20 compiler, and Vulkan development headers and loader installed:\ncmake -S runtime -B runtime/build/linux\nPass the directory containing index.json and the translated SPIR-V files to vk_pipeline_check.\nmake_game.py runs on Linux too. The pipelines stage uses the vk_pipeline_check built above\n(runtime/build/linux, or runtime/build). The runtime stage builds on Windows only, so on Linux it needs a\nWindows build of runtime/; without one it is left out of the default stages, and asking for it fails before any\nRunning the loader on Linux\nThe loader is a Windows program and runs under Wine or Proton; CI runs its tests under Wine on every change to\nruntime/ (the wine job of the Runtime workflow). Give the runtime stage a folder with gta6rt.exe and\ngta6rt_tls_host.dll, such as the runtime-windows artifact of that workflow's windows job, unzipped:\npython3 -I make_game.py --runtime-build ~/Downloads/runtime-windows\nIt copies both files into --out and writes gta6rt.sh next to them, which starts gta6rt.exe with wine, or\nwith the Wine named by WINE (for Proton, its files/bin/wine). The same option skips the build on Windows.\nEvery package file's name, size and SHA-256 for each supported version\nCarves DXBC containers from shaders.rpf without its encrypted table of contents. Unpacks the XBCH → FSSM native RDNA2 program, disassembles it with LLVM (gfx1013) and translates it to SPIR-V\nFinds validated resources inside RPF9 archives and the loose .ttpf texture pools\nMaps the PE image, resolves imports to shims (GDK, XMem, d3d12_xs)\nPE/RTTI inspection, raw carving, texture tiling probes, GPU test harnesses\nThe translator's runtime contract (user SGPRs, push constants, memory model, bindless images, vertex fetch,\nsystem values) and all the format notes are in docs/design.md; the format, asset, runtime\nand validation notes are indexed in docs/README.md. The Xbox D3D12 plan is in\ndocs/d3d12_vulkan.md. Audio bank formats and the export tools are in\ndocs/audio.md; docs/related_projects.md records the prior art we may\nread and the licence limits on it.\nShader translator on hardware: compute, mesh and geometry now translate (see the translator contract in\ndocs/design.md); still open are the vertex-shader input layout (v0 = VertexIndex is\nunverified if the console runs VS as NGG) and wave64 cross-half code in VS/PS/GS on 32-wide subgroups.\nXbox D3D12 → Vulkan: the d3d12_xs command list and descriptor heap model.\nModel layouts the decoder rejects today.\nSee CONTRIBUTING.md. Never attach game files, extracted assets or generated shaders to issues or\nrpf9-explorer by breadbbbb: the RPF9 model, texture-pool and AWC\ndecoders used by tools/rpf9dump (git submodule).\nMesa (MIT): GFX10 resource-descriptor layout (tools/gfx10_formats.json),\nand the ACO/RADV rules used as reference for 16-bit results and shader system registers.\nlibHttpClient (MIT) by Microsoft: the XTaskQueue/XAsync implementation\nin third_party/xtaskqueue, used by the offline xgameruntime shim.\nLogo: \"Grand Theft Auto VI\" icon by SlyDevil on SteamGridDB.\nGrand Theft Auto and all related names are trademarks of their owners. This project is not affiliated with or\nGPLv3 (or any later version). See LICENSE. Third-party code keeps its own license.",
