    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-21-catherine-talks-openly-about-how-cancer-changed-her-in-docum",
      "title": "Catherine talks openly about how cancer changed her in documentary on BBC",
      "summary": "\"There is a much, much deeper part to what a cancer diagnosis shifts in you,\" says Catherine in the documentary.",
      "body": "Catherine talks openly about how cancer changed her in documentary on BBCFigure caption, Watch: Trailer for new programme detailing Princess Catherine's cancer journeyThe Princess of Wales has spoken about how \"cancer does change you,\" in a forthcoming documentary called Heal to be shown on the BBC.\"It changes your body, it changes you physically - but there is a much, much deeper part to what a cancer diagnosis shifts in you,\" says Catherine in the first glimpses from the documentary, which features her private thoughts on her illness and recovery.The highly personal film, to be screened on BBC One on 29 November, will see Catherine share her experiences of cancer, saying: \"Healing is a journey.\"It includes new footage of her completing the Three Peaks Challenge earlier this year, which involved climbing the highest peaks in England, Scotland and Wales over a day.\"It's clear we're not meant to carry life's challenges alone,\" says Catherine's voice-over, accompanied by images of other cancer patients and the princess hugging her own family, including Prince William.\"It is often through pain that we realise there is a greater beauty that lies beyond that,\" she says, once again reflecting the sense of change from her cancer experience.Image source, Matt Porteous/Kensington PalaceImage caption, Kensington Palace shared a photo of Catherine doing the Three Peaks Challenge, ahead of the documentaryCatherine has never disclosed what type of cancer she faced, but in March 2024 she revealed that she had a diagnosis of cancer and was receiving chemotherapy.In January 2025 she announced that she was in remission and in the following months began a gradual return to royal duties.She has often spoken about her different understanding of life after her illness, and the need to take a more holistic approach to well-being beyond physical recovery.The princess has talked about the good days and bad days that followed her treatment - and wanted to dispel the idea that patients could immediately return to how they felt before their illness.This documentary, which will be introduced by Kirsty Young, promises to be the fullest and most personal account of that journey, with her own insights into the impact such illness can have and how she found her own healing process.She has since returned to working for her charities and earlier this week was in India for the Earthshot Prize ceremony with Prince William, the first time they had travelled together on overseas royal duties since the princess's cancer treatment.Image source, EPA/ShutterstockImage caption, Catherine visited India for William's Earthshot Prize which was hosted in Mumbai - the first time she'd been at the Earthshot awards since 2022Among the film crew for the documentary, as director of photography, was Will Warr, who has worked with Catherine and her family before.His pictures have been used in a video birthday message by Catherine and a Christmas card which showed a relaxed image of the princess and Prince William and their children.The filmmaker also made the personal video statement that marked the end of her chemotherapy in September 2024, with her message: \"Out of darkness, can come light.\"Kate Phillips, the BBC's chief content officer, said it was hoped the 30-minute documentary \"will resonate with BBC viewers and provide comfort and support to those navigating their own health challenges\".Catherine was treated at The Royal Marsden Hospital and funds from the distribution of this documentary, which is to be broadcast in other countries, will be paid to The Royal Marsden Cancer Charity.When cancer patients finish treatment at the London hospital they can symbolically ring a bell.\"I didn't ring the bell,\" says Catherine, speaking to her brother James Middleton.\"Maybe this is your ringing the bell,\" he says, about carrying out the Three Peaks Challenge and this documentary about her healing.Heal will be shown on BBC iPlayer from 12:00 GMT and BBC One at 18:40 GMT on 29 November. It will also air internationally, including on NBCUniversal in the US on 30 November, Channel 9 in Australia on 1 December and CTV and Noovo in Canada on 7 December",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T12:00:03.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/c010/live/8fc02730-e5a7-11f1-8df2-d7de7bfd91b9.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c85y14zvexz9o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
