      "id": "bbc-2026-11-21-staff-gagging-clauses-lifted-ahead-of-probe-into-culture-at-",
      "title": "Staff gagging clauses lifted ahead of probe into culture at data watchdog",
      "summary": "Ministers are expected to publish the details of how an independent review of the working environment at the Information Commission will be carried out next week.",
      "body": "Non-disclosure agreements (NDAs) signed by staff at the UK's information watchdog will be lifted to allow them to speak out about its culture, the government has confirmed.Ministers are expected to publish the details of how an independent review of the working environment at the Information Commission will be carried out next week.It follows the departure of the watchdog's boss five months ago amid allegations of sexually inappropriate behaviour.A government spokesperson confirmed that NDAs, known as \"gagging clauses\", would not be enforced by the Information Commission, saying the regulator \"will not use the terms of any agreement to prevent people from participating in the review\".The government \"will set out further details on the review, including its terms of reference and the independent reviewer, shortly\", they added.The Information Commission is one of the UK's most high-profile regulators charged with the crucial task of regulating AI, data protection and Freedom of Information requests.It recently won a court battle with TikTok after it fined the social media platform £12.7m for misusing children's data, and has penalised bodies including the Ministry of Defence after an email blunder exposed the details of interpreters fleeing Afghanistan.But it has been without permanent leadership since Information Commissioner John Edwards resigned earlier this year.It came after an independent investigation found a \"case to answer\" over reports of alleged inappropriate behaviour towards staff including claims of sexual harassment.The Information Commission said the investigation found Edwards' behaviour \"fell short of the conduct expected from a public official\".In a resignation statement posted on his LinkedIn page in June, Edwards said he disagreed with how the investigation had been carried out but accepted his position had \"become untenable\".The statement continued: \"I have accepted that there have been occasions where I exercised poor judgement and made attempts at humour that were inappropriate and caused offence.\"Freedom of Information requests have found that since the start of Edwards' tenure in 2022 to the present, 16 current and former employees have signed non-disclosure or confidentiality clauses costing more than £354,000.Nine of those , externalincluded a \"non-derogatory clause\" which legally prevents employees from making negative comments publicly.While this is a small proportion of the more than 1,000 staff employed by the commissioner, government advice stipulates that public bodies should only use confidentiality clauses \"when necessary\" and that they should \"not seek to stifle or discourage staff from raising concerns\".Since 2025, new laws also mean they cannot be used to silence employees subjected to harassment or abuse.After Edwards' departure, the government announced a review, external into the Commission's culture and governance, with the then-Secretary of State for Science, Innovation and Technology Liz Kendall telling a committee of MPs she was \"absolutely appalled at what the independent investigator found - sexual harassment and bullying\".Five months later, the investigation has not begun.In recent weeks, the two major civil service unions, the PCS and FDA, have pushed for an external examination of the events leading up to Edwards' resignation in meetings with government officials.The unions have also asked for those who signed non-disclosure agreements with the ICO to be released from them so they can contribute to the review.The PCS union says other concerns had previously been raised with them about Edwards, including a bullying complaint, which was investigated at the time. An internal staff survey, published in August and released under FOI, external points towards wider issues at the Commission, with 14% of all employees reporting they had either personally experienced bullying, harassment and discrimination.Multiple current and former employees have also told the BBC of experiencing or witnessing incidents of bullying or harassment.PCS General Secretary Fran Heathcote said the union's members at the Commission \"have been failed for too long by a toxic leadership culture that has allowed bullying and harassment to persist, leaving many staff fearful of speaking out\".Heathcote said she welcomed the \"positive engagement\" the union had received in recent weeks from the Commission and officials at the Department for Culture Media and Sport (DCMS), which oversees it.But she added: \"For the review to command the confidence of staff, it must be genuinely independent, robust in its approach, and prepared to identify where accountability lies.\"Conservative MP Caroline Dinenage, who chairs the DCMS select committee, said the Commission \"needed a firm new direction\" after the departure of its former boss. \"Instead, a leadership vacuum has compounded what went before at a rudderless organisation where it seems the worst behaviours in the workplace are not being challenged,\" she added.She said it was \"vital\" the government brought in \"someone who can set a new tone and is dedicated to changing attitudes and protecting staff\" to head up the Commission. A spokesperson for the Information Commission said its commitment to an independent review reflected its \"ongoing commitment to listening and learning from the events of the past year and those leading up to it\".\"Our new Board, appointed this summer, has been working exceptionally hard to provide fresh leadership, ushering in a new governance structure and a new strategy,\" they added.\"It is committed to listening carefully to the review's findings and recommendations, and building on the positive work on our culture already taking place across the organisation.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
