    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-20-price-survives-match-darts-to-reach-grand-slam-semis",
      "title": "Price survives match darts to reach Grand Slam semis",
      "summary": "Gerwyn Price faces two match darts before winning a thrilling quarter-final against Nico Springer at the Grand Slam of Darts, as Luke Littler's conqueror Jermaine Wattimena is knocked out.",
      "body": "Gerwyn Price faced two match darts before winning a thrilling quarter-final against Nico Springer at the Grand Slam of Darts, as Luke Littler's conqueror Jermaine Wattimena was knocked out.Three-time champion Price led for most of the match but his German opponent refused to let him out of reach.Springer missed a number of chances to level, including three darts at double top to make it 13-13 as he fell 14-12 behind, before he eventually made it 14-14 and then took a 15-14 lead.However, Price produced a stunning 143 checkout to take it to a decider in which Springer had the throw.The German left himself 73 after his 16th 180, only to miss double eight and double four for the match prior to Price taking out 78 with treble 18 and double 12.\"I'm quite lucky to come through that game,\" Price told Sky Sports. \"I missed so many chances - I should've punished him.\"I kept him in the game, I kept missing doubles after doubles. Even though he was scoring as well as I was, I was missing opportunities after opportunities but I'm glad I had another one at the end.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T22:30:34.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/fca0/live/97982430-e52f-11f1-9bfd-15139fdcafee.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/darts/articles/ck7928yjd1dgo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-20-nasdaq-erases-all-2026-gains",
      "title": "Nasdaq erases all 2026 gains",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/19/stock-market-today-live-updates.html\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50583254\nPoints: 14\n# Comments: 0",
      "body": "Stocks tumble FridayThe three leading U.S. indexes experienced a massive sell-off on Friday.The S&P 500 dropped 3.7% to close at 6,910.39, while the Nasdaq Composite plummeted 5.28% to 22,617.73. The Dow Jones Industrial Average declined 949.73 points, or 1.93%, to 48,147.33.— Sean Conlon'Risk is to the downside' when below the 200-day moving average, BTIG saysAll three major averages were trading below their 200-day moving averages on Friday, and BTIG believes more turmoil could be ahead.\"Generally speaking once something is below a 200 DMA, the risk is to the downside,\" said Jonathan Krinsky, the firm's chief market technician.Thursday marked the sixth consecutive day of declines for the S&P 500, and Krinsky found that over the last 30 years, there are only three prior instances of six-day losing streaks that close below the 200-day moving average but not by more than 3%.\"The 'slightly' good news is that all three saw SPX higher five days later. The not so good news is that all of them saw meaningful downside after that small pop,\" he said.When assessing Thursday's performance, Wolfe Research found that the broad market index's \"violent\" reversal after its attempt to move back to its 200-day moving average at the open was succeeded by the \"worst breadth reading\" since last December, seeing decliners outnumber advancers by 12:1.\"Broken trends and deteriorating momentum remain significant headwinds,\" wrote technical analyst Rob Ginsberg in a Thursday note. \"The bulls need to mount a powerful defense here to stabilize the market and begin reversing the broader deterioration.\"— Sean ConlonGas prices fall ahead of Thanksgiving, but remain above last yearA customer purchases gas at a station on September 15, 2026 in Chicago, Illinois. Scott Olson | Getty ImagesThe national average for regular gasoline fell to $3.69 a gallon on Friday, down from $3.72 on Thursday, according to AAA. Prices are still about 59 cents higher than a year ago.The drop comes ahead of Thanksgiving next week, with 73 million people expected to take road trips. \"It's possible the national average will drop another 15 cents by next Thursday and avoid setting a new record,\" according to AAA's post.Brent ​crude futures, the international benchmark, were up 0.64% to $92.57 a barrel on Friday, while U.S. West Texas Intermediate futures for December rose 0.93% to settle at $83.98 a barrel.Stock Chart IconStock chart iconBrent and WTI prices - YTD— Deena Zaidi and Nick WellsHumana upgraded to buy at TD CowenIn this photo illustration, the Humana Inc. logo is displayed on a smartphone screen.Rafael Henrique | SOPA Images | Lightrocket | Getty ImagesHumana shares rose 3% after TD Cowen upgraded the healthcare services company to buy from hold, raising the price target to $485, implying a 28% upside from the stock's Thursday close of $380.Stock Chart IconStock chart iconHumana - YTD\"Taking into account 2026 performance, 2027 Medicare Advantage benefit design and 2028 Stars results, we see a relatively clear path towards a meaningful step-up in 2027 EPS growth and  more than $40 EPS in 2028,\" the bank said in its note.Adjusted EPS estimates for 2026, 2027, and 2028 remain unchanged at $9.51, $18.41, and $40.42, respectively.— Deena ZaidiAnthropic tops Vanguard's retail investor trading charts in debutDaniela Amodei, co-founder and president of Anthropic PBC, center left, and Dario Amodei, chief executive officer of Anthropic PBC, during the company's initial public offering (IPO) at the Nasdaq MarketSite in New York, US, on Thursday, Nov. 19, 2026.Michael Nagle | Bloomberg | Getty ImagesAnthropic was the most-traded single stock by Vanguard's self-directed retail traders on Thursday, underscoring the buzz among mom-and-pop investors around the artificial intelligence leader's historic IPO.Just about 0.2% of Vanguard's self-directed investors bought Anthropic on Thursday, which was its first day on the public market. These investors bought a median of 20 shares or roughly $1,950.Vanguard said about around out of every 10 Anthropic buyers were men. By comparison, men account for 55% of Vanguard's investor base.To be sure, Vanguard said a higher percentage of its base traded SpaceX during its public market debut session in June.Anthropic jumped 17% in trading on Thursday after it went public. But the stock has dropped nearly 10% in afternoon trading Friday, which is its first full day on the market.— Alex HarringBTIG's Krinsky says technicals show the sell-off isn't over yet BTIG's chief market technician Jonathan Krinsky in an appearance on CNBC's \"The Exchange\" Friday pointed to technicals that make him think the sell-off isn't over just yet. He specifically noted Apple, the largest stock by market cap, which is only down by 3% from its 2026 highs earlier this month. \"When the S&P has been down at least 8% or more, when it's made its final bottom, the median drawdown for Apple has been 23%,\" he said. \"It would be historically unprecedented to see a final bottom on the S&P with Apple where it is right now.\" He added that 50% of the S&P 500's stocks are above their 50-day moving average, a level that is higher than a number closer to below 40% — or even 20% — when durable bottoms hit. Krinsky did note, though, that relief for semiconductors may be coming, as the group is retreating to levels that it last traded at in early April when the group started to surge higher. The iShares Semiconductor ETF (SOXX) in late trading Friday was at levels it last closed below on April 9. Stock Chart IconStock chart iconApple vs. S&P 500 1 month. — Davis Giangiulio Anthropic debut underperforms other blockbuster IPOsCEO of Anthropic Dario Amodei inside the Nasdaq MarketSite ahead of the anticipated IPO of artificial intelligence company Anthropic in New York City on November 19, 2026. Charly Triballeau | Afp | Getty ImagesAnthropic shares gave back much of that advance on Friday, falling more than 12% in afternoon trading. — Sean ConlonTrump wants to ban U.S. companies from building data centers abroad US Vice President JD Vance and US Secretary of State Marco Rubio listen as US President Donald Trump speaks during a press briefing in the Brady Briefing Room of the White House in Washington, DC, on Nov. 20, 2026. Ken Cedeno | Afp | Getty ImagesPresident Donald Trump said on Friday that he wants to stop U.S. companies from building data centers in other countries. \"We're going to fight very hard for the data centers,\" Trump said at a White House press conference. He added that companies would face tremendous penalties for building data centers abroad. \"We want them to build it here.\"He added that he is concerned about U.S. companies building in China instead. \"We're not allowing that to happen,\" he said. — Kevin Breuninger and Davis GiangiulioVIX volatility index climbs above 30, up as much as 13% intraday The VIX volatility index, a gauge of how much the S&P 500 might move in the next 30 days based on options trading, rose another 13% intraday Friday to 32.27, after jumping as much as 16% Thursday, according to FactSet data.The VIX, a gauge of skittishness on Wall Street, has now risen for six of the past seven days. The index hasn't closed above 30 since late March.Stock Chart IconStock chart iconThe VIX in November— Scott SchnipperFed Governor Cook warns about falling behind on inflationLisa Cook, governor of the US Federal Reserve, during the Stanford Institute for Economic Policy Research (SIEPR) Policy Forum at Stanford University in Stanford, California, US, on Wednesday, May 27, 2026. David Paul Morris | Bloomberg | Getty ImagesFederal Reserve Governor Lisa Cook expressed caution about the dangers posed by inflation, saying Friday that if the central bank doesn't act now it risks having to do more later.\"Inflation has been above target for more than five and a half years, and every additional day poses greater risk that price- and wage-setters will adjust their inflation expectations upward,\" Cook said in remarks delivered in Chile. \"Furthermore, waiting longer to implement rate increases risks getting behind the curve,\" she added. \"If we fall behind, additional and larger rate increases may be required later to catch up, which could increase stress on our financial system and have unnecessarily damaging effects on American households.\"Cook was one of only two FOMC members who voted against the pause. Markets strongly expect the committee to approve a quarter percentage point hike at the Dec. 8-9 meeting.— Jeff CoxTech sector heads for worst week since April 2025The tech selling intensified in late-morning trading, with the sector down more than 5% on the day. That decline pushed its week-to-date losses to 10.5% as of 11:57 a.m. ET. That would be tech's biggest one-week drop since April 4, 2025, when the Trump administration announced massive tariffs on imported products. Stock Chart IconStock chart iconXLK year to date— Fred ImbertNasdaq Composite turns negative for 2026The Nasdaq Composite dropped Friday, turning negative for 2026, as even Anthropic's IPO and Nvidia's earnings failed to revive the artificial intelligence trade. The tech-heavy index was last lower by 2.6% for the trading session, falling slightly on the year.  Stock Chart IconStock chart iconNasdaq Composite, 1-day— Sarah Min Bitcoin breaks below $60,000 for the first time since JulyBitcoin signage in Times Square in New York, Dec. 9, 2025.Michael Nagle | Bloomberg | Getty ImagesBitcoin slid more than 4% with the broader market, falling below the $60,000 level for the first time since July.The flagship cryptocurrency was last trading at $59,554.95 and is now heading for a retest its July low of $57,737.35.Stock Chart IconStock chart iconBitcoin, 1-dayEther fell 6% to $1,650.79, its lowest level since July 2.  — Tanaya MacheelConsumer sentiment reading moves higher as inflation outlook improves sharplyA woman shops at a store in New York, the United States, Nov. 4, 2026. Zhang Fengguo | Xinhua News Agency | Getty ImagesThe outlook for consumers improved in November as expectations for inflation eased, according to a University of Michigan survey Friday.The survey's headline sentiment index rose to 51.5, up 7.3% from a month ago and exactly in line with the Dow Jones consensus estimate. Inflation expectations at the one-year horizon declined sharply, down to 3.7%, which was a full percentage point below a month ago and well below the mid-month November reading of 4.3%. This was the lowest reading since March. The five-year outlook slipped to 3.3%.\"Despite the softening in inflation expectations, consumers continue to report overwhelmingly that inflation—rather than unemployment—will cause greater hardship for consumers in the next one or two years,\" survey director Joanne Hsu said.— Jeff CoxThe recent losses in tech stocks and the S&P 500 'have further to run,' says Capital EconomicsThe recent pressure seen in technology stocks as well as the S&P 500 may not be over yet, according to James Reilly, senior markets economist at Capital Economics.\"The success of Anthropic's IPO yesterday as other AI-related stocks tumbled highlights how different parts of the AI ecosystem no longer move as one,\" he wrote in a Friday note. \"We think the falls in tech stocks and the S&P 500 have further to run — and how this plays out within the stock market will depend on what is driving these falls.\"Reilly believes the S&P 500 index should fall to 6,500 by the end of next year. The index closed at 7,175.83 on Thursday.— Sean ConlonStocks open lowerThe three major averages were trading in the red on Friday morning.The Nasdaq Composite declined 1.3% just after 9:30 a.m. ET, while the S&P 500 moved down 0.5%. The Dow Jones Industrial Average lost 48 points, or 0.1%.— Sean ConlonSeaport is ‘cautious’ on Anthropic after its record-smashing IPOPeople walk past a billboard advertising the artificial intelligence company Anthropic in Times Square on its initial public offering (IPO) day in New York City on Nov. 19, 2026.Leonardo Munoz | AFP | Getty ImagesAnthropic made its historic Wall Street debut on Thursday. But one research firm is taken a cautious stance on the stock.Seaport Research initiated coverage of the artificial intelligence stock with a neutral rating. \"Anthropic is an incredible company, but it is also asking for an incredible leap of faith by investors,\" analyst Jay Goldberg said in a note. \"We think the company will need time to grow into its valuation and answer some very big questions about its growth trajectory. We are not negative on the company, but we are cautious.\"CNBC Pro subscribers can read more here.— Liz NapolitanoAnthropic, Ross Stores and Intuit among the stocks making moves before the bellAnthropic: The stock was down more than 9%, a day after the AI giant made its public market debut. The stock rallied 17% on Thursday, though it closed well below its highs of the session.Ross Stores: Shares fell 5% as the discount retailer's forecast disappointed traders.Intuit: Shares slid roughly 8% in premarket trading after the financial software company cut its fiscal 2027 revenue outlook, citing a slowdown at Credit Karma.Check out CNBC's live blog here to read more about the stocks making premarket moves.— Deena Zaidi, Darla Mercado and Fred ImbertVIX rises above 30The CBOE Volatility Index (VIX) was trading above 30 on Friday morning as the selling pressure on U.S. equity futures ramped up.The index, also known as Wall Street's \"fear gauge,\" was last at 30.99, reaching its highest level since March.Stock Chart IconStock chart iconVIX, 1-day— Sean ConlonUbisoft drops 14% as outlook disappoints On Jan. 9, Ubisoft said it was postponing the launch of \"Assassin's Creed Shadows\" and had appointed advisors to review strategic options.Ina Fassbender | AFP via Getty ImagesShares of French video game publisher Ubisoft fell 13.2% in European morning trade, after the maker of the \"Assassin's Creed\" franchise forecast net bookings of around 250 million euros ($274 million) for the October to December period.That was below a 361 million euro consensus estimate cited by analysts at TD Cowen and represents a fall from 338 million euros in the same quarter last year, at a time when hype in the market is being dominated by the launch of Take-Two's \"Grand Theft Auto VI\". Ubisoft cited a \"particularly crowded holiday season\" in its outlook. Ubisoft on Thursday also reported net bookings down 16.5% year-on-year in its fiscal first half, with second-quarter net bookings of 389 million euros, above its guidance of around 370 million euros.Stock Chart IconStock chart iconUbisoft share price.— Jenni ReidTreasury yields are sharply lower as investors flee to safe havensU.S. Treasury yields fell on Friday, as an equities sell-off drove investors into safe-haven assets ahead of Federal Reserve Governor Christopher Waller's speech later in the session.The 10-year Treasury yield — the key benchmark for mortgages, loans and credit card debt — was down 6 basis points at 4.989%.The 2-year Treasury note yield, more sensitive to short-term Federal Reserve interest rate decisions, was also 6 basis points lower at 4.713%. The 30-year Treasury bond yield – which moves on wider geopolitical developments – was down 4 basis points at 5.361%.— Natalie Feary U.S. tech giants tumble in premarket; global chip firms downPremarket moves point to continued downward momentum in Big Tech, including Anthropic, which is seen 6% lower at 4:25 a.m. ET after its blockbuster debut. Nvidia slipped 1.9% in premarket with the stock at its lowest level in more than a year following a rollercoaster period. Among other Mag 7 stocks, Alphabet, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon and Tesla dipped into the red, while Apple nudged 0.5% higher. Semiconductor stocks fell, with Broadcom down 3%, AMD losing 4.95% and Micron shedding 3.8%. European chip players ASML and ASM International dropped 5.8% and 5.4%, respectively. That came after Japanese semiconductor stocks tumbled during Friday trade, with Tokyo Electron closing 7.06% lower, Lasertec shedding 9.21% and Renesas Electronics losing 7.02%.— Jenni Reid No market can weather a proper unwind of the tech trade: investment strategistBénédicte Lowe, equity derivatives strategist at BNP Paribas, told CNBC there was a \"mind-boggling\" disconnect in the fact that a market sell-off has coincided with the biggest-ever IPO in Anthropic — but that investors were \"panic selling\" as they try to gauge whether AI sentiment has peaked.The sell-off is now in a second leg in which investors are even ditching traditionally defensive plays in a broad risk-off environment, she said. Tech worries are seeping into sectors such as FTSE 100 mining companies, hit by fears of a recession.\"No market can weather a proper unwind of the tech trade,\" Lowe said.Any upside going into year-end will require a stabilization of the tech story and market moves, she added. — Natalie Feary and Jenni Reid Highly rated corporates is where 'everyone is going to park their money,' analyst saysMatthias Reschke, head of European Investment Grade Finance for JP Morgan, told \"Squawk Box Europe\" that corporate spreads are looking \"very resilient\" in the wake of the equity market sell-off, though the market is \"much more nervous than it used to be.\" With risk appetite down across markets and spreads widening, even for investment-grade corporates, he said this is where he expects \"everyone is going to park their money.\"The big question is if inflation will come down next year, he added. Reschke expects persistent inflation pressures, meaning government 10-year yields may \"remain elevated for a while.\"— Natalie Feary European stocks open lower, joining global tech sell-offEuropean stocks fell at Friday's open, with the benchmark Stoxx 600 index down 0.83% after hitting a four week-low on Thursday. The region's technology stocks joined the global sell-off, last down 2.7%, with chip stocks ASML, ASM International and Be Semiconductor among the worst performers.Stock Chart IconStock chart iconStoxx 600 index.— Jenni Reid'Lot of upside' in AI stocks despite growing market skepticism, Anthropic investor saysThe market is skeptical about \"all layers of the AI layer cake\" but that can be a \"great setup for investors,\" Brett Winton, chief futurist at ARK Investment Management who has Anthropic in all his portfolios, told CNBC's \"Squawk Box Europe\" on Friday. A key worry is that the huge R&D spend of Anthropic, OpenAI and SpaceX will generate sufficient returns, he said.\"The market really thinks that Anthropic's revenue per gigawatt of compute has essentially peaked and is only going to go down from here. And the history and the returns they've got to their training suggest exactly the opposite, where they already have amazing gross margins,\" he said. \"What they're getting is the ability to serve more intelligence per GPU [graphics processing unit] that they use, and as they deliver on that, they're able to monetize per gigawatt of GPU more successfully... That's why you've seen Anthropic's growth margins expand as quickly as they have, even as their top line has been explosively growing. So we think that's going to continue.\"On concerns about the infrastructure buildout around AI, Winton said people increasingly doubted whether companies will be willing to pay for the compute they're delivering to market. \"We take exactly the opposite view. So the overall market for knowledge workers we think is a $40 trillion market for AI software selling into the knowledge workforce, and so there's plenty of room to run if collectively Anthropic and OpenAI are only annualizing revenue on the order of $150 billion per year right now.\" \"So we're really in the earliest, earliest stages of what will be a really dynamic game, and we think there's a lot of upside from here.\"— Jenni Reid SoftBank sees worst day in over two years as AI sell-off hits AsiaPedestrians walk past signage outside the building where the SoftBank Group headquarters is located in Tokyo on Nov. 11, 2025. Kazuhiro Nogi | AFP | Getty ImagesShares in Japan's SoftBank Group closed 14.48% lower on Friday, their worst day since August 2024, as a broader sell-off swept through Asian technology stocks.The decline highlights investor concerns over SoftBank's aggressive bets on artificial intelligence, particularly its exposure to OpenAI, as markets question whether massive investments in AI infrastructure will generate sufficient returns.The group is one of the biggest backers of OpenAI, Anthropic's rival. Read more.— Jenny LeeAsia-Pacific markets ended sharply lower as tech sell-off sweeps the regionAsia-Pacific markets ended sharply lower on Friday as a sweeping sell-off in technology stocks dragged major indexes across the region into negative territory. Japan's Nikkei 225 plunged 5.06% to close at 56,436.65, while the Topix dropped 3.88% to 3,674.22.South Korea's Kospi sank 5.21% to close at 4,869.96, while the small-cap Kosdaq shed 5.71% to 645.68.Australia's S&P/ASX 200 closed at 8,592.6.Mainland China's CSI 300 slipped 0.76% to 3,954.57, while Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index was down 2.24% in its final hour of trading.— Jenny LeeUK retail sales beat forecastsShoppers walk past the window display for the United Colors of Benetton store on Oxford Street where a 70% clearance sale is on, on 13th October 2026, in London, England. Richard Baker | In Pictures | Getty ImagesBritish retail sales volumes rose by 0.7% in October, the country's Office for National Statistics said Friday, continuing a choppy trend following a 0.6% decline in September and 0.8% gain in August. The monthly figure was better than the flat reading forecast in a Reuters poll. Retail activity was boosted over the summer by the soccer World Cup, while analysts expected weakening consumer sentiment and higher U.K. inflation to weigh on activity.Traders continue to expect the Bank of England to hike interest rates by 25 basis points in December, though the odds of a hike have fallen to around 80% from 90% a week ago, according to money market pricing on LSEG.— Jenni Reid Gold hits more than 1-year low as sell-off deepensFILE PHOTO: 24 karat gold bars are seen at the United States West Point Mint facility in West Point, New York June 5, 2013.Shannon Stapleton | ReutersSpot gold fell 2.81% to $3,520.05 per ounce on Friday, putting the precious metal on track for its worst weekly decline in eight months, according to Reuters.Bullion, which hit its lowest since September 2025, has lost about 8.9% this week, according to LSEG data, pressured by a stronger U.S. dollar and growing expectations that the Federal Reserve will raise interest rates in December. Gold futures declined 4.55% to $3,519.20, a fresh 52-week low.  — Jenny Lee Taiwan stocks head for worst weekly drop in nearly three decadesTaiwan's benchmark Taiex fell 5.65% on Friday, bringing its weekly losses to 14.89%, its steepest weekly decline since October 1997, according to LSEG data.Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Co., the world's largest contract chipmaker and a key Nvidia supplier, dropped 4.52%. Other major Taiwanese technology stocks also fell, with Foxconn losing 4.08% and Wistron declining 4.49%.The broader Asia tech stocks sold off on Friday, following declines in U.S. peers overnight.— Jenny LeeSamsung Electronics names new memory chip, mobile business chiefs in leadership reshuffleSamsung Electronics announced a leadership reshuffle Friday, naming new heads for its semiconductor and mobile businesses.Sang Joon Hwang was promoted to president and named head of the memory business, while Sangyeun Cho was appointed president and will lead System LSI, which develops semiconductor chips including mobile processors and image sensors.Jong Myeong Lee was promoted to president and named chief technology officer of the device solutions division, the company said.Samsung shares fell 6% amid a wider rout in Asian tech names, following a sell-off in U.S. AI stocks.— Jenny LeeAlibaba shares rise 2% after robust earnings, bucking broader Asia market sell-offShares of Chinese tech giant Alibaba rose 2.42%, bucking a broader sell-off in Asian markets, following robust earnings.The gains followed Alibaba's quarterly earnings report on Thursday, which showed operating income surging 443% from a year earlier to 29.12 billion yuan ($4.34 billion). Net income more than tripled to 64.42 billion yuan, helped by investment gains and improved operating performance.Alibaba's gain stood out against a broader regional sell-off, with Japanese and South Korean technology shares sharply following overnight losses on Wall Street. Hong Kong's benchmark Hang Seng Index also dropped 2%.— Jenny LeeSoftBank shares plunge over 16% as Asian chip stocks tumble after Anthropic IPOTOKYO, JAPAN - FEBRUARY 03: SoftBank Group CEO Masayoshi Son delivers a speech during an event titled \"Transforming Business through AI\" in Tokyo, Japan, on February 03, 2025. SoftBank and OpenAI announced that they have agreed a partnership to set up a joint venture for artificial intelligence services in Japan.Tomohiro Ohsumi | Getty Images News | Getty ImagesShares of Japan's SoftBank Group plunged 16.16% on Friday, leading a broader decline in Asian technology stocks following Anthropic's much-anticipated Wall Street debut.SoftBank's substantial investment in Anthropic rival OpenAI has left it particularly exposed to shifts in AI investor sentiment.Japanese chip stocks also fell sharply, with Tokyo Electron down 7.19%, Lasertec losing 6.75% and Renesas Electronics dropping 6.71%. The sell-off extended to South Korea, where SK Hynix and Samsung Electronics declined 6% and 4.7%, respectively. The decline in Asian tech names follows a slide overnight in U.S. stocks, with the Nasdaq Composite dropping more than 2%. — Jenny LeeJapan's Nikkei plunges over 4% as inflation data raises rate hike expectations, tech stocks slideJapan's Nikkei 225 plunged 4.5% after data released Friday showed core inflation jumping to the highest this year at 2.2%, raising expectations of another rate hike in December.The probability of a hike next month now stands at 72%, according to LSEG data.The index was pressured by sharp declines in tech and related names, with SoftBank Group dropping 16%, chip-testing equipment maker Advantest plunging over 9% , while Tokyo Electron slid 7%.— Vinay DwivediJapan's core inflation in October hits 2026 high of 2.2% as energy prices biteCore inflation in Japan soared to a fresh high in 2026, coming in at 2.2% in October as higher energy costs and their knock-on effects pushed up prices in the wider economy. Core inflation, which strips out prices of food, was lower than the 2.3% expected by Reuters-polled economists, but was higher than the 2.1% in prior month.Energy subsidies for households expired in September, exposing consumers to higher prices brought about by the Iran war. Energy prices climbed 2.2% in October, a sharp spike from the 0.6% decline in September. Gas prices jumped 5.8% compared to the 2.4% rise the month before. — Lim Hui JieSouth Korea's Kospi plunges nearly 4% as Asia markets slide at openAsia-Pacific markets opened lower Friday, with South Korea's Kospi leading losses in the region.Kospi fell 3.91% with tech heavy weights SK Hynix and Samsung Electronics down 6% and 4% respectively, and the small-cap Kosdaq declined 3.54% in early trading. Japan's Nikkei 225 fell 1.46%, while the Topix was down 1.29%. Australia's S&P/ASX 200 declined 1.30%.— Jenny LeeAsia-Pacific markets set to open lower after Wall Street slides Asia-Pacific markets were set to open lower on Friday, tracking losses on Wall Street as Anthropic's highly anticipated stock market debut failed to boost investor sentiment around the broader AI names.Futures for Japan's Nikkei 225 in Chicago were at 57,890 while in Osaka they were last at 57,880, compared with the index's previous close of 59,444.21.Futures for Hong Kong's Hang Seng index last traded at 22,759 compared with the index's last close of 22,841.62.Australia's S&P/ASX 200 was down 1.02% in early trade.— Jenny Lee Thu, Nov 19 20266:25 PM ESTNvidia shares inch lower after California AG issues the company a subpoenaVcg | Visual China Group | Getty ImagesCalifornia attorney general Rob Bonta sent an investigative subpoena to Nvidia, looking into whether the company's advanced chips may have gone to China.\"My office is investigating whether Nvidia or [former CEO] Jensen Huang's actions resulted in China obtaining advanced chips or confidential information – and if Nvidia misled investors and the public about this risk, in violation of the law,\" Bonta said in a statement.Shares were last down 0.2% in extended trading. Earlier in the day, Nvidia slid to a new 52-week low.Read more from CNBC's Dan Mangan on the subpoena against Nvidia here.— Darla MercadoThu, Nov 19 20266:14 PM ESTWhy stocks sold off during Anthropic's record IPOAnthropic PBC signage ahead of the company's initial public offering (IPO) at the Nasdaq MarketSite in New York, US, on Thursday, Nov. 19, 2026. Michael Nagle | Bloomberg | Getty ImagesThe reason for the juxtaposition between Anthropic's strong IPO showing versus the market's sell-off Thursday is investor worries over two headwinds, according to Rebecca Walser, chief investment officer, Walser Wealth Management: The potential of slowing down with the Democratic wave in the recent midterm elections, combined with the scandal brought on by Jensen Huang stepping away as Nvidia CEO. \"If we are systemically facing headwinds that are going to slow down the AI data center development, they're going to be quite a bit of unwinding in valuations,\" Walser explained, adding that it will take a while for that uncertainty to \"reverberate through all of the companies that have had positive upside because of the big AI capex.\"Still, Anthropic's record IPO is \"a really big tell that the future of AI is secure, but we are facing headwinds, and we are going to have the volatility of the pullback from those headwinds,\" Walser added. — Paulina LikosThu, Nov 19 20266:06 PM ESTStock futures open flat",
