      "id": "bbc-2026-11-20-burnham-rejects-calls-for-devolution-of-powers-in-letter-to-",
      "title": "Burnham rejects calls for devolution of powers in letter to Welsh FM",
      "summary": "The prime minister says he is \"not persuaded\" by the argument that devolution would benefit Wales.",
      "body": "The prime minister has rejected long-standing calls from the first minister of Wales for certain powers to be transferred from Westminster to Cardiff.In a letter to Rhun ap Iorwerth, Andy Burnham said he was \"not persuaded\" by the case to devolve responsibilities over policing and justice, the Crown Estate and rail infrastructure to Wales.The first minister accused the prime minister of \"turning his back on devolution\" and not viewing Wales as an \"equal nation\".Burnham's position is also at odds with Welsh Labour leader Ken Skates who reiterated his position at a party conference last weekend that policing and the Crown Estate should be devolved.The letter, seen by BBC Wales, follows what had been seen as positive talks between the prime minister and first minister in Glasgow last month.Asked about the prime minister's position on devolving policing following that meeting, ap Iorwerth said: \"I think instinctively the prime minister wants to go there.\"Burnham had also previously said he was \"open\" to the idea.However, in his letter on Friday, Burnham said he was \"not persuaded\" by the case.\"The integrated England and Wales policing system is essential to maintaining interoperability, enabling seamless cross-border working and ensuring forces in Wales can draw on national and specialist capabilities,\" Burnham said.\"Retaining that integrated framework helps ensure policing in Wales can respond effectively to threats and to protecting the public,\" he added.On justice, Burnham said he \"strongly believes\" that the current devolution settlement enables Wales to \"benefit from the resources, expertise and resilience\" of a shared justice system.\"Effective partnership working is therefore essential to improving outcomes for victims, reducing reoffending and building safer communities.\"The Crown Estate includes the seabed off the Welsh coast.The land belongs to the reigning monarch, and the revenue from its property portfolio goes directly to the UK Treasury.The previous Welsh Labour government as well as the current Plaid Cymru administration have long called for it to be devolved.The prime minister told members of his party last week that Welsh Labour may have lost sight of some of the principles of devolution before it was thrown out of power in a historic election defeat.However, in his letter Burnham said the Crown Estate's \"current model provides the best means of maximising the economic and social benefits for Wales\".He went on to say the UK government is \"not persuaded by the case for devolving the management of the Crown Estate in Wales, nor to prepare for a future transfer\".Finally, on rail infrastructure, Burnham told the first minister: \"I believe funding and delivery of infrastructure is best done by the new Great British Railways, in collaboration with Transport for Wales and Welsh government.\"I am therefore not persuaded by the case for full devolution of rail infrastructure, as it would fragment the current system and affect the service provided to the public by the highly integrated cross border network.\"A spokesperson for the first minister accused the prime minister of \"turning his back on devolution\".\"His warm words on devolution are now ringing hollow and there is no longer any doubt that when he talks of driving power out of Whitehall, he only means to the regions of England,\" they said.The spokesperson added that Burnham's \"refusal to even explore the devolution of the Crown Estate, justice and policing, and rail infrastructure proves that the prime minister does not view Wales as an equal nation.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T19:41:30.000Z",
