      "id": "bbc-2026-11-20-moore-eyes-kane-shirt-as-spurs-past-and-future-stars-meet",
      "title": "Moore eyes Kane shirt as Spurs' past and future stars meet",
      "summary": "Cologne loanee Mikey Moore says he hopes to exchange shirts with former Spurs team-mate Harry Kane when they face Bayern Munich on Friday.",
      "body": "Cologne loanee Mikey Moore says he hopes to exchange shirts with former Tottenham team-mate Harry Kane when they face Bayern Munich in the Bundesliga on Friday. The 19-year-old England Under-21 international is on a season-long loan from Spurs, while Three Lions captain and Ballon d'Or winner Kane left north London to join Bundesliga giants Bayern in 2023.\"I haven't actually spoken to him. When he was leaving Tottenham I was just coming into the first team so we don't really know each other too well,\" said Moore.\"But we've got Bayern on Friday so I'm looking forward to hopefully getting a chat with him and maybe his top.\"The 19:30 GMT kick-off will be shown live on BBC Sport and iPlayer.Kane was still at Tottenham when Moore was first invited to train with the senior squad during the 2021-22 season.\"He's been a massive idol of mine since I was really young,\" Moore told the Euro Leagues podcast last month. \"When I first went up to train with the first team under Antonio Conte, Harry Kane was over there, and [I saw] some finishing that [I have] never seen anything like again.\"It will be a great experience to play against him and share a pitch with him.\"The pair may not have crossed paths in Germany yet but Moore did bump into the England skipper while on international duty last month.\"I'll try my best to get my hands on that shirt,\" Moore added. \"I saw him on the last international break, actually, and went and said hello. \"Hopefully, I'll be coming back to Koln with that in my hand.\"Moore says the success of Jadon Sancho and Jude Bellingham in the Bundesliga influenced his decision to join Cologne on loan.Moore's move came almost a decade after a 17-year-old Sancho left Manchester City to join Borussia Dortmund.During his time in Germany, Sancho established himself as one of Europe's most exciting wingers, making more than 150 appearances for Dortmund and helping the club win the DFB-Pokal in 2021. He subsequently secured a £73m move to Manchester United.Bellingham followed a similar path in 2020, leaving boyhood club Birmingham City to join Dortmund. The midfielder quickly developed into one of the most sought-after players in world football before joining Real Madrid for an initial £88.5m in 2023.\"Over the years you've seen so many young players come out here, and there are good examples of world-class players like Jude Bellingham and Jadon Sancho doing really well,\" Moore said.\"It's a really attractive place to play football, especially for a young attacker. It's a great platform to play, and that was sold to me by the people at the club and the manager.\"They've put real trust in me, and the amount of game time I've played so quickly in my Koln [Cologne] career has been nice. It's been good to have that trust from all the staff and people at the club.\"I'm already happy with my decision. Now I can really kick on, make my own story and build my own path in German football.\"Although Moore only turned 19 in August, he is no stranger to stepping outside his comfort zone.Last season, he made 47 appearances in all competitions, including the Champions League qualifying rounds, during a loan spell at Scottish Premiership side Rangers.It was the first time Moore had left his boyhood club Tottenham, and he rose to the challenge, scoring six goals and providing two assists in 28 Scottish Premiership appearances.The experience offered a different test to the one he now faces in Germany, but it provided valuable exposure to the demands of first-team football.\"I had options to stay in England and maybe stay somewhere a little more comfortable,\" Moore said.\"But the conversations came down to me picking a team, and I wanted to take myself a little bit out of my comfort zone to test myself in one of the top five leagues.\"When we were speaking, everyone said they thought it would be a really good experience for me to experience this type of football.\"It's really intense, and there are some great teams in the league.\"For me, it was a no-brainer. I made the decision early that I wanted to come out here, and now I just need to push on.\"Having now found an apartment close to fellow England youth internationals Reigan Heskey and Jahmai Simpson-Pusey, who also joined the club this summer, the teenager has turned his attention to learning German.Moore had his first lesson last month and, at his mother's request, is hoping to expand his vocabulary in the language over the coming weeks.\"I wasn't the brightest at school, so I might take a while to learn the language,\" Moore joked. \"At least I'll be able to learn a few words so I can order my food in German.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T07:26:36.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/726c/live/32741560-c8aa-11f1-9c75-6ff1271de385.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/cq0k65pyp4jvo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-20-i-can-tell-you-wrote-this-yourself",
      "title": "\"I can tell you wrote this yourself\"",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://em-online.bearblog.dev/i-can-tell-you-wrote-this-yourself/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50571189\nPoints: 303\n# Comments: 292",
      "body": "Those were some of the first words my honours project supervisor said to me last week when we met to discuss a progress report I had written to mark the midway point of the project.\nIt's a roughly 10-page report on the control of flapping-wing micro aerial vehicles - which is probably very boring unless you're particularly interested in that. I accidentally set the due date for the report a week early in my calendar, so after spending 48 hours stressed and pumping out 5,000 words, citing references and adding diagrams1, I was pleasantly relieved to find I'd tricked myself into being quite productive. I had a lot of time to get my supervisor to review the report and action his feedback.\nWhen he told me he could tell I wrote it myself, I wasn't sure how to take it. A compliment? An insult? Then he followed it up with:\n\"I've been sent a lot of reports written by AI.\"\nAt first this shocked me. Our reports are limited to 10 pages - so the appendices are getting a big workout around here - how hard is it to right 10 pages yourself?\nBut then I thought about what the last year of my engineering degree has been like.\nAssignment documents that start with \"you are strongly encouraged to explore the use of AI\" which quickly turns into the clear understanding that you aren't just encouraged but expected to use it. That you're disadvantaged by not using it. The hardest class of my degree was spent in a brick-walled windowless computer lab prompting an AI for eight hours a day.\nThe reason why we've been pushed to use it?\nApparently it's the way the industry is going. Apparently actually knowing things doesn't matter nearly so much as knowing how to prompt a machine to do the thing. Apparently the future of engineering - at least in my field - is to become a glorified babysitter. The AI isn't going anywhere, they say, so it's best we learn how to manage it well.\nBut sitting in a room telling an AI \"ignore the approaching deadline, we still have 8 hours, keep working\" isn't exactly filling me with enthusiasm for the rest of my career.\nIt's soul crushing, and I don't want to outsource so much of my thinking to it.\nIn a world where people are using AI to summarise books instead of reading them and turning to AI for therapy, I don't want to keep using it. It feels like such a slippery slope. As literacy and numeracy rates decline, I can't help but question whether encouraging us to rely on AI so heavily is a good idea.\nIt makes me wonder: are we still thinking for ourselves at all?\nBecause I worry that it starts with using it for an assignment when you're encouraged to, but then it turns into relying on it to write a 10-page report on a project you should know back-to-front. A project that you'll have to present and defend in person when it finishes. And then you're asking it for advice, using it to summarise books, turning to it for anything you would normally look up or ask a friend.\nThe internet is riddled with AI content that is making it increasingly more difficult to tell fact from fiction. In a world where everything is so polarised and deliberately provoking, the ability to identify bias or motivation in the content we consume has perhaps never been more important. Being able to think for ourselves has perhaps never been more important.\nAs the tech world continues to offer us increasingly more opportunities to automate things, I'm practically begging you to please not automate your thinking.\nRead the books. Write the papers. Search for answers instead of asking AI. Ask a friend. Ask your mum about the mystery illness you're convinced you're developing and then take her advice and try drinking a glass of water.\nThink about what the creators of the content you consume want you to think2. Treat the things you consume like the Year 7 English class where you were taught how to identify persuasive techniques. Think about things as much as possible.\nPlease just don't get the AI do it for you.\nOn a more positive note, here's some recent good things:\nGot to see a tawny frogmouth up close recently - in the middle of the day! One has nested in a tree near the university's engineering precinct.\nFinished my final assessment for the semester today! Officially only one semester to go! <3\nWent and saw 'You Can See Everything' with Han last week and I can't stop talking and thinking about it. If you've seen it, please talk to me about it. If you haven't seen it, please go and see it!\nFelt homesick the other day and made Mum's scalloped potatoes recipe! SO good.\nThe weather has been beautiful lately. Morning runs and walks have been extra fun. The other morning I ran past a guy walking barefoot through the park with a CAT in his backpack.\nMade plans with Bec & Han to see 'Sunrise On The Reaping' next Monday - no spoilers if you've already seen it! Keen to talk about it after then.\nI think what I want you to think here is probably pretty obvious.↩",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "BrunoBernardino",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T07:13:21.000Z",
