      "id": "bbc-2026-11-20-next-round-of-bbc-cuts-will-be-more-noticeable-to-audiences-",
      "title": "Next round of BBC cuts will be more noticeable to audiences, report warns",
      "summary": "The BBC has already cut jobs, programmes and services but has further to go to reach its £500m target.",
      "body": "Further job losses and programme cuts at the BBC will be more noticeable to audiences than previous savings measures, a new report has warned. The corporation announced plans earlier this year to cut about 1,800 roles and close some programmes and services, but it has further savings to make to reach its £500m target.In its report, the National Audit Office (NAO) anticipated the BBC's next round of cost-cutting measures would be more difficult to deliver and more perceptible to audiences.The BBC welcomed the report, saying it had \"consistently argued that the current funding model is not sustainable and needs reform\".The NAO report, external also said the BBC's current measures were expected to reduce the BBC's workforce by 11% over three years.Gareth Davies, head of the NAO, said: \"As the BBC approaches charter renewal in just over a year's time, there is no doubt that there are difficult choices ahead.\"The BBC's finances have declined in recent years as a result of falling licence fee income and rising production costs.The organisation has so far achieved savings by reducing staffing levels, rationalising its estate, modernising technology, simplifying processes and changing the mix and scope of content and services, the public spending watchdog said.The NAO noted the BBC's licence fee income had fallen by 26% in real terms over the last decade, in part because of the significant changes in audience habits and successive government freezes in the licence fee.The proportion of UK adults using BBC television, iPlayer, radio or online services on an average week also fell from 90% in 2021-22 to 81% in 2025-26, the NAO said.The report noted the corporation's public service broadcasting operating deficit had reached £209m in the last financial year. The BBC has already announced plans to reduce spending by about 10%, including cuts to programming across its news, nations and content divisions.Celebrity Mastermind, Blankety Blank, Live At The Apollo, Football Focus, Ski Sunday and RuPaul's Drag Race UK are among the TV programmes to have been cut so far. Radio programmes are also being dropped from the schedules, such as BBC Radio 4's You and Yours, and The World Tonight. The corporation has also announced plans to close the linear broadcast services of BBC Radio 4 Extra, BBC Three and BBC Scotland, with the latter two becoming online-only services.The NAO said the BBC expected to face difficult choices about the scale and scope of its services as part of its latest savings programme, with further content reductions expected to have a greater impact on audiences.It warned the corporation was likely to find it harder to identify further savings following its previous cost-cutting programmes.The NAO also found the broadcaster had not properly monitored the cumulative impact of its previous savings on audiences, service quality and organisational capability.It also raised concerns about the BBC's risk management and governance arrangements, and said stronger oversight would be needed to deliver the more demanding savings programme.In response to the report, the BBC said it welcomed the findings, adding that it had had to \"make tough decisions due to sustained financial pressures driven by declining licence fee income, changing audience behaviour, increased competition and rising costs\".Berangere Michel, the BBC's group chief financial officer, said: \"We welcome this report and are pleased that the NAO has acknowledged the significant savings already delivered by the BBC, with the programme on track to meet its target one year ahead of schedule.\"The BBC must respond to significant cost pressures while reinventing for a changing world. This continues to require tough decisions, while ensuring that the impacts on audiences and colleagues are minimised.\"We accept the NAO's recommendations and have already begun implementing them to strengthen the delivery of our current savings programme, while recognising there are further opportunities to build on this progress.\"She added: \"We will continue to engage with government to secure a new Charter that provides sufficient, sustainable and universal funding so the BBC can deliver for audiences for generations to come.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
