    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-20-fish-and-eggs-al-desko-the-dos-and-don-ts-of-lunch-at-work",
      "title": "Fish and eggs al desko? The dos and don'ts of lunch at work",
      "summary": "Figure caption, Do you take a full lunch break?There are two types of office workers: those who eat lunch al-desko, and those who have to endure the smell of everyone else's. Few things are more likely to test workplace relationships than the person sitting next to you proudly unveiling a container of reheated fish.From tuna sandwiches and boiled eggs to crumbly and sticky trea…",
      "body": "Figure caption, Do you take a full lunch break?There are two types of office workers: those who eat lunch al-desko, and those who have to endure the smell of everyone else's. Few things are more likely to test workplace relationships than the person sitting next to you proudly unveiling a container of reheated fish.From tuna sandwiches and boiled eggs to crumbly and sticky treats that leave a mess on the keyboard, what is considered OK to eat in the office can be surprisingly divisive.\"We all know not to bring in a microwave fish pie, that is the law,\" says civil servant Sarah. She admits she has, in fact, committed the offence herself in a previous workplace and jokes \"that's the reason I left\".'Suss out the smelliness'Casey, a content creator for an energy company, says he used to eat tuna at his desk but has since \"reformed\".He says you have to \"suss out the smelliness\" and then decide if it's acceptable to eat at the desk.Image caption, Casey and Manny often eat at their desk and their favourite pretzel snack is causing some problems given how sticky and crumbly it isBut fish isn't the only food causing office friction and Casey says his regular snack of cinnamon pretzels has been causing \"quite a bit of havoc\".\"The sugar's going everywhere and it's getting in my laptop keys,\" he admits. No-one in the office has commented on it yet but he says he's hiding the crumbs by \"shuffling them off the desk\".Boiled eggs also split opinion. Laura, who works in finance, says she's been teased by colleagues for eating them.\"In my defence, it was high protein, it was cost-effective and healthy – I swear by a good boiled egg.\"She thinks that no food is off limits at the desk. \"Coming from an Asian background where you'd eat fish for breakfast I am very open to it being eaten.\"Image source, Barbara LeathamImage caption, Ashlea Fisher says it's important all of her employees are mindful about the food they are eating at their desk Ashlea Fisher, who runs her own recruitment company, has tackled the issue of smelly food in the office head on when she asked a member of staff to stop bringing in fish after \"the smell was so bad that a pregnant colleague was feeling physically sick\".\"I'm a big believer in being able to have an honest conversation with your team,\" she says, and suggests that other people in her position should approach a conversation lightly and be conscious of not embarrassing colleagues.\"Nobody should feel judged for what they eat, and we should respect different cultures and dietary requirements, but being mindful of the people around you is simply good workplace etiquette.\"Do you take a lunch break?While what we eat in the office at lunch can cause tension, there's also the question of whether we're even taking the time to enjoy our lunch in the first place.In a world of back-to-back meetings and overflowing inboxes, it's easy to skip our break and shovel down a sandwich with one hand while replying to emails with the other.A 2025 report by Just Eat found that the youngest workers, Gen Z, were the most likely to take a full break, with 56% doing so.However, the next generation up, millennials were more likely than any other to eat lunch at their desk.\"While it can be convenient to eat at your desk, it's not best practice to remain glued to the screen all day,\" says Alan Price, an employment law and HR expert, and CEO of BrightHR.\"Taking a break isn't just an employment right, there are proven benefits for both physical and mental health.\"Image caption, Finance worker Laura takes a lunch and dinner break when she's working long hours Laura, whose working day can occasionally finish at 2am, says stepping away from her desk is a non-negotiable and she makes a point of taking an hour for lunch, describing it as \"essential for maintaining my sanity\".\"I take my lunch break as much as possible, for the sake of my eyes and mental wellbeing,\" she says.But others are less precious about it. Content creator Manny says he has lunch at his desk \"most of the time\".His work is task-based and his company operates a policy of \"do the work when you want to do it\".\"Usually you just take 20 minutes to eat something, but on another day you might take an hour. You just kind of feel it out,\" he says.Get in touchDo you have comments or questions about this story?More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
