      "id": "theverge-2026-11-19-apple-is-hiring-engineers-to-build-its-own-ai-agent",
      "title": "Apple is hiring engineers to build its own AI agent",
      "summary": "Apple is working on its own AI agent that can perform a range of tasks for you, including browsing the web, working across apps, writing code, and calling services. That's according to a job listing spotted by Bloomberg, which says Apple is looking for an engineer to lead the creation of an agentic AI assistant.\nAn AI agent from Apple would put the iPhone maker in the running to compete with the l",
      "body": "Apple is working on its own AI agent that can perform a range of tasks for you, including browsing the web, working across apps, writing code, and calling services. That’s according to a job listing spotted by Bloomberg, which says Apple is looking for an engineer to lead the creation of an agentic AI assistant.\nAn AI agent from Apple would put the iPhone maker in the running to compete with the likes of Meta’s Muse, OpenAI’s Dots, Anthropic’s Duet, and Google’s Tasks. Muse, ChatGPT, and Perplexity can already take control of a computer through their Mac apps, while Google just started letting Gemini do the same.\nApple began rolling out its AI-upgraded Siri in September, which you can use to ask questions about what’s on your screen, as well as perform actions across apps. It looks like Apple is planning to give Siri more agentic capabilities, as a report from Bloomberg suggests the company could add the ability to rotate and crop images in Photos, or organize Safari tabs. This lines up with the company’s new job listing, which says the engineer will work closely with teams across its Siri, UI Frameworks, and Apple Foundation Models divisions.\n“People already do their most valuable work by moving fluidly between tools: a browser, a document, a terminal, an editor, a service,” the job listing says. “We’re building AI agents that can do the same… These agents plan and carry out multi-step tasks across apps, browsers, command lines, code, and APIs, and they can coordinate specialized sub-agents along the way.”\nWith its upcoming iOS 27.3 update, Apple is also giving developers the ability to integrate Siri AI into “all apps.” This is rolling out as part of a Developer Preview and will eventually allow Siri to perform tasks across a wider range of apps.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "AI",
        "Apple",
        "Apple Rumors",
        "News",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Emma Roth",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-19T22:31:41.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/DSC04239.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.628272251309,100,78.743455497382",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/1038227/apple-ai-agent-engineer-job-listing",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-19-eu-uk-talks-going-down-to-the-wire-says-no-10",
      "title": "EU-UK talks going down to the wire, says No 10",
      "summary": "The EU and UK are expected to reach new deals on food trade and visas for young people at a summit on Friday.",
      "body": "Negotiations between the EU and UK are \"going down to the wire\" ahead of Friday's summit in Brussels, Downing Street has said. The summit is aimed at reaching new agreements between the two sides on food trade rules, visas for young people and carbon emissions pricing. Sources have told the BBC the UK is set to agree to a quota of 100,000 young people per year coming to the UK as part of a new youth mobility scheme. Downing Street has not commented on the details, which would also allow young Britons to live in Europe.Conservative leader Kemi Badenoch accused Prime Minister Andy Burnham of \"reopening the divisions and debates\" of the Brexit referendum. Speaking to the BBC's Today programme, she said she wanted to see easier trading with the EU, but added \"not trade at any cost\". She also said the new deals could see UK companies having to follow EU rules, even those that do not trade with the bloc. Speaking to broadcasters on Thursday evening, Burnham was asked if there were any circumstances in which the UK would walk away from the deal on Friday.\"There would always be those circumstances because I'm in this to get the best deal for Britain, always,\" he said.\"But what's in it for us is the possibility of lower food prices and I can't turn my back on that, lower costs for business, cutting red tape, and a really big win potentially of more collaboration on immigration - a Channel action plan - which is a really big deal if we can land that tomorrow.\"So I hope people can understand why I have pushed this because it could be a really good deal for Britain.\"The prime minister's spokesman has said if a deal is secured, the government will introduce legislation to Parliament next week to implement changes. A new youth mobility scheme would aim to make it easier for young people to work and travel both in the EU and UK. The UK already has schemes in place with 13 non-EU countries including Australia, Canada and India. In 2024, the EU proposed allowing 18 to 30 year-olds to work or study in the EU and UK for up to four years.Labour, which was then in opposition, rejected the deal arguing it amounted to reinstating freedom of movement. Since coming to government, the party has suggested it would consider a more restricted scheme with a time limit, a cap on the numbers coming to the UK and a ban on participants from claiming benefits.The UK's request to limit numbers has proved to be a sticking point in the talks, along with the EU's request that EU students should pay the same tuition fees at UK universities as domestic students. An agreement on trade could also remove checks and paperwork faced by food companies exporting to the EU.The government argues this could add £5bn to the economy and cut food prices.However, it could mean the UK will have to follow certain EU rules on products, for example on gene-edited crops or labelling. On immigration, Burnham has said the UK and EU could link up their law enforcement efforts, including sharing more data and criminal records of foreign nationals.The prime minister has said the summit would allow the UK to raise concerns about the Made in Europe scheme, which is designed to protect industries from unfair Chinese competition.Burnham has previously suggested he could commit Labour to building a closer long-term future relationship with Europe, including asking to rejoin the organisation as a full member - however, he has ruled out major changes before the next election.The party's 2024 manifesto promised not to return to the EU, the single market, customs union or freedom of movement, which allows EU citizens to live and work elsewhere in the bloc without a visa.Earlier this week, former deputy prime minister David Lammy urged the prime minister to go into the next general election promising to rejoin the EU. However, the subject has divided his cabinet with one minister warning of the costs that would come with reversing Brexit. Reform UK leader Nigel Farage, speaking to The Sun, external, said he does \"not believe for a minute\" the country would \"ever vote to rejoin\" the EU. He added: \"I think what Burnham is doing is playing a very cynical political game. He's trying to pick up votes from the Lib Dems, pick up votes from the Greens, and using it as a massive distraction from all the problems that he can't deal with.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-19T22:26:04.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/cbfc/live/34a43100-e423-11f1-9e08-55a8a3b48445.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c623j4kpg94go?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-19-woman-drugged-and-advertised-for-sex-by-husband-speaks-out-a",
      "title": "Woman drugged and advertised for sex by husband speaks out as abusers found guilty",
      "summary": "Joanne Young said the actions of her husband over 13 years had \"cost me my life as I knew it.\"",
      "body": "A woman who was drugged, raped and advertised for sex online by her husband has said she will \"never feel completely safe\" after four men were found guilty of sexually abusing her, in the first trial of its kind in England.Joanne Young, who has waived her right to anonymity, said: \"The person I trusted most in the world betrayed me in the most horrific way.\"Philip Young, 50, had already pleaded guilty to multiple charges of drugging and raping his wife over a 13-year period at their home in Swindon, Wiltshire. He invited men via websites and apps such as Craigslist, Grindr and Fab Swingers to the couple's home without her knowledge.On Thursday, Norman Macksoni, 48, Richard Wilkins, 62, Connor Sanderson-Doyle, 32, and Mohammed Hassan, 38, were found guilty of multiple sexual offences by a jury at Winchester Crown Court. Wilkins and Macksoni were each found guilty of one count of rape.Joanne Young, who divorced Philip Young last year, said the abuse had \"cost me my life as I knew it.\"On the steps outside the court, she said: \"What happened to me was not the act of one man alone, others were invited to take part and they chose to.\"James Foster, specialist Crown Prosecution Service (CPS) prosecutor, said: \"This is the first time in the UK that a jury have convicted or been asked to consider a case of organised drug-facilitated sexual abuse, which has been organised by the husband.\"The crimes of the former Tory councillor emerged after the highly publicised case in France of Gisèle Pelicot, whose husband, Dominique Pelicot, drugged and raped her and invited 50 other men to abuse her. Gisèle Pelicot also waived her right to anonymity in that trial.Her husband was jailed for 20 years in France in 2024 and all the defendants were found guilty of at least one charge.If you have been affected by any of the issues raised in this story, information and support can be found at the BBC's Action Line.Police and prosecutors building the case against Philip Young sought the expertise of the French investigators to assist them in bringing him to justice.Detectives discovered that Philip Young, whose most recent address was in Enfield, north London, had taken 480 intimate images and videos of his wife on 200 occasions and posted images online on at least 500 occasions – with the vast majority of these taken at the family home in Swindon.The trial of the four defendants heard the investigation into Philip Young had begun after police were alerted to the fact he had uploaded extreme pornographic images to the internet in 2023.Wiltshire Police said it was still looking for another man wanted in connection with alleged sex offences against Joanne Young.They described him as being of \"a large build, hairy, with scarring on the back of his head, prominent lips, short or shaved hair, and a distinctive tattoo on the left side of his chest\".Wiltshire Police added the extent of drug-rape offences was unknown because victims might not know they had been targeted.A police spokesperson said: \"While cases involving organised drug-facilitated sexual assault remain relatively rare compared to other forms of sexual offending, intelligence and other recent cases would suggest this may be a significantly under-reported crime type.\"Victims are often unaware they have been drugged, may have fragmented memories of events, or may initially attribute symptoms to alcohol consumption. \"These factors make detection and reporting particularly challenging.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-19T22:17:29.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/db7d/live/acdc01e0-e467-11f1-99b6-a35a62fa4264.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/crq5n5pe935no?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-19-synology-security-advisory",
      "title": "Synology Security Advisory",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.synology.com/en-uk/security/advisory/Synology_SA_26_21\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50567209\nPoints: 69\n# Comments: 33",
      "body": "Synology has released a security update to address a vulnerability:\nCVE-2026-120382 allows remote attackers to read or write arbitrary files and conduct denial-of-service attacks.\nUpgrade to 7.4.1-90080-2 or above (scheduled for release by 2026-11-20).\nUpgrade to 7.3.2-86009-6 or above (scheduled for release by 2026-11-20).\nOnGoing (Upgrading to 7.3.2-86009-6 or above is recommended).\nOnGoing (Upgrading to 7.3.2-86009-6 or above is recommended).\nDeactivate the OAuth Service package.\nCWE-88: Improper Neutralization of Argument Delimiters in a Command\nAn improper neutralization of argument delimiters in a command vulnerability in the OAuth API in Synology DiskStation Manager (DSM) before 6.2.4-25556-9, 7.1.1-42962-10, 7.2.1-69057-14, 7.2.2-72806-11, 7.3.2-86009-6, and 7.4.1-90080-2 allows remote attackers to read or write arbitrary files and conduct denial-of-service attacks.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "choult",
