      "id": "bbc-2026-11-19-fiona-harvey-who-allegedly-inspired-baby-reindeer-to-appear-",
      "title": "Fiona Harvey, who allegedly inspired Baby Reindeer, to appear in court on harassment charge",
      "summary": "Harvey, who allegedly inspired Netflix's Baby Reindeer, racially aggravated harassment without violence.",
      "body": "A woman who allegedly inspired the stalker character Martha in the hit Netflix drama Baby Reindeer is to appear at Harrow Crown Court on charges of racially aggravated harassment without violence.Fiona Harvey, 61, is accused of sending several emails and three voicemails including antisemitic material to lawyer Mark Lewis, between 4 September and 9 October 2025.During a hearing at Highbury Corner Magistrates' Court on Thursday, the court heard that Lewis initially believed Harvey was a potential client.Harvey of Camden, north London, did not indicate a plea, but elected for a Crown Court trial.The charge is an either-way offence, which means it can be heard in either a magistrates' court or Crown Court.She will next appear at Harrow Crown Court for a pre-trial preparation hearing on 17 December. Harvey has previously identified herself as the woman on whom Martha is based, after the release of Richard Gadd's Emmy Award-winning series in 2024.She is suing the streamer £128m for defamation, negligence and privacy violations, arguing that that Netflix told \"brutal lies\" about her to over 50 million viewers around the world.Netflix denies the claim, which will be heard in front of a jury on May 18, 2027, at the US District Court for the Central District of California.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-19T13:39:56.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/5a38/live/03ae11c0-e41d-11f1-9e08-55a8a3b48445.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cq143j81g6l3o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-19-viagogo-may-have-broken-law-on-hidden-fees-watchdog-provisio",
      "title": "Viagogo may have broken law on hidden fees, watchdog provisionally finds",
      "summary": "The UK's competition body says initial findings suggest the ticket-reselling website used drip pricing.",
      "body": "Ticket-reselling site Viagogo may have broken the law on hidden fees, the UK's competition body has said in a provisional finding.The Competition and Markets Authority (CMA) said it found that Viagogo failed to show consumers the total price upfront when they were buying tickets \"and added fees later in the process, a practice known as drip pricing\".The CMA added that this was an initial finding, and it should not be assumed that the company had actually broken the law.In a statement, Viagogo said it was co-operating fully with the CMA, adding that the investigation was continuing and \"no final findings have been made\".The CMA said last year that it was investigating a number of companies over their online pricing practices, including Viagogo.The competition watchdog said it had looked into the experience of UK fans buying tickets for events such as concerts and sport on three websites that operate under the Viagogo brand.\"The CMA believes that mandatory costs such as taxes and booking fees were added at later stages of the checkout process and not included in the total price from the start,\" it said.\"Drip pricing can cause fans to be caught out by extra charges and buy tickets they might not otherwise have selected, had they been able to compare prices accurately from the start.\"The CMA said Viagogo would now have an opportunity to consider its findings and respond before a final decision is made.\"This is an ongoing investigation and no final findings have been made,\" Viagogo said in a statement.\"We have engaged constructively with the CMA and continue to cooperate fully with its investigation.\"The CMA has powers to tackle anti-competitive behaviour. It can now decide if consumer laws have been broken - rather than having to go through the courts.Drip pricing is one of the practices the CMA has been focusing on particularly.On Wednesday, it said it was investigating three companies, external over the issue, and in August it announced that the Trainline, Virgin Atlantic and RED Driving School had been engaged in the practice.In general, the CMA can impose penalties of up to 10% of global turnover for a breach of consumer law. However, it will consider a number of factors, including the severity of conduct, before deciding on the size of the penalty.In June this year, the watchdog ordered rival ticket reseller StubHub UK to refund more than 50,000 customers and pay a £900,000 fine for not showing people the total price upfront when buying tickets.StubHub UK received a 40% reduction to its financial penalty after it admitted to breaking the law and agreed to settle the case early with the CMA.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-19T13:35:52.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/3865/live/755fe980-e407-11f1-ab35-e1af93fa5cce.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmvg2ex7j30lo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
