      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-19-ruby2-hell-pre-authentication-rce-on-stock-rails",
      "title": "Ruby2ßhell: Pre-authentication RCE on stock Rails",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://verialabs.com/ruby2shell/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50556287\nPoints: 259\n# Comments: 103",
      "body": "Affected software   Ruby versions     Ruby series Affected Fixed in     4.0 4.0.0 to 4.0.9 4.0.10   3.4 3.4.0 to 3.4.12 3.4.13   3.3 3.3.0 to 3.3.13 3.3.14   3.2 and older All releases back to 2.4.0 EOL    Your deployment\nThe bug is reachable from the following frameworks in their default configuration. Other frameworks are not exposed by default, but application code that upcases strings received in a legacy encoding may also be vulnerable.     Framework Status on an affected Ruby     Rails Affected   Sinatra (classic style) Affected   Other frameworks and plain Rack Not exposed by default    Self-hosted software\nThe latest version of the following popular software bundles an affected Ruby.\nSoftware Bundled Ruby Status     GitLab 19.6.0 3.3.13 Affected   GitHub Enterprise Server 3.22.5 4.0.9 Affected   Mastodon 4.7.6 4.0.10 Fixed   OpenProject 17.9.8 4.0.10 Fixed\n2.0.20261119-0203  4.0.10 Fixed   Chatwoot 4.20.0 3.4.4 Affected\n19 November 2026 at 14:29 UTC.\nUpgrade Ruby to a fixed release:\nInstall the vendor's fixed release. See\nSelf-hosted software for fixed versions\nMost of Ruby's single-byte encodings have no capital\nß. Upcasing it writes two bytes, SS, but\nthe loop only bounds-checks one (to < to_end).\nwhile (*pp < end && to < to_end) {    /* room for one byte */    code = *(*pp)++;    if (code == SHARP_s) {        if (flags & ONIGENC_CASE_UPCASE) {            *to++ = 'S';              /* first byte */            code = 'S';        }        /* ... */    }    /* ... */    *to++ = code;                     /* second byte, unchecked */}\nThe output buffer is allocated as the input length plus 20\nbytes. Each ß adds one byte to the output, so an\ninput with 21 of them overflows the buffer by one byte.\nThe same loop is in several ISO-8859 and Windows-125x encodings\nand has been there since Ruby 2.4.0. It is reached by\nUTF-8 strings take a different code path and are not affected.\nA one-byte heap overflow. The byte written is S\n(0x53) when upcasing or s\n(0x73) when case-folding, and it lands directly\nafter the output buffer. The attacker controls the size of the\nbuffer through the input length.\nCopying the result out of the buffer then reads one byte out of\nWe'll be publishing a proof of concept in a week, once people\nhave had time to upgrade.",
