      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-18-search-browser-minimalist-webkit-browser-with-chrome-extensi",
      "title": "Search Browser – Minimalist WebKit browser with Chrome extension support",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://searchbrowser.app/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50551398\nPoints: 48\n# Comments: 30",
      "body": "Search is a browser for the Mac with nothing in the way. About ten megabytes, the engine already in macOS, and a window that opens in a third of a second. Free, for any Mac on macOS 14 or later.\nVersion 1.0.14 · 10.8 MB · Free · Intel Mac?\nIt works: pick a tab, open a new one, swipe the tabs sideways for another space.\nTwo ways to hold it. Titles across the top, or down the side. The grey slides to the tab you pick either way, and a pinned site is one letter.\nTab strip. The grey slides to the tab you pick.\nSidebar. Room for more, and titles that never give ground.\nPins. A site becomes a letter — or its icon. Either stays through force-quits.\nSpaces. Separate sets of tabs — work, home, a side project — each with its own icon. Two fingers sideways over the tabs and the next one slides in beside this one; past the last, a new one, signed in where you already are or starting afresh. Try it in the window above.\nRead, watch, and nothing else. Reading mode keeps the article and its pictures, set to be read. Leave a video's tab and it follows you in a small window, above every app.\nReading mode. ⌘⇧R. The words and their pictures; the rest of the page gone.\nFloating video. ⌘⇧P, or just leave the tab. Skip, pause, or send it back with a click.\nLight enough to forget it's there. Measured against Chrome and Safari on the same Mac, the same afternoon, the same pages. Medians of three runs; the method and every run.\n10.8 MB on disk. No engine of its own to carry.\n320 ms to the first window. Launched cold, no page open.\n51 MB before the first page. Every process the app owns.\n11 processes with five tabs open. Where Chrome runs 29.\nPinned tabs. A letter at the head of the row. ⌘W puts it to sleep; it never leaves.\nAds blocked before they load. In the network layer — no scripts, no counter.\nHide anything. ⌘⇧H, click a thing, it's gone from that site for good.\nReading mode. The article, its pictures, nothing else. ⌘⇧R.\nFloating video. Leave YouTube or Netflix and the video follows, above every app.\nPasswords in your keychain. Shown with Touch ID. Passwords, bookmarks and history come over from Chrome or Dia in a click.\nSpaces. Separate sets of tabs, each with its icon. Two fingers sideways to switch; stay signed in, or start afresh.\nChrome extensions. Straight from the Chrome Web Store, in a click. On macOS 15.4 or later.\nA sidebar that steps aside. Tabs down the side or across the top. ⌘S folds the column away; the left edge brings it back.\nPrivate tabs. ⌘⇧N. Its own cookies, no history, no place in tomorrow's session.\nWeb Inspector. Inspect Element on any page, the console at ⌥⌘J — WebKit's own tools.\nOpen source. The code is on GitHub — read it, build it yourself, or send a fix.\nA browser for the Mac by Office Commun, a design studio in Paris. Tabs, an address field, history, passwords, downloads — and as little around the page as we could manage. About 7 MB.\nNo. Search runs on WebKit, the engine that ships with macOS and powers Safari. That's why it's tiny: there's no second engine inside. Pages look exactly as they do in Safari.\nYes. No account, no subscription, and it's open source.\nmacOS 14 or later, on Apple silicon or Intel. Intel Macs have a download of their own: Search for Intel, which updates itself from its own feed.\nYes. File › Bring Things Over… brings bookmarks, history, passwords and extensions from Chrome, Arc (its spaces too), Brave, Edge, Firefox, Zen and others, or from an exported file. macOS asks once for the other browser's key, and passwords land in your keychain under Search's name. Nothing in the other browser changes. Leaving Arc? Here is how it comes over.\nBetween your Macs, yes, if you turn it on: spaces, pins, bookmarks and a few settings go through a folder you choose, in iCloud Drive or Dropbox. No account, and never your history, passwords or sign-ins.\nEvery hour Search reads a small file on officecommun.com, signed by Office Commun; Search › Check for Updates… looks right away. If there's a newer build, it downloads it in the background, checks it was signed by Office Commun, swaps it in, and it's there next time you open Search. Settings, pins, history and passwords are never touched.\nSettings › This Mac › Make it the default.\nYes: brew install --cask driceroland/tap/search. Search updates itself either way, and brew upgrade works too.\nMore questions and answers · What changed in each version\nSearch is free, for Macs on macOS 14 or later, Apple silicon or Intel. About ten megabytes, a third of a second, and nothing in the way.\nVersion 1.0.14 · 10.8 MB · Intel Mac? · The numbers · Changelog · Roadmap · Privacy",
