      "id": "bbc-2026-11-18-states-to-debate-social-security-contributions-cut",
      "title": "States to debate social security contributions cut",
      "summary": "The amendment is being brought forward by deputies Gavin St Pier and Tom Rylatt.",
      "body": "\"Social security contributions have been increased each year as part of a 10-year programme of increases,\" St Pier said.\"Without this amendment, since 2021, employee contributions will have increased by 1% and self-employed contributions will have increased by 1.6%.\"Rylatt described the amendment would give \"breathing room for working islanders\".He said: \"ESS's [Employment and Social Security] 10-year plan emphasised a deliberately flexible approach that could take account of the economic circumstances of the day, alongside the outcome of the long-debated tax reform.\"With little sign of cost-of-living pressures easing in 2027, and with tax reform now decided, we believe this coming year is the right time to use that flexibility for the benefit of islanders.\"He said the amendment for 2027 would amount to a loss of between £8m to £9m for the Guernsey Insurance Fund, but that it would be \"a one-off departure from the existing trajectory\".Follow BBC Guernsey on X, external, Facebook, external and Instagram, external. Send your story ideas to channel.islands@bbc.co.uk, external.",
