      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-17-latent-lattice-language-models",
      "title": "Latent Lattice Language Models",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://shivamduggal4.github.io/latent-lattice-language-models/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50539039\nPoints: 10\n# Comments: 3",
      "body": "Language models typically increase per-token computation through deeper or looped layers and latent reasoning steps,\nwhile richer residual connections improve information flow across layers.\nWe instead increase computational capacity: the number of active computational states a model maintains.\nWe introduce the Lattice of Latents, a set of per-layer horizontal latent streams that run\nrecurrently along time, alongside the vertical residual stream that flows through depth.\nLike hyper-connections, mHC, and attention or value residuals, the Lattice enhances the residual stream, but rather than\nadding or rerouting residual pathways through depth, it provides companion computational states that persist across tokens.\nUnlike hybrid Transformer–SSM architectures, whose recurrent states primarily serve as internal memory for sequence mixing,\nthese latents directly condition the vertical stream’s attention, KV-cache writes, MLP computation, and decoding.\nOn Transformers trained on 20B FineWeb-Edu tokens, a 406M-parameter Lattice model lowers validation perplexity from 15.41 to 14.40\nThese gains extend across architectural variants.\nThe Lattice composes with prior residual-stream enhancements: with hyper-connections and input residuals,\nit lowers validation perplexity from 15.41 (base Transformer) and 14.22 (Transformer with same enhancements) to 13.61.\nOn Gated DeltaNet, Mamba-2, Triadic GDN, and GDN–Mamba-2 hybrids, it improves perplexity at every tested native state size,\nwith Lattice-GDN & Lattice-Triadic-GDN variants outperforming pure baselines with 4× larger native states.\nThese results identify persistent per-layer state as a complementary axis for scaling language-model computation.\nVertical residual streams flow through depth; horizontal latents persist across tokens.\nSwitch between training and inference, and compare models.\nClick the figure to pause. Input injection can be switched on or off at any moment; the animation continues from the same point.\nInteractive versions of the main results. Hover over any bar or point for details.\nLanguage modeling after 20B-token pretraining\nLLLM (dmodel, dr) has a vertical residual-stream width dmodel and horizontal-latent width dr.\nLLLM + Jacobi refiner (J3)\nGFLOP/token: floating-point operations per token, excluding attention scores (which add only ≈ 0.1 GFLOP/token) and the embedding lookup.\nJ3 runs three sweeps; its sequential rollout uses a single pass at inference (click Show full table for results).\nScaling pretraining tokens: 10B → 20B → 30B\nFineWeb-Edu validation perplexity as training continues. Circle area is proportional to parameter count.\nBoth LLLMs use only 22 layers vs. 40 for the Transformer 40L, so with the same attention width (1024) they keep a 45% smaller KV cache.\nLattice of Latents vs. prior residual-stream enhancements\n400M models, 20B FineWeb-Edu tokens. Lattice models narrow the vertical width from 1024 to 932 to stay iso-parameter.\nThe Transformer and its single enhancements appear first, then the Lattice and its single enhancements, then two-enhancement combinations.\nHC: hyper-connections; mHC: manifold-constrained HC; value: value residual (ResFormer; learned weights for the single +value runs, fixed 0.5 in x0 + value); x0: nanochat-style input residual.\nA hard regime: the vertical residual stream is squeezed to 256 dimensions.\nPress play to add the horizontal-update enhancements one at a time and watch them recover most of the gap to a full-width Transformer. 10B-token pretraining.\n+ adds a component; ↻ replaces one. Parameter counts are shown under each bar.\nPure vs. Lattice recurrent models (∼405M parameters, 10B FineWeb-Edu tokens) across native-state sizes per mixer. Each Lattice model is iso-parameter with its pure counterpart.\nBars appear from highest to lowest perplexity, each at its state-size position.\nNative-state size counts recurrent-memory entries per mixer (1K = 1024 scalars), excluding the horizontal latents and convolution states. Lattice models use input injection + vstream.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "E-Reverance",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-17T22:17:18.000Z",
      "imageUrl": null,
      "originalUrl": "https://shivamduggal4.github.io/latent-lattice-language-models/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-17-quad9-s-withdrawal-from-france-a-canary-in-the-coal-mine",
      "title": "Quad9's withdrawal from France: A canary in the coal mine",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://blog.cloudflare.com/quad9-withdrawal-from-france/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50538994\nPoints: 800\n# Comments: 361",
      "body": "Last month, the Swiss non-profit foundation Quad9 announced that it will withdraw its free public DNS resolver from France. This difficult decision followed months of litigation and a French court ruling that imposed daily financial penalties on Quad9 for failing to block domains associated with alleged copyright infringement.Quad9’s departure from France is both disheartening and understandable. The court’s ruling put the organization in the impossible bind of having to choose between crippling litigation and penalties, or fundamentally altering its service in a way that would undermine the privacy and security of its users. Left with no good option, Quad9 is choosing the exit door instead.The withdrawal of a basic infrastructure service from France fundamentally harms the free and open Internet, and it should serve as a warning of the damage that will result if rightsholders and policymakers continue demanding maximal blocking across every layer of the Internet. It not only deprives French Internet users of a trustworthy and important tool; it creates a real risk of disruption to their daily lives. Quad9’s resolver is used by a wide variety of individuals and organizations in France — including government bodies, educational institutions, hospitals, and utilities — and Quad9 has no way to directly notify those individuals and institutions that their resolver will soon stop working. If the affected parties do not change their resolver before Quad9 removes its service, their Internet will fail with no explanation.Quad9 is not the first public DNS resolver to withdraw from a country following blocking orders, and it almost certainly won’t be the last. Cisco previously withdrew its OpenDNS resolver from France, Portugal, and Belgium (temporarily) under similar circumstances. And a Belgian court recently issued an order that would impose substantial penalties if Cloudflare, Google, and Cisco do not begin blocking websites through their public resolvers by early January 2027. That’s something Cloudflare has never done.Cloudflare has spent years advocating against orders of this kind. We are concerned that rightsholders’ unrelenting demands for blocking by public DNS resolvers will end up causing real harm to the Internet.A refresher on DNS, the Domain Name SystemTo understand why this is such a problem, it helps to understand how the Internet works under the hood. DNS resolvers are a foundational piece of the Internet’s infrastructure. DNS is the Internet’s address book: when you type a website name such as cloudflare.com, the DNS system translates that domain name into the IP address your device needs to connect to it. A DNS resolver is the service that looks up that address and sends it back to your device, much like an operator finding a phone number in a directory.DNS resolvers can be operated by anyone, but they are commonly run by Internet service providers (ISPs), mobile networks, businesses, and universities. If you’re reading this blog post at home and have never thought about DNS before, chances are that you’re using your ISP’s default resolver. Because you use your DNS resolver every time that you visit a website, it’s important for it to be reliable and trustworthy.And because the DNS resolver has access to all of your browsing activity, which resolver you use has real privacy implications.In addition to DNS resolvers that are provided by your local ISP, there are a variety of public DNS resolvers available. Cloudflare launched our public DNS resolver 1.1.1.1 in April 2018 to give back to the Internet. Our goal was to make a fast, reliable, privacy-first consumer DNS service available for free to everyone. We succeeded. 1.1.1.1 is among the fastest DNS resolvers in the world, including in Europe. Cloudflare's resolver doesn’t collect any user data, let alone sell it to advertisers. 1.1.1.1 has always been one of our favorite examples of Cloudflare fulfilling its mission to help build a better Internet.1.1.1.1 is one of many public resolvers that anyone can use. In addition to 1.1.1.1 and Quad9’s 9.9.9.9, some of the other best-known public resolvers include Google’s 8.8.8.8 and Cisco’s OpenDNS. Just because these services are offered for free doesn’t mean they’re easy to provide. A well-run global public DNS resolver is an extraordinary engineering feat, built to securely and privately resolve hundreds of billions or even trillions of queries a day across a globally distributed network of hundreds of server locations.As Quad9 notes, there are many other DNS resolvers available to users, many of them offered by essentially anonymous services whose practices are unknown or undocumented. It’s easy to change your DNS resolver, and people do so for a variety of reasons including performance, reliability, or to access specific security features.Why public DNS resolvers should not be required to blockRightsholders have a legitimate interest in preventing online piracy. But trying to address online infringement through public DNS resolvers is fundamentally the wrong approach.Among other things, such blocking is ineffective. Think back to the analogy of an operator finding a phone number in a directory. If a court wants to shut down an illegal business, forcing the directory operator to cross the business's name out of the phone book doesn't actually close the business. The storefront is still open, the products are still there, and anyone who already knows the address, or uses a different phone book, can walk in.The same is true for the Internet. DNS resolvers are just one link in the transmission chain. The only way a DNS resolver can “block” content is by declining to return the IP address for a particular website. But a public DNS resolver cannot remove the actual infringing content from the Internet, nor can it take down the servers hosting it.Because of this, blocking by public DNS resolvers is remarkably easy to bypass. Any Internet user who wants to reach a blocked site can circumvent the block in less than a minute simply by switching to one of the thousands of other DNS resolvers available online. In fact, users don’t even need a resolver at all to reach infringing content if they already know the underlying IP address. Unsurprisingly, while blocking via public resolvers may inject some friction for some users when accessing content, there is no evidence that it actually reduces the total amount of online infringement that occurs.In addition to being ineffective, as we’ve written previously, DNS blocking is a blunt instrument that causes collateral damage. Because DNS operates at the domain level, a resolver cannot block a specific infringing video or file; it must block the entire domain. This affects all content on that domain, whether legal or illegal. It is also entirely opaque to the user: when a resolver is forced to block a domain, the website simply fails to load. The user receives no explanation or legal notice, making it impossible to know if the site is down for maintenance, if their network is broken, or if the site was blocked by a court order.The problem is substantially worse with respect to public DNS resolvers. Because public resolvers operate globally, any blocking will naturally cross borders, meaning that a single country could dictate what content is accessible around the world. For example, while the EU-dedicated DNS resolver DNS4EU operated by Whalebone blocks in response to country-specific orders, it does so across the entire EU. Efforts to reengineer public DNS resolvers to allow for more localized blocking would fundamentally alter the service, harming performance and resilience, undermining user privacy, and fragmenting the open Internet.For these reasons, Cloudflare, Google, Cisco, and Quad9 have all opposed efforts to mandate blocking by public DNS resolvers, with some success. Several years ago, courts in Germany ruled in favor of Cloudflare and Quad9, rejecting requests to require DNS blocking. More recently, a French court rejected penalties against Cloudflare for failing to block through 1.1.1.1, finding that other actions we had taken through our CDN and reverse-proxy service were sufficient to comply with the underlying order.Undeterred, rightsholders have continued to press for blocking by public resolvers with a level of determination that is hard to explain. A recent study found that the majority of online piracy in Europe is hosted in Europe. If rightsholders want to genuinely address online infringement, there are far better places to focus their efforts — most notably by requiring the hosting providers who actually store the pirated files to take them down.Forcing public DNS resolvers out of markets hurts the InternetThe French court’s penalty order against Quad9 represents the culmination of these rightsholder efforts. As a small non-profit that only offers a public DNS resolver, Quad9 had no alternative methods for demonstrating compliance with the order. The French court therefore granted rightsholders’ request to issue penalties for non-compliance, forcing Quad9 to undermine its own service or leave the country.Quad9’s withdrawal from France represents a genuine loss for the Internet. In the short term, removal of Quad9’s resolver is likely to cause immediate and potentially severe disruptions to Internet access. Because of the way their service is set up to preserve privacy, Quad9 has no way to know who exactly is using its resolver, let alone to directly communicate to them about the upcoming withdrawal of services. That means that individuals, hospitals, universities, and government offices all may be caught unaware when the resolver stops working. Again, because of how DNS resolvers work, those users will not receive a detailed error message explaining what happened — their Internet will simply stop working. The problem will be even worse when devices like security systems or thermostats are hardwired to use Quad9’s resolver. Fixing these issues may be slow or even impossible.More generally, driving Quad9 out of France does absolutely nothing to prevent online infringement, but it does leave everyday Internet users with fewer safe, reliable options, particularly ones like Quad9 that also protect users from phishing and malware. Taking established public DNS resolvers away forces Internet users to fall back on the default resolvers offered by their ISPs (which may monetize their browsing data), try lesser-known resolvers operated by anonymous or less trustworthy organizations, or run their own resolver. The irony is that Quad9 and Cloudflare are being pulled into court precisely because we are transparent, reputable organizations. Penalizing transparent services only pushes users toward less accountable corners of the web.The way forwardIf nothing else, we hope that Quad9’s announcement may serve as a wake-up call to rightsholders and policymakers that seeking to block infringement at every layer of the Internet has real costs.We believe there are far better ways to work together to fight piracy without compromising the integrity of the Internet, and Cloudflare continues to pursue innovative collaborations to that end. Our collective efforts in addressing online infringement would be put to better use in seeking to have such content removed at the hosting provider, which would both achieve a more durable solution and avoid collateral damage to the Internet.In the meantime, we will keep resisting misguided efforts to require blocking by public DNS resolvers. Along with Google and Cisco, we have appealed the Belgian order that would impose penalties on us if we do not start DNS blocking by January 2027. We hope to get a resolution before the end of the year that would avoid another situation like the one Quad9 faces now in France.We have grave concerns about the disruption that would be caused if additional resolvers are forced to withdraw from markets, and we will continue to advocate against that possible result.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "subset",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-17T22:13:34.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://blog.cloudflare.com/_emdash/api/media/file/01M7NJA0M7FAQEQZBW0K0W5KKS.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://blog.cloudflare.com/quad9-withdrawal-from-france/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 9,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-11-17-nvidia-says-it-does-not-intend-to-reinstate-jensen-huang-as-",
      "title": "Nvidia says it does not intend to reinstate Jensen Huang as CEO",
      "summary": "Starting Nvidia's quarterly earnings call on Tuesday, new board chair Harvey Jones said that when former CEO Jensen Huang resigned, \"the Nvidia board unanimously accepted his resignation and does not intend to reinstate Jensen as CEO.\" \nJones added, \"We will not be addressing or taking questions on today's call regarding the circumstances of Jensen's resignation and the ongoing investigation.\"\nHua",
      "body": "Starting Nvidia’s quarterly earnings call on Tuesday, new board chair Harvey Jones said that when former CEO Jensen Huang resigned, “the Nvidia board unanimously accepted his resignation and does not intend to reinstate Jensen as CEO.”\nJones added, “We will not be addressing or taking questions on today’s call regarding the circumstances of Jensen’s resignation and the ongoing investigation.”\nHuang had publicly floated the idea of returning earlier on Tuesday. In a statement posted to X, he wrote that he “did not live up to the standards expected of everyone” at the company, that he regretted his mistakes, and that he would cooperate with the Nvidia board’s investigation into his actions. He added that he’d be willing to return to the chief executive role if offered it, though a day earlier, the Nvidia board reportedly said it was not considering bringing him back. “NVIDIA is a once-in-a-generation company,” Huang wrote. “If the Board of Directors decides that my return is in the best interest of our shareholders, our employees, the company, and America, I would be honored to answer these calls and return as CEO.”\nBut since his November 12th resignation, Huang remains entangled in scandal. He’s reportedly had three affairs, two with subordinates at Nvidia, one with an alleged Chinese intelligence asset suspected of facilitating illicit chip sales. (Huang has pushed against restrictions on Nvidia chip sales in China.) He’s scheduled to testify before Congress on December 1st, and an internal company investigation is ongoing.\nTech industry heavyweights have still voiced their support for Huang, dubbing him “irreplaceable” and petitioning for his return. Some have cast doubt on the allegations that Alice Huang (no relation) was spying on behalf of China and painted the leaks about their relationship as a conspiracy against the former CEO. President Donald Trump, a close ally of Huang for much of the past year, has also backed him. On Monday, Trump said he “would not mind” Huang returning, adding that “I think he learned a big lesson — I guess maybe one lesson is more important than another.”\nTrump’s closeness with Huang could also complicate things for Nvidia, however. The company has ramped up its lobbying in Washington, D.C., spending $5 million in 2025 and $4.7 million so far in 2026. But as Democrats prepare to take Congress, they’re planning to investigate Trump’s dealings with Big Tech and other industries, alleging potential corruption. “The election wasn’t very positive for AI, and [the news around Huang’s resignation] is only going to complicate things, especially for the company in particular but for AI as a whole going forward,” Brendan Glavin, director of insights at OpenSecrets, told The Verge in an interview last week.\nLior Prosor, managing partner at Deep33, a tech VC firm, said last week that he didn’t expect Huang’s resignation to pop the AI bubble, although Huang has had a significant hand in mobilizing funds for the AI infrastructure buildout in the U.S. Under Huang’s leadership in August, Nvidia entered into partnerships with six financial institutions for about $500 billion to fund such endeavors. “NVIDIA supplies roughly 75 percent of the AI compute hardware the industry consumes, and demand continues to dramatically outpace supply,” Deep33’s Prosor said in an interview with The Verge. “Neither its customers nor its competitors can easily replace the capabilities it provides, and the economic incentives driving the AI trade remain unchanged.”\nEven so, a potential market cooling has some worried, as Anthropic’s IPO, slated to potentially be the largest of all time, is set for Thursday. The chipmaker had agreed to buy $10 billion in Anthropic shares via private placement, on top of a $10 billion funding promise Nvidia had already made last year.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
