      "id": "bbc-2026-11-17-we-can-t-afford-to-hire-under-25s-say-firms-will-milburn-pla",
      "title": "We can't afford to hire under-25s, say firms. Will Milburn plans help?",
      "summary": "The proposal to cut employers' National Insurance costs is \"essential\" to tackling the Neet problem, one business group says.",
      "body": "ByNick Edser and Simon BrowningBusiness reportersLowering employers' National Insurance (NI) payments for those between 21 and 24, and cash incentives to take on some workers are among proposals put forward in Alan Milburn's report to get more 18-24 year-olds working.Many companies have complained that recent increases in National Insurance and the minimum wage have made it more expensive to take on staff.We talked to firms and business groups about the issues they face when hiring younger workers and what they make of the proposals.Costs the key issueImage caption, Bournemouth-based café owner Ben Blades says he cannot justify the extra cost of training up new staffCutting NI costs is \"right in the sweet spot of what firms need to hit the hire button\", said the Recruitment and Employment Confederation (REC).The Milburn report proposes NI thresholds could be raised over the next five years, from £5,000 to £10,000 for those aged 21 to 24, and suggests prioritising the retail and hospitality sectors.Bournemouth-based café owner Ben Blades says the high costs he faces currently make it difficult to employ young people with no work experience.\"My labour costs are already over 45%. We simply cannot justify the extra cost to put on an extra staff member to train someone up for three months,\" he says.\"In hospitality our margins have been squeezed and squeezed and squeezed\"As a result, he says it currently makes more sense to employ someone who already has experience.Allen Simpson, chief executive of UKHospitality, says the recommendation to raise  the threshold is \"the right one\" and \"would be an immediate shot in the arm for job creation\".But the British Chambers of Commerce (BCC) argues the Milburn report does not go far enough in tackling costs facing businesses.It says while the NI proposal is welcome, the change should be wider than just retail and hospitality, otherwise it \"risks adding complexity and blocking off potential career paths\".Cash incentivesImage caption, Ally Fir says if a state scheme was running she'd \"jump on it right away\"Other proposals put forward by the Milburn review include cash incentives to employ younger workers.Employers who take on any young person who has been Neet - not in education, employment or training - for six months should receive a £3,000 payment, and small businesses - with less than 50 employees - that do so should receive an extra £2,000There would also be an extra £2,000 payment if the young person has been in care or is disabled.Ally Fir runs a bookshop in Westbourne, alongside her partner Matt, and thinks more state support is needed.She says incentivised schemes for small business \"would help us to employ more, especially young people. If a scheme like that was going we'd jump on it right away.\"Fir says she gets job applications \"every single day\". She'd like to take more people on but \"it is the cost of employing people that makes that impossible\".Tom Parsons has already had experience of using government schemes to employ young people at his design consultancy, Aetha, in Poole, Dorset.The Kickstart Scheme in 2020 meant he could take on two young people in fully funded six-month job placements offering 25 hours a week.Image caption, Tom Parsons says the old Kickstart Scheme was a game-changer for those employed under it\"One of them stayed with us for five years and the other one I think about three years. And it completely changed the game for them,\" Tom told the BBC. \"So if there was a scheme like that, again, that would really help us.\"He says he wants to employ young people but costs remain a huge barrier.Guaranteed hours a concernOne of the government's flagship employment rights reforms risks weakening the hiring of young people, according to Milburn's report.Ministers are abolishing what they call ''exploitative'' zero-hours contracts and replacing them with guaranteed hours contracts which would be based on the number of hours people normally work.Much of the detail is yet to be finalised but the Employment Rights Act is due to be enacted next year.Milburn is concerned that the changes could cause problems \"at a time when the youth labour market is already weak'.'The REC said companies were \"particularly worried\" over the guaranteed hours measure.It added that addressing the Neets problem gave ministers \"the perfect opportunity\" to reconsider the issue.Help getting staffImage source, Dean GreeneYoung people should have a legal right to a route into education, training or work, and would be supported to do so under an \"opportunity guarantee\", Milburn has proposed.This could help firms which are struggling to recruit. Thirty-five-year-old Dean Greene, a sales director at waste management facility Williams Environmental, says it's tricky finding staff.\"We didn't get many applicants for it at all,\" says Greene, referencing an ad for a marketing and sales role.\"There wasn't a huge uptake from that sort of 16-to-24 range,\" he adds.He thinks the lack of applicants may be because the waste industry is perceived as \"mucky and dirty\", but he is confident many would be interested if they knew the job was available.Pointing to himself as an example of progressing in the industry, he tells me how he started as an admin officer scanning paperwork at 18 before remaining with the company for 17 years.Get in touchShare your experiences and ask your questions.",
      "source": "BBC",
