    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-17-us-bank-services-returning-to-normal-after-major-outage",
      "title": "US bank services returning to normal after major outage",
      "summary": "Chase Bank, the largest US bank, said a software bug might be causing the issue, but that it was not \"systemic'\" or \"cyber-related\".",
      "body": "Thousands of customers at major US banks, including JP Morgan Chase, Wells Fargo and Bank of America, reported trouble accessing services, though Chase said its issue has now been resolved.Chase Bank - the largest banking institution in the US - said a \"software bug\" in its hardware systems was behind the glitch and it was not a cyber security incident.A spokesperson said \"this was not a systemic issue\" but noted the impact may have affected other banks \"only to the extent that they were a party to a transaction with us\". The issue comes after a string of unrelated technical problems, including a massive Microsoft outage and a network failure that grounded all US flights.In addition, United Airlines also reported a \"widespread system outage\".The problems with the banks started on Monday afternoon. There were almost 5,000 reports of problems with Chase Bank's services, according to the Downdetector website.Clients reported issues accessing their online accounts, viewing balances and performing transactions, like bank transfers and wire transfers, according to the BBC's US media partner CBS News, though Chase Bank told the BBC that customers still had access to their accounts and funds.A Chase Bank customer in Texas told CBS that he could not deposit a check at a Dallas branch on Monday, reporting that he was told his transaction could not be completed. He said a bank teller told the customer in front of him that the \"system was down\" but did not provide additional details. Chase Bank told its customers that \"some transactions are taking longer than usual to complete\" but they still had \"access to their accounts and funds\". The bank, a subsidiary of JP Morgan Chase, serves more than 86m customers at 5,000 branches across the US, as well as 7.4m small businesses, according to the company's webpage. It is also the top credit card issuer in the US.The exact nature of the bug was unclear. The BBC has contacted Chase Bank. Bank of America customers had issues at ATMs and using mobile banking services online, while US Bank customers reported issues with their online accounts and the bank's mobile app, CBS reported.Customers using another institution, US Bank, also reported issues transferring funds and Wells Fargo customers said they had problems signing into their accounts, getting account balances and making transactions.US Bank said some its systems were not directly affected but clients using the Zelle, a digital payment platform, received error messages saying their payments could not be sent. Zelle has said its service is \"operating as it should\" and issues at other banks could be affecting payments.Customers also reported issues with Truist, PNC and Capital One but all these companies told the BBC they have not been impacted. As of 18:00 EST (23:00 GMT), Chase, Bank of America and US Bank had seen a fall in the number of reported outages.Just as the banking issue affected US customers on Monday, United Airlines confirmed a \"widespread system outage\", which it described as \"a technology issue affecting departing flights\". That issue resolved within a few hours.The Chicago-based airline did not say what is believed to have caused the issue, but a spokesperson said it is not related to cybersecurity.The issue did not appear to be affecting any other airlines. The United issue is separate from the one that hit the US Federal Aviation Administration (FAA), but comes as other industries report different outages around the US.On Friday, the FAA issued a rare nationwide a ground stop, halting all US air travel due to a telecommunication and network upgrade issues.The halt lasted just over four hours, but the knock-on effect continues to snarl US air travel. Additional reporting by Max Matza.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-17T15:04:24.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/d633/live/6fb04df0-e212-11f1-b9ca-b9964628501b.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cj752wvqneq6o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-11-17-the-aliens-are-coming-in-3-body-problem-s-first-season-2-tra",
      "title": "The aliens are coming in 3 Body Problem’s first season 2 trailer",
      "summary": "The first season of Netflix's 3 Body Problem series ended with the remaining members of the Oxford Five disillusioned at the idea of humanity being utterly unprepared to deal with the San-Ti's arrival on Earth. But in the first trailer for the show's upcoming second season, it looks like the world's greatest minds have been dreaming up inventive ways to fight back against the oncoming alien threat",
      "body": "The first season of Netflix’s 3 Body Problem series ended with the remaining members of the Oxford Five disillusioned at the idea of humanity being utterly unprepared to deal with the San-Ti’s arrival on Earth. But in the first trailer for the show’s upcoming second season, it looks like the world’s greatest minds have been dreaming up inventive ways to fight back against the oncoming alien threat.\nThough Sophon (Sea Shimooka) insists that the San-Ti wish humans no ill will and only want to speak in the new trailer, MI6 chief Thomas Wade (Liam Cunningham) is certain that the aliens’ claims of sending an “emissary” is a ruse. Wade remembers vividly how the San-Ti likened humanity to bugs, and he knows that extermination is central to their plan to take the planet for themselves.\nWith the Staircase Project having been an abject failure and the San-Ti beaming ominous messages into the sky, people have reason to believe that the aliens might really be able to conquer the world when they arrive from another galaxy in a few hundred years. But for all the doom and gloom, Dr. Saul Durand (Jovan Adepo) can’t shake the feeling that the San-Ti’s attempt at assassinating him could mean that he and the other Wallfacers might actually have a chance at turning the tides.\nIn addition to some new glimpses of the San-Ti’s virtual reality (and that nifty headset), the season 2 trailer features a handful of what appears to be people working on a weapons system based in space. The shots make it look like 3 Body Problem’s next chapter will be bigger and more epic as its heroes get even more serious about coming up with novel ways to handle their invaders. The trailer doesn’t really make clear how much of Cixin Liu’s novel this new season will tackle in its six episodes. But when the show returns on January 14th, it’s probably going to be another wild ride.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Books",
