      "id": "theverge-2026-11-17-this-e-ink-photo-frame-can-double-as-your-family-s-digital-d",
      "title": "This E Ink photo frame can double as your family’s digital dashboard",
      "summary": "Trmnl, a company best known for its highly customizable E Ink dashboards that can function as anything from a desktop calendar to a stock tracker, has introduced a new display that's also well suited for showing artwork and photos. It's not the company's first color E Ink device, but instead of being limited to just four colors, the new Frame can display thousands of shades using a 31.5-inch E Ink",
      "body": "Trmnl, a company best known for its highly customizable E Ink dashboards that can function as anything from a desktop calendar to a stock tracker, has introduced a new display that’s also well suited for showing artwork and photos. It’s not the company’s first color E Ink device, but instead of being limited to just four colors, the new Frame can display thousands of shades using a 31.5-inch E Ink Spectra 6 panel, similar to what you’ll find in products from Aura and Pocketbook.\nThe Frame is now available for preorder for $1,299 in a handmade wooden body and you can choose from four different types of wood: white oak, cherry, sapele, and walnut. The first batch will be a limited run of around 470 units that’s already more than half sold out, and delivery of most of the displays is expected to happen in early 2027. Optional add-ons include a matching wooden kickstand if you don’t want to hang it on a wall, and a larger 20,000mAh battery. However, the Frame should run for months between charges with the standard 10,000mAh option.\n[Image: The Frame can be used to only show art and photos, or you can add extra information using a large collection of downloadable plugins. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/11/frame.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nWhat sets the Frame apart from other large format E Ink art frames and posters is that it’s open source and can do more than display images. Through plugins you can build yourself, or download from a marketplace featuring over 1,400 at this point, the Frame can also be used as a dashboard showing the weather, to-do lists, or whatever info you want hanging on your wall.\nIf you do use it for displaying imagery, Trmnl developed Lazarus, a color conversion engine the company believes provides the “best color matching on the market for any Spectra 6 display.” If you disagree, because the Frame is open source, you can adjust the color tuning to match your own preferences.\nAs with Trmnl’s other E Ink products, the Frame can be connected to the internet to dynamically display whatever information you need it to. But to further extend its battery life, you can also fill a microSD card with a folder of images files and use the Frame offline. You can customize how frequently a new photo is displayed, or set up schedules so that your favorite piece of art stays on screen longer than others. While the Frame is more expensive than other E Ink Spectra 6 products currently available, the appeal here is the flexibility to use it as more than just a digital poster.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Gadgets",
        "News",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Andrew Liszewski",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-17T14:49:21.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/11/frame1.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.708427060106,100,78.583145879787",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/1035258/trmnl-e-ink-spectra-6-frame-dashboard-display-poster-photos",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-17-we-don-t-need-more-apps-we-need-better-open-source",
      "title": "We don't need more apps. We need better open source",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://jordankrueger.com/blog/better-open-source-not-more-apps/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50532086\nPoints: 24\n# Comments: 4",
      "body": "Every day, a dozen people post a new app they built with AI over a weekend: a habit tracker, a to-do list, another note-taking app. I’m glad! More people building things is one of the best parts of this moment.\nBut far less of that energy goes into the open source software we already rely on.\nPeople call this new way of building “vibecoding.” I hate the name; it makes a real shift in who gets to write software sound like a joke. I’ll call it AI-assisted coding, and I think it could aim somewhere more useful.\nFree and open source software already does most of what my clients and I need. What it often can’t do is run well on a cheap server, install in one step, or stay up without forum threads at 11pm.\nI self-host a lot of my own stuff, and I help progressive nonprofits with their tech for a living. I run a Matrix server (Synapse, Element and a moderation bot) for a community with thousands of users. I also run apps on Coolify and Hetzner, keep my paperwork in Paperless-ngx, and own a Mac Mini that’s always one download from a full disk.\nThe software is usually great. Running it is the hard part. Install guides assume you know five other tools. Upgrades break things. Apps eat 4GB of RAM to serve a handful of people. Error messages tell you nothing.\nMy nonprofit clients would love to leave Big Tech for privacy, cost and owning their data. But the open source option usually needs a part-time sysadmin they don’t have, so they pay for the corporate tool.\nFour jobs worth your weekend\nMake it faster. uv took a job Python developers do every day, installing packages, and made it 8 to 10 times faster than pip in its makers’ benchmarks (up to 115 times with a warm cache). Find the slow part of something people already use, measure it, fix it.\nMake it easy to install. Too many apps need a database, a cache, a background worker, a reverse proxy and a long config file before you see a login screen. Aim for one command, sensible defaults and a setup that works the first time on a cheap server.\nMake it friendly to run. Installing happens once; running it happens every day after. Give it a simple admin screen, safe upgrades, built-in backups, errors that say what to do next, and an easy, secure way to manage logins and add admins.\nMake it lighter. If a tool runs happily on a $5 server, an old laptop or a Raspberry Pi, far more people can use it. Lighter software costs less to run and uses less energy.\nA new to-do app usually helps the few people who find it. Making a tool millions already use 20% lighter helps all of them, every day.\nThis is also the work that slides when maintainers are tired, and many are unpaid volunteers. We saw the risk in 2024. Someone spent years earning the trust of the worn-out solo maintainer of xz, a compression tool inside most Linux systems, then slipped a backdoor into it.\nI think this unglamorous work suits AI well: profiling slow code, writing tests that make big changes safe, testing installers, rewriting cryptic errors, writing the missing docs. It takes patience more than brilliance, plus a person who checks every line.\nIt matters politically too. If organizations are going to own their data instead of renting it from a few giant companies, open source has to be something a 20-person nonprofit can run. I want AI’s gains shared widely. Better public tools that anyone can use, change and share are one way to get there.\nDon’t be a jerk about it\nMaintainers have already seen the wrong way. The curl project ended its bug bounty in January 2026 after a flood of fake, AI-generated security reports. By April, its lead developer wrote that the fakes were mostly gone and almost every report used AI, most of them very good. The new problem is volume. Even good work can bury a maintainer. So:\nUse the thing first and find what actually hurts.\nTalk to maintainers before writing code. Nobody wants a surprise 4,000-line pull request.\nBring numbers. “Startup went from 40 seconds to 4, here’s how I measured it” beats “faster.”\nKeep changes small and tested.\nSay you used AI, and own the result. Stick around to fix what breaks.\nIf they say no, help downstream with a better installer or setup guide, and keep it current.\nPick one open source tool you use. Find the most annoying thing about installing or running it. Spend a weekend fixing it, with the people who maintain it. Then tell me about it.\nuv: Python packaging in Rust, Astral\nThe end of the curl bug-bounty, Daniel Stenberg\nHigh quality chaos, Daniel Stenberg\nXZ Utils backdoor, Tukaani Project\nSubscribe toMission Control ➡️I share thoughts on online tools, learnings from my nonprofit advocacy consulting business, projects I'm launching, Artificial Intelligence, and personal updates — directly to your inbox.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "pandemicsoul",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-17T14:45:36.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://jordankrueger.com/images/gallery/blog-better-open-source-not-more-apps.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://jordankrueger.com/blog/better-open-source-not-more-apps/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-17-out-of-work-and-not-in-college-how-quickly-could-milburn-s-p",
      "title": "Out of work and not in college: How quickly could Milburn's plans help you?",
      "summary": "The government-backed report says its recommendations could help 440,000 young people into work by 2036.",
      "body": "Image source, Getty ImagesByHayley ClarkeEducation reporterAlan Milburn has said the country needs a \"complete system reset\" to bring down the number of young people not in work or education - known as \"Neets\".In a major review, published on Tuesday,, external he suggested a raft of proposals to address the problem, which mostly cover England but he says can be adapted in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland too.Each of his recommendations will need to be accepted by the government and implemented and paid for before they become reality - and that could take some time. So how might today's proposals affect you if you are Neet?A new Youth Opportunity ServiceAt the heart of the recommendations, Milburn says, is a new Youth Opportunity Service. He wants this to be implemented locally, with each area drawing up a plan to help their own young people.It would bring together employment, careers, health and practical support, with each young person getting a named adviser and a personalised plan.Milburn wants areas to begin building their Youth Opportunity Service from 2027, with one in place everywhere by the end of 2029.Within 14 days of entering the service, a young person would then meet with a named adviser. Within four weeks, they would have their \"personalised participation plan\", setting out what both the support services and the young person involved would do, and when.Jobs wise, Milburn wants there to be initiatives like work placements of up to 12 weeks, structured volunteering opportunities, and help to create businesses.He also wants those who remain Neet - despite enagaging with support - to be provided with a paid six-month job, funded by the government.The government has already committed to a legal guarantee of job support in theory, but the exact workings still need to be worked out.More apprenticeships and college placesAlongside more work opportunities, Milburn wants a \"major expansion\" in further education for 18- to 24 year-olds, as well as more apprenticeship places.Over the next decade, he wants the number of young people completing an apprenticeship to double, and for apprenticeships to rival university as an option for school leavers.Colleges should also get new funding for participation and work-readiness, the review says, initially targeted at 16- and 17-year-olds who are Neet or at serious risk of becoming so.He also wants the government to develop new industry-led technical colleges and specialist campuses.If the reforms are enacted, Milburn says young people should have better access to high-quality further education and apprenticeships by 2028.Under the proposals, schools, colleges and universities would be judged by whether pupils make successful transitions into further learning and work, rather than just academic achievements.By 2036, he wants 35% of each year group to have started a high-quality apprenticeship by age 25.More mental health supportMilburn has said he wants a fundamental transformation of how mental health support for young people is delivered.Young people who are Neet should be guaranteed fast-track access to services within three months, according to his recommendations. This would include things like vocational rehabilitation, talking therapies and counselling as part of a young person's plan with their local Youth Opportunity Service - which would be rolled out between 2027 and 2029 under the plans.There has been a big rise in mental distress among children and young people in recent years, and Milburn's report comes at a time the NHS is facing huge pressures.Milburn wants mental health support to be available for all young people digitally, without needing a diagnosis.Some areas are already using these approaches, but scaling them up would be a major challenge and would take a long time to introduce across the board.Plus, Milburn wants \"fitness to work\" notes to be taken out of the hands of GPs and put into community work and health teams, and for them to be \"a trigger for help - not simply a certificate of absence\".Milburn wants these changes to begin in 2027 and would be linked to the Youth Opportunity Service rollout, which would be complete by 2029 under the plans.More incentives for employers to hire young peopleMilburn has said he wants companies to be incentivised to employ more young people, with the report highlighting a competitive jobs market and applicants who often fail to hear back from potential employers.The report includes three asks of employers: to create more genuine first jobs, more first-job apprenticeships and much wider access to meaningful work experience.Plus, Milburn wants large employers to find ways of responding to rejected job applicants.These changes should be felt by both employers and the young people looking for jobs by 2029, the report says.To achieve this, the report suggests a £3,000 payment for employers who take on someone who has been Neet for six months, with a further premium of £2,000 available if they are a small business.An extra payment of £2,000 would go to employers taking on somebody taking substantially greater barriers, such as care leavers or disabled young people.These payments are proposed to be part of changes to the Youth Jobs Grant - which already exists, external - but they would expand under Milburn's proposals from 2027.Get in touchShare your experiences and ask your questions.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-17T14:43:05.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/4190/live/a11a6ca0-e284-11f1-b166-176de21e2352.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6x2wnz3k7lno?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
