      "id": "bbc-2026-11-17-why-milburn-s-neets-reforms-will-only-save-200m-a-year-by-20",
      "title": "Why Milburn's Neets reforms will only save £200m a year by 2036",
      "summary": "Milburn's plan to help young people promises to save money from the UK's welfare bill. BBC Verify has taken a look at the numbers.",
      "body": "Image source, BBC/PAByBen ChuPolicy and analysis correspondentAlan Milburn's long-awaited plan to help young people who are neither learning nor earning promises to save money from the UK's welfare bill.Milburn said he believes his proposed reforms, which would reduce benefits for some young people who are not in education, employment or training (Neets) but aim to get them jobs in the long run, could save as much as £13.8bn in welfare costs over the next 10 years.But after the extra spending he has proposed for job guarantees from the government, hiring incentives for firms, and health and education support, his reforms are only projected to save £200m a year of taxpayers' money overall by 2036.BBC Verify has taken a look through the numbers.How many young people claim benefits?Analysts have expressed concerns about the sharply rising number of young people claiming health and disability benefits in recent years.The Milburn report estimates that around £8bn a year is currently being spent on benefits directly for young people through the welfare system, made up of £4.6bn on Universal Credit and £3.2bn on Personal independence payments (Pip).The number of 16-24 year olds in England and Wales claiming the health element of Universal Credit has increased from 105,000 in 2020 to 193,000 this year according to the Department for Work and Pensions (DWP).Young Pip claimants have also increased from 230,000 to 511,000 over that time, while official forecasts suggest this number is set to rise, external above 800,000 by 2037.Would Milburn's reforms reduce welfare spending?Milburn's reforms would combine two major policies in relation to young people:A significant increase in government spending to help them into employment and trainingReductions in the welfare payments available to some of the groupMilburn's long-term plans call for an additional £5.8bn in spending per year by 2036 on things such as employment subsidies and mental health support to help get young people into work or education.He is also proposing a new benefits system so young people would not be able to access the same kind of health and disability benefits as older people, including creating a Youth Allowance instead of giving under-25s Universal Credit and a new Additional Cost Allowance that could replace Pip benefits.Milburn estimates that these reforms would contribute to a reduction of total welfare spending of £3.6bn a year by 2036 relative to where spending would otherwise have been in that year, and said it could save a cumulative £13.8bn from the welfare bill over the next decade.Milburn said his diagnosis applies UK-wide, but education, skills, health and local government are devolved to Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland. He has called on the devolved administrations to agree \"a shared set of headline outcomes\".How big is the total welfare bill?Official forecasts show the total welfare system, external is projected to cost £353bn this year. There is no official forecast of the level of total welfare spending in 2036, but likely inflation means it would be above the £431bn currently projected for 2032.Around half of this cost is the state pension, on which spending is forecast to rise from £154bn a year to £193bn a year. Spending on Pip and disability living allowance are also set to rise over that time from £40bn a year to £57bn a year.However, as a share of the overall economy, the increase in the cost of the welfare system is less dramatic.Total welfare spending is forecast to rise from 11.1% of GDP this year to 11.3% in 2032. This would be below the the peak of around 12% seen in 2013 following the global financial crisis.That has led some economists and researchers to question the argument, external that welfare spending is out of control.The reductions touted as part of Milburn's reforms, if delivered, is likely to only reduce the UK's benefits bill by a relatively small amount. As a share of today's welfare bill, the £3.6bn of annual savings in 2036 would be only 1%.How much could the reforms save?As well as the £5.8bn that would be spent on supporting young people into work, training or college, Milburn estimates the government would reap £2.4bn a year in additional taxes, as more people would be in work.This £2.4bn of additional taxes combined with the £3.6bn in lower welfare spending would add up to £6bn a year in 2036.That implies a net saving for the government as a result of Milburn's proposed package of £200m by that year, although this saving would grow over time.The £200m net savings figure in the Milburn report is also in today's money - and would be larger in cash terms in 2036, though the actual cash figure will depend on inflation over the next decade.Additional reporting by Tom Edginton and data journalism by Christine Jeavans",
