      "id": "bbc-2026-11-17-bbc-tracks-down-businessman-at-centre-of-council-scandal",
      "title": "BBC tracks down businessman at centre of council scandal",
      "summary": "Reporters find Liam Kavanagh at an exclusive apartment complex in Bucharest.",
      "body": "At the heart of the story are two seemingly very different men.Kavanagh, known by some as Mr Sunshine, was the charismatic businessman selling investments in solar farms. Sean Clark was Thurrock's finance director - the council official who found the money to invest with him.And Clark found a lot of it.He borrowed huge sums from other local authorities around the UK, often through deals that could be arranged with a few emails. At one point Thurrock owed more than £1bn to other councils.The strategy initially appeared to be a triumph. The investments were generating millions of pounds in income at a time when councils were struggling with government spending cuts.But behind the apparent success, warning signs were mounting.Journalist Gareth Davies, of the Bureau of Investigative Journalism, has spent years investigating what happened.\"I think this story is so shocking because of the scale of the greed, of the incompetence - the fact that this council gambled with huge sums of public money and lost.\"Describing the lack of records surrounding some of the deals, Davies said: \"There wasn't even evidence at the time that these transactions were made, that any of this had ever taken place. I mean, it's mind-boggling.\"Thurrock's own financial adviser, Arlingclose, was also sounding the alarm.Arlingclose chief executive Mark Pickering described the council's investment activity as \"pretty extreme\" and said his firm felt \"the likelihood of them blowing up was pretty high\".\"You try several times to help a friend - and after a while you have to leave them to their own devices, don't you?\" he said.Arlingclose eventually cut ties with Thurrock. But Clark carried on borrowing and investing.Meanwhile, leaked bank records show millions moving from Kavanagh's company into his personal accounts, alongside payments for luxury purchases.He also started to look like a different man after Thurrock's investments.\"You can look in terms of the photographs, before and after,\" said Davies.\"At one end you've got a fairly unkempt man whose nickname in the early days was the sticky Bandit because he looked like one of the bad guys out of Home Alone.\"A few years later - this bronzed, muscular man who's obviously enjoying a luxury lifestyle.\"For six years, Kavanagh avoided answering many of the key questions about what happened.So we went looking for him, and eventually traced him to an exclusive apartment complex in northern Bucharest.",
