      "id": "bbc-2026-11-16-from-batman-to-bond-how-robert-pattinson-landed-the-role-of-",
      "title": "From Batman to Bond - how Robert Pattinson landed the role of 007",
      "summary": "The Twilight, Batman and Odyssey star's profile will go even more stellar when he plays James Bond.",
      "body": "Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Robert Pattinson has starred in films including the Twilight franchise and The Batman ByHelen BushbyCulture reporterRobert Pattinson's career is already flying high with roles in films including the Twilight series, The Batman and The Odyssey, so signing up to play James Bond will propel his profile into the stratosphere. He's already established himself as a leading man, action star and character actor, which is clearly why he is about to play the suave, British secret agent 007.At 40, he is slightly older than some of the other men reportedly lined up for the role, but he's also the most famous - something the new Bond studio Amazon-MGM may have favoured, with an eye on the box office. His recent work has garnered both critical and box office success, including Christopher Nolan's summer blockbuster The Odyssey, in which he played the villainous Antinous.Pattinson was also called \"Oscar-worthy\" by the Independent and \"superb\" by The Times, for his performance in November's Primetime, as the real-life host of US TV show To Catch a Predator.He also helped lead 2022's The Batman to the second-biggest North American opening of the Covid-19 pandemic behind Spider-Man: No Way Home. The Guardian said he played the \"brooding\" role with \"appealing vulnerability, external\". Meanwhile in dark romantic comedy The Drama, he and his co-star gave a \"career-highlight performance\", external. It's one of three films he appeared in with Zendaya this year, along with The Odyssey and Dune: Part Three.Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Robert Pattinson starred in 2022's The Batman, which was described by critics as a grim, gritty, and gripping super-noirBorn in Barnes, south-west London, Pattinson grew up with his father, a classic car trader and mother, who worked at a modelling agency, and two elder sisters. His interest in acting started while he was still at school, when he joined the Barnes Theatre Club.\"I wouldn't be acting if it wasn't for that little club,\" he told Your Local Guardian in 2008, external.\"I went originally because a lot of pretty girls went there,\" he said. \"I worked backstage and I started acting when all the good people left and I was the only one tall enough to play the lead.\"In a weird sort of way, that stuff was probably the best I've done and the stuff I am most proud of.\"He landed his first big film role more than two decades ago, playing Cedric Diggory in 2005's Harry Potter and the Goblet of Fire. A relatively minor character, but it was enough to get him noticed.Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Robert Pattinson and Kristen Stewart starred in five Twilight films from 2005 to 2012Three years later, he had a starring role in the first of five Twilight films, along with Kristen Stewart, in which they played star-crossed lovers - vampire Edward Cullen and mortal-turned vampire Kristen Stewart. Based on Stephanie Meyer's hugely popular young adult books, the films globally grossed more than $3bn (£2.2bn) by 2012, and established Pattinson as a heartthrob. He spoke in his early career of finding his often adoring, screaming fans hard to deal with. \"Although is part of the job, it feels like they have been paid to turn up and scream at me at 7.30pm,\" he said. \"Nothing I don't think can prepare you for the fans. I don't know why I'm not getting used to it but I'm completely overwhelmed every time.\"Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Pattinson and Zendaya have appeared in three films together this year - The Drama, The Odyssey and Dune: Part ThreeThe following year though, he was back in big-studio territory, with Odyssey director Christopher Nolan, in sci-fi thriller Tenet, and again in 2022 with The Batman, directed by Matt Reeves and co-starring Zoe Kravitz and Paul Dano.Pattinson told Screen Daily why he returned to big-budget films, explaining: \"I guess I was trying to do the opposite of what I've been doing. I started thinking, 'I really want to do a big thing.' \"I hadn't done something with a massive budget for ages. But you approach it exactly the same way.\"The Batman got generally positive reviews, re-establishing Pattinson with a broader range of fans. By 2025, when he was on the red carpet for Parasite director Bong Joon Ho's sci-fi black comedy, Mickey 17, he said it was \"quite strange\" to have a fanbase that was now dominated by men, external.\"There's like a bunch of guys, which is a new one for me!\" he told the Associated Press.Pattinson's private life has often created interest in the press, not least his previous relationships with Stewart, and then the singer FKA Twigs. Since 2018, he has been in a relationship with model Suki Waterhouse. They got engaged in December 2023 and had their first child, a daughter, in March.Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Robert Pattinson and Suki Waterhouse have been a couple since 2018With his latest role as James Bond, Pattinson will be extremely busy, not least because the forthcoming Batman sequel is reportedly due out in 2028. But the actor will have a head start as 007, having already worked with Bond 26 director Denis Villeneuve, who also helmed Dune: Part Three. Pattinson was full of praise for him on their most recent film, despite the strain of working in the furnace-like heat of the desert. \"The scale of this stuff is just unbelievable, you're having your own Dune theme park, where the gimbal is going upside down and flying, and this is not like work at all,\" he told the New York Times, external.\"There are no negatives. Apart from having your sweat drained out of the top of your head, and getting like bulbous sweat bubbles. But even then, I didn't even really notice them. \"He's so easy to work with, Denis, it's never really a stress.\"Sounds like Pattinson will have the cool head needed to juggle his forthcoming roles - including James Bond. You could even say he may end up being shaken, but not stirred. More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-16T15:02:41.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/5f95/live/31a7c6d0-e1e7-11f1-9f14-b33806cfd783.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c574290jvk8wo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 5,
      "language": "en"
    },
