      "id": "bbc-2026-11-16-women-s-criminal-records-wiped-for-abortion-offences",
      "title": "Women's criminal records wiped for abortion offences",
      "summary": "Women arrested or convicted for having illegal abortions will have their criminal records wiped as new atomatic pardons come into force in England and Wales.",
      "body": "Ms Packer, who was 41 at the time, took pills called mifepristone and misoprostol, which were prescribed over the phone due to Covid restrictions, to end a pregnancy in 2020.Prosecutors alleged that she knew she had been pregnant for more than 10 weeks, which she denied.Jurors deliberated for more than six hours to reach their unanimous verdict, clearing her name.Speaking with the BBC and Nicola's consent, her friend Ms Tumbridge said the whole incident was \"incredibly traumatic\". \"Nicky had what's known as a cryptic pregnancy - that's when you don't have any sign of a pregnancy, no baby bump, no other symptoms. \"She thought she was just a few weeks pregnant and she sought an abortion and was prescribed that by the NHS. Unfortunately, she was a lot more far gone.\"Nicola experienced a stillbirth and went to hospital. The staff there called the police. \"She was arrested. She spent a time in her hospital room recovering with two police officers present, one was a man. She was treated appallingly. She was made to use the toilet with the door open in front of the police officers.\"It took five years of investigations for a trial to eventually find her not guilty. \"Of course, by that point the damage had been done,\" says Tumbridge, \"and she'll live with that trauma for the rest of her life.\"At the time, The Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists said Nicola's trial demonstrated \"just how outdated and harmful\" that law was.It is now no longer a criminal offence for a pregnant woman to terminate her own pregnancy at any stage.Women affected do not need to take any action. Minister for Victims and Tackling Violence Against Women and Girls, Alex Davies-Jones, said pardoning women for abortion offences related to their own pregnancy had been a priority. \"I'm pleased to be able to commence the change that Parliament voted for.\"For those affected, I know that clear and accessible information about what happens next is vital. Women do not have to take any direct action themselves, but can find out more information online and contact our dedicated casework team.\"Heidi Stewart, from the abortion care provider BPAS, said: \"While no pardon can undo the distress and trauma these women have experienced, this historic change in the law recognises the profound harm caused by their criminalisation and represents a meaningful step towards justice.\"Abortion in England and Wales is legal with an authorised provider up to 24 weeks, with very limited circumstances allowing one after this time, such as when the mother's life is at risk or the child would be born with a severe disability.It is also legal to take prescribed medication at home if a woman is less than 10 weeks pregnant.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-16T11:45:33.000Z",
