      "id": "bbc-2026-11-16-gas-starts-flowing-from-jackdaw-after-government-green-light",
      "title": "Gas starts flowing from Jackdaw after government green light",
      "summary": "Ministers last month announced the Jackdaw gas field, which lies 150 miles east of Aberdeen, had been approved after years of uncertainty.",
      "body": "Image caption, The Jackdaw platform is located 150 miles east of AberdeenByPaul O'HareBBC ScotlandGas has begun flowing from the Jackdaw gas field, with the UK energy secretary saying it will help to heat millions of homes and businesses this winter.Ministers last month announced the Jackdaw field, which lies 150 miles east of Aberdeen, had been approved after years of uncertainty and a court case.It has now been announced that it has begun producing gas which will start flowing into Britain, according to its operator Adura.The UK government approved production from the gas field in the North Sea last month, amid intense debate about the project's environmental and economic impact.Jackdaw is operated by Adura under a joint venture between Shell and Norwegian state energy firm Equinor.A decision is yet to be taken on whether production can begin at Rosebank, the UK's largest known untapped oil field, which is located off the coast of Shetland in the North Atlantic.Energy Secretary Miatta Fahnbulleh said: \"Last month, I gave Jackdaw the green light and today its gas will start flowing into Britain, thanks to the hard work of the skilled North Sea workers.\"It means homegrown gas from this project will help to heat millions of British homes and businesses this winter and beyond, in a boost for our energy security.\"It also will help minimise the UK's use of imported higher emission gas as we continue our transition to renewables and nuclear.\"Claire Mueller, Jackdaw project director at Adura, said the gas field was \"now helping to power Britain\".She added: \"At a time of considerable uncertainty around the world, Jackdaw will provide secure energy from our own waters to heat British homes and power our economy, while reducing our reliance on imports with higher emissions.\"Jackdaw also demonstrates what can be achieved when Britain makes pragmatic, evidence-led decisions about the energy it needs, while properly accounting for our climate responsibilities.\"Mueller said the decision was a \"positive step towards restoring confidence in the North Sea\".She added that Adura was \"proud to be producing the oil and gas our country will continue to need for decades\".Figure caption, Why is the Jackdaw gas field approval so controversial?Adura has said Jackdaw will provide about 6% of UK gas production, enough to power 1.4 million homes. After imports are included, that amounts to about 2% of total UK demand.The gas will be pumped to the existing Shearwater platform for processing – known as a tie-back in industry terms – before being piped to the St Fergus gas terminal in Aberdeenshire.The UK government said that over its expected 11-year lifespan, Jackdaw would support the UK's gas supply, protect hundreds of jobs and add £2.9bn to the economy.The timeline of Jackdaw and RosebankThe decision to green-light Jackdaw drew criticism from some Labour backbenchers and environmentalists, while Conservatives called on the government to also approve the Rosebank oil field.Both projects were approved for development by the previous Tory government in 2022 and 2023 respectively but these decisions were struck down by the Scottish courts after a challenge from environmental campaigners.This followed a Supreme Court ruling that developers had to account for the climate impact caused by burning fossil fuels in their planning applications.Prime Minister Andy Burnham has pledged to stick to the UK's net zero target despite giving the green light to Jackdaw.He previously said the decision would \"significantly strengthen our energy security by increasing domestic gas production by about 6% and support the UK's energy supply for more than a decade\".A decision on the larger Rosebank oil project is yet to be made.Image source, EPAImage caption, Protesters gathered outside the Department for Energy Security and Net Zero in Westminster on Wednesday morningThe co-leader of the Scottish Greens, Ross Greer, described the Jackdaw approval as \"climate vandalism\".He said: \"It is a devastating betrayal of our children's future and it won't even lower energy bills in the here and now.\"Environmental campaigners argue that this summer's heatwaves demonstrate the need to tackle climate change by rejecting both Jackdaw and Rosebank.Areeba Hamid, the co-executive director of Greenpeace, said approving Jackdaw was \"the wrong decision\" in terms of lowering energy bills, providing energy security, and addressing climate change.Oxfam said the government had \"drilled straight through the UK's climate commitments\" and accused the prime minister of sending \"the wrong signal as the impacts of climate change intensify\".The charity says the government must commit to not approving any new oil and gas licences, including the proposals for Rosebank.Image caption, Jackdaw's operators said its approval was vital to avoid domestic supply shortagesJackdaw sits close to the boundary between the British and Norwegian sectors of the North Sea, in an area where Westminster originally granted licences to explore for oil and gas in 1970 and 1989.The field was discovered in 2005 but it was expensive and technically difficult to exploit.Shell did not commit to its development until 2022, shortly after the Conservative government had granted consent for production.But in January 2025, after a challenge by environmentalists, the Court of Session in Edinburgh ruled that consent for Jackdaw and Rosebank had been granted unlawfully.The judge, Lord Ericht, said the developers should have provided a more detailed account of the fields' impact on climate change as part of the permitting process.The judge allowed the companies to continue with preparatory work short of producing oil and gas while they crafted new environmental impact assessments taking into account the eventual effect of actually burning the fossil fuels, not just the emissions which would be created in extracting them.On the Front Line: Inside the PlatformAn inside look at Jackdaw, the UK’s newest and most contested gas platform.When BBC News visited the platform this summer, Adura CEO Neil McCulloch said its approval was vital to avoid domestic supply shortages this winter.However, the UK government has previously suggested that Jackdaw will have little impact on energy security and none on reducing domestic gas and electricity bills.In August, in an interim response to a consultation on the future of the country's gas supplies, Energy Minister Michael Shanks said the UK would inevitably become more reliant on imports as domestic production declined, external.\"With diminishing output from the North Sea over the past decades, any gas produced from the UK Continental Shelf represents a tiny fraction of global demand and so will not lower prices nor materially boost supply,\" wrote Shanks.Labour has previously said it is opposed to the issuing of new exploration licences but it will consider new production projects if they are tied back to existing facilities.McCulloch described the Jackdaw decision as \"an important and positive step forward\" - but said attention now turned to Rosebank.He has urged the UK government to approve production from the field, in which Adura owns an 80% stake. The other 20% is held by London-based Ithaca Energy.Rosebank contains an estimated 300 million to 500 million barrels of oil.But while most of Jackdaw's gas is likely to be consumed in the UK, Rosebank's oil will be taken off by tanker and sold on international markets.More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
