      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-16-turingfonts-publish-uncrawable-and-uncopiable-texts",
      "title": "TuringFonts: Publish Uncrawable and Uncopiable Texts",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://jfmdev.github.io/TuringFonts/index.html\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50513238\nPoints: 3\n# Comments: 0",
      "body": "TuringFonts is a simple technique for generate content (for websites or PDF files) that can't we crawled by bots or copied using the clipboard.\nIt's useful in situations in which you want to publish some sensitive content that you don't want to be indexed by search engines like Google or Bing (such as e-mail addresses, telephone numbers or personal names) or when you don't want to allow the user to copy and paste your text (to avoid plagiarism or unauthorized modifications).\nThis technique is based on the simple substitution cipher method and in the use of custom fonts.\nThe simple substitutions cipher in a method of encoding in which each letter is replaced by another one. For example, you could decide to replace each letter by his neighbour in the alphabet (write 'b' instead of 'a', 'c' instead of 'b', etc.), in that case the text \"Hello\" would be write as \"Ifmmp\".\nA font (in computing) is basically a file that indicates how to draw each letter. So, for example, some fonts define that the letter 'O' must be draw rounded while others indicate that must be oval.\nSo basically you encrypt your text using some substitution cipher and then you apply (to that text) a Turing font that inverses the substitution previously made.\nA Turing font is a font whose symbols has been unordered on purpose. So when a letter is found, another letter is draw in his place. For example, a Turing font may indicate to draw an 'a' when encountering a 'b', draw a 'b' when encountering a 'c', etc.).\nIn that's way any bot or computer, that get access to your website or PDF file, is going to read the encrypted text while all humans visitors are going to read the original text.\nThis is what all computers and bots are going to read:\nThis is what all humans are going to see*:\n(*) Try selecting and copying this text into a notepad to see what happens\nHave some doubt? Check the live demo or download the sample PDF file.\nIn order to use this technique, you must follow a two step process:\nEncode you text using some substitution method. You can use this encoder to do it.\nApply, to the previous text, a Turing font. In the download section you can find a list of fonts ready to be used.\nIf you want to use your own substitution alphabets and/or generate your own Turing fonts, I recommend you to read the advanced topics.\nSelect a font, a substitution method and download the corresponding TTF file.\nInstall the downloaded font into your computer.\nEncode your text using the substitution method previously chosen.\nCopy the encoded text into a word processor and apply the font installed.\nVerify that the text can be read and export the document as PDF (indicating that the fonts used must be embedded).\nSelect a font, a substitution method and download the corresponding Zip file. Unzip the file in one of your website's folder.\nDeclare the selected font in the CSS code of the page in which you are going to use it.\nEncode your text using the substitution method previously chosen.\nCopy the encoded text into the page and apply the font.\nVerify that the text can be read.\nIn this section you will find a list of Turing fonts ready to be used.\nEach row of the table contains a font type, and each column indicates the substitution algorithm used for generate the font. For each combination, you can download either a TTF file or a ZIP file (containing the font in all the required formats to be used at the web).\nMore fonts may be added if you gently ask for them.\nAll fonts, scripts and content of this work are licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "negura",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-16T02:19:08.000Z",
      "imageUrl": null,
      "originalUrl": "https://jfmdev.github.io/TuringFonts/index.html",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-16-universal-xss-in-ublock-origin-from-website-driven-element-p",
      "title": "Universal XSS in uBlock Origin from website-driven element picker",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://iter.ca/post/ubo-uxss/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50513160\nPoints: 120\n# Comments: 23",
      "body": "Earlier this month I found a one-click universal XSS issue with uBlock Origin (the MV2 Firefox version, although a similar but less problematic issue also affected the MV3 version, uBO Lite), where a malicious website could trick the extension into adding arbitrary trusted filters. Trusted filters can inject JavaScript code into pages, and so the malicious website could create a trusted filter on another website and run arbitrary JS in the context of any site. This issue is fixed now; you can read the GitHub advisory for some more details.\nGPT-5.6 Sol did most of the hard work of finding the issue. I figured there might be some kind of UXSS from filter injection, so I asked it to take a look, and it found the issue. I then checked and verified the issue it proposed. I wrote all of the prose in this post myself; Claude wrote most of the code for the PoC.\nuBlock Origin has an element picker that you can pull up by right-clicking on the page. You can use this to create a filter rule to block an element on the page without leaving the page.\nThe element picker is implemented by injecting an iframe to a URL like moz-extension://76b2331a-894c-4d25-8b89-fcce37e79cc4/web_accessible_resources/epicker-ui.html. That page can talk to the extension background script and tell it to add filters.\nBasically the attack here is:\nFigure out the element picker URL\nCreate our own invisible (opacity 0) iframe that embeds it\nDrive the picker to put the filter we want in it\nPosition the invisible iframe to trick the user into pressing the invisible “Create” button\nFiguring out the element picker URL\nOn Firefox, the only information we needed to figure out was the extension internal UUID which is random per-installation.\nOn the default setup uBO has a lootdest.*##+js(no-fetch-if, adsbygoogle, war:googlesyndication_adsbygoogle.js) filter rule (from uAssets), which hooks fetch. From inside the fetch hook, it makes an XHR request to moz-extension://<extension UUID>/web_accessible_resources/googlesyndication_adsbygoogle.js and returns the response from that as the fetch body, instead of actually making the request. The filter rule is intended to replace a network request for JS from an ad server with a dummy script that has the correct API but doesn’t do anything.\nBut the attacker website can itself hook the XHR made from inside the no-fetch-if hook to extract the random extension UUID.\nXMLHttpRequest.prototype.open = function(m, url, ...a) {\ne[0] // <- has the extension UUID!\nreturn origOpen.call(this, m, url, ...a);\nThen the attacker website can run fetch('/adsbygoogle.js').catch(() => {}); to trigger the filter, and make their hooked XHR run. Note that there are several ways that the extension internal UUID could be leaked; it’s not just this one filter rule.\nThen you can use that to iframe the element picker. Given an iframe element in the page, you just set its src to `moz-extension://${origin}/web_accessible_resources/epicker-ui.html`.\nNow we can drive the element picker. We can create a MessageChannel and do iframe.contentWindow.postMessage({ what: 'epickerStart' }, '*', [ch.port2]); to send it to the picker iframe. Then we talk to it: it sends us a start message, we send it a showDialog message with a filter and a resultsetDetails message1 to enable the create button and set opt.\nNormally the picker restricts what filters you can enter, and doesn’t let you add scriptlet filters. But there’s a trick we can do to add arbitrary filters. There’s an opt field we set in the second message which is normally the type of network request to match (like image), and gets appended to the filter with a $ if set, like $image. But since we control it, we can put a newline in it and make it \"image\\ngoogle.com##+js(trusted-rpnt, script, ...)\" to inject an arbitrary trusted filter that injects a script.2\nThis is where the main issue is - the element picker shouldn’t accept messages from the attacker. One of the fixes was having a secret password that the real element picker gets from the content script, that the element picker sends in the initial postMessage.\nI didn’t bother actually implementing this, but malicious websites could overlay stuff on top of the embedded element picker or make it invisible by setting the iframe opacity to 0. So as long as the website can get the user to click on the page, it can add arbitrary trusted filters for arbitrary domains.\nFilters added by the element picker are always treated as trusted (at least until a recompile), which is why it can add a trusted filter. If the user later changes their custom filters it would cause a recompile, clearing the filter trust. But in practice the attacker could just immediately open the victim webpage, so this doesn’t help much.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "zaik",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-16T02:04:48.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://iter.ca//ubo-epicker.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://iter.ca/post/ubo-uxss/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-16-suspected-china-spy-linked-to-nvidia-s-ex-boss-left-in-2025-",
      "title": "Suspected China spy linked to Nvidia's ex-boss left in 2025, Megaspeed says",
      "summary": "The Nvidia scandal has raised questions over whether advanced AI chips ended up in China, despite US restrictions.",
      "body": "ByDearbail JordanBusiness reporter and Lily JamaliNorth America Technology correspondentReporting fromPalo Alto, CAAlice Huang, a suspected China spy who has been linked to Nvidia's former boss Jensen Huang, left her executive role at Megaspeed in August last year, the  company says.Jensen Huang resigned as chief executive of the AI chip-making giant Nvidia after it emerged he had allegedly had affairs with Alice Huang as well as with one of his employees.Megaspeed is the biggest buyer of Nvidia chips in South East Asia and the scandal at Nvidia has raised questions about the possibility that high-end AI chips may have ended up in China despite US export restrictions.The Singapore-based company said on Sunday it had launched an internal investigation into its business.Megaspeed said it was aware of reports \"containing assertions about our business, supply chain and global operations that are not supported by verifiable evidence\".It added that it \"conducts legitimate and lawful business\" and has \"retained external legal counsel to conduct a thorough internal investigation\".Alice Huang stepped down from her role as director of Megaspeed near the end of 2023, according to Washington-based security analysis group C4ADS but it said she was still \"practically in control\" of the company.Alice Huang - who is of no relation to Nvidia's former boss - had been investigated by Singapore authorities in February 2025 as part of the country's probe into Megaspeed and other companies.Also on Sunday, US Congressman John Moolenaar said that Jensen Huang had agreed to testify before the House Select Committee on China, which he chairs, as well as the Foreign Affairs and the Intelligence committees.The committees had threatened to issue subpoenas to Jensen Huang if he did not comply with their requests to testify.Moolenaar told CBS, BBC's US partner, that none were ultimately issued. Jensen Huang's testimony is expected to take place behind close doors, with the dates still being finalised. Moolenaar said: \"That's going to be a very important meeting to understand to what degree privileged information was accessed, and to what degree this potential alleged Chinese spy influenced Nvidia's philosophy and may have had an influence on some of our policies with respect to AI and export controls.\" Separately, a group of tech leaders including Space X's Elon Musk, have been trying to rally support to have Jensen Huang reinstated as Nvidia's chief executive.A representative forJensen Huang declined to comment on a report in the Financial Times suggesting that he was discussing a comeback to the firm he co-founded.The BBC understands that the likelihood of him being reinstated is very low while the company's internal investigations are ongoing.The move would also face scrutiny from Washington, with Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent calling for Nvidia to hire its next chief executive from outside the company.For the moment, Nvidia's chief financial officer Colette Kress is leading the firm and will present the company's results on Tuesday, 17 November. On Friday, Bessent blamed the US Commerce Department, led by fellow cabinet colleague Howard Lutnick, for failing to properly oversee the export of high-end chips. Bessent told CNBC that several agencies \"were in favour of putting Megaspeed on the entity list for months... but Commerce has the pen... and you will have to ask them about their internal process\".The so-called entity list restricts US trade with overseas firms and individuals.Lutnick has been called to testify before the Senate Banking Committee next month over his department's role in allowing Nvidia to export its advanced chips.Other congressional committees have asked both Lutnick and his department to retain all records relating to Nvidia and Jensen Huang.Megaspeed is suspected of giving backdoor access to Nvidia chips to Chinese companies via its data centres in Malaysia, Indonesia and Thailand.Nvidia has been banned from selling its advanced semiconductors to China.While Singapore does not prohibit the transfer of advanced AI chips to companies abroad, such moves can breach US export controls.Singapore has said that while it does not enforce \"unilateral export controls imposed by other countries\" it expects all companies in its jurisdiction to respect such regulations.Megaspeed said it \"designs and maintains its global operational and supply-chain control frameworks to comply with all applicable laws and regulatory regimes\". It said this includes \"US export control requirements and local jurisdiction rules in every market where we operate\".China has denied that Alice Huang is a spy and warned the US not to use the scandal as a \"pretext for imposing a technological blockade\" on AI technology.Additional reporting by Osmond Chia",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "tech",
