    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-11-15-jensen-huang-might-be-plotting-a-return-and-more-of-the-late",
      "title": "Jensen Huang might be plotting a return and more of the latest Nvidia drama",
      "summary": "The drama around Nvidia co-founder and former CEO Jensen Huang is evolving quickly. The latest edition of our  Stepback newsletter should get you up to speed, but there have already been a number of new developments since this morning. That includes rumors that Jensen Huang might be trying to find a path back to CEO, and a statement from Megaspeed claiming that Alice Huang hasn't worked for the co",
      "body": "* Some big names, including Elon Musk and Epic Games CEO Tim Sweeney, have publicly thrown their support behind a public letter demanding Nvidia “bring Jensen back.”\n* The public letter calling for Jensen Huang to be reinstated has over 150,000 signatures, but it’s difficult to verify their authenticity. Business Insider reports that signatures include “Tim Cruise, Hollywood actor” and “Xi Jingping,” likely a misspelling of Chinese president Xi Jinping.\n* The Financial Times reports that Jensen Huang has discussed the possibility of returning as CEO with close allies, as some Silicon Valley insiders and employees attempt to pressure the board.\n* Not all believe Jensen Huang should be allowed to return to his seat. According to the Financial Times, one ally acknowledged that, “Having an affair with a subordinate is a fireable offense.”\n* 23andMe founder Anne Wojcicki told Bloomberg that “Power corrupts, and people start to think they are invincible … We are all complicit in making these people think they are gods. And people do stupid things when they think they are untouchable.”\n* Wojcicki endured a messy public split from Google co-founder Sergey Brin, as their separation in 2013 became public along with the news that he was romantically involved with a Google employee, before their divorce settlement was finalized two years later.\n* According to Bloomberg, many women in Silicon Valley see Jensen Huang as simply another example of a rich man abusing their position and “leveraging power to pursue sexual relationships with their employees.”\n* Megaspeed released a statement saying that Alice Huang left the company “effective Aug. 30, 2025, and currently has no role with the company,” adding that “Megaspeed does not comment on Ms. Huang’s personal and private matters.”\nCorrection, November 15th: A previous version of this article described Brin’s reported relationship with a Google employee as an affair, updated to clarify that it was reported after he and Wojcicki separated.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "AI",
        "News",
        "Nvidia",
        "Policy",
        "Politics",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Terrence O’Brien",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-15T20:43:49.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/11/Nvidia_Jensen_huang_03.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,12.339110464558,100,75.321779070884",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/1033999/jensen-huang-return-nvidia-megaspeed",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-15-vulcan-rocket-fails-during-return-to-flight-launch-40-amazon",
      "title": "Vulcan rocket fails during return-to-flight launch, 40 Amazon satellites lost",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.space.com/space-exploration/launches-spacecraft/ula-vulcan-centaur-return-to-flight-amazon-leo-internet-satellite-launch\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50510442\nPoints: 45\n# Comments: 12",
      "body": "United Launch Alliance's Vulcan Centaur rocket suffered an anomaly during its launch today (Nov. 15), resulting in the loss of 40 Amazon internet satellites.The Vulcan Centaur lifted off from Florida's Cape Canaveral Space Force Station today at 12:40 p.m. ET (1740 GMT), hauling 40 satellites for the Amazon Leo broadband constellation toward low Earth orbit (LEO). But they never got there.\"Based on data received by our Launch Director, we confirm that the Vulcan vehicle launched from Space Launch Complex 41 suffered an anomaly 10 minutes into the flight and failed to deliver the Amazon mission to orbit,\" ULA said in an update on X today, 50 minutes after launch. \"Since the anomaly just occurred, we don’t have any specific data yet to understand what occurred today.\"\nA United Launch Alliance Vulcan Centaur launches 40 Amazon internet satellites from Florida on Nov. 15, 2026. (Image credit: ULA)Today's launch was supposed to be a bounce-back mission for the Vulcan Centaur, the successor to ULA's workhorse Atlas V rocket.Vulcan debuted in January 2024 and had four total flights under its belt before today. On two of those missions — its second, in October 2024, and its fourth, in February of this year — the rocket experienced an issue with one of its solid rocket boosters (SRBs).In both cases, an SRB nozzle burned through and fell off about 30 seconds after launch. Vulcan Centaur overcame the problem on both launches, successfully delivering its payloads to their designated orbits. But the U.S. Space Force was concerned enough that it decided to pause national security launches on Vulcan until ULA addressed the problem. (Vulcan's third and fourth launches were Space Force missions.)ULA worked hard on that front and got Vulcan ready to return to flight, which finally occurred today after nine months..\"The integrated team has made great strides in improving our understanding of the SRB anomaly that occurred on the previous Vulcan flight and have identified several potential contributors, which have been mitigated through design and process improvements to the nozzle,\" the company said in an Oct. 28 update.That nozzle work apparently paid off: The SRBs appeared to perform as expected during today's launch. But a different, more serious problem cropped up today, and Vulcan Centaur could not overcome it.\nThe Vulcan Centaur rises into the sky on Nov. 15, 2026. (Image credit: Vulcan Centaur)Today's launch was supposed to help build out Amazon's broadband constellation in LEO, which was previously called Project Kuiper but is now known as Amazon Leo. It will eventually compete with SpaceX's Starlink network, which has been up and running for several years.Amazon Leo will consist of more than 3,200 satellites. Before today, about 400 of them had reached space on 15 launches, which were conducted by three different rockets — the Atlas V, SpaceX's Falcon 9 and Arianespace's Ariane 6. Today's launch was the first such mission for the Vulcan Centaur, which explains ULA's name for the launch — Vulcan Leo 1.The Amazon Leo launch was part of a very busy stretch in spaceflight. SpaceX launched its Starship megarocket on Friday (Nov. 13), on a test mission that featured the first-ever catch of the vehicle's Ship upper stage. And Rocket Lab launched its 100th-ever mission early Saturday morning (Nov. 14), sending a private Japanese Earth-observing satellite to orbit.Editor's note: This story was updated at 12:50 p.m. ET on Nov. 15 with news of liftoff, then again at 1:45 p.m. ET with news of the anomaly and loss of the mission.\nMichael Wall is the Spaceflight and Tech Editor for Space.com and joined the team in 2010. He primarily covers human and robotic spaceflight, military space, and exoplanets, but has been known to dabble in the space art beat. His book about the search for alien life, \"Out There,\" was published on Nov. 13, 2018. Before becoming a science writer, Michael worked as a herpetologist and wildlife biologist. He has a Ph.D. in evolutionary biology from the University of Sydney, Australia, a bachelor's degree from the University of Arizona, and a graduate certificate in science writing from the University of California, Santa Cruz. To find out what his latest project is, you can follow Michael on Twitter.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "jonbaer",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-15T20:37:28.000Z",
