      "id": "techcrunch-2026-11-14-transportation-secretary-blames-routine-equipment-upgrade-fo",
      "title": "Transportation secretary blames ‘routine’ equipment upgrade for grounding flights nationwide",
      "summary": "FAA administrator Bryan Bedford also said there's \"zero evidence of anything AI-related.\"",
      "body": "Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy said Saturday that a nationwide ground stop halting flights across the United States was caused by seemingly routine maintenance work.\nDuffy said that during “routine maintenance,” copper wire at a remote location was upgraded to fiber optic cable by L3Harris, a longtime contractor for the Federal Aviation Administration. After the upgrade, a router overloaded a critical communication system, leading to the ground stop.\n“It’s pretty straightforward work that we were doing yesterday at, again, a very remote location,” Duffy said during a Saturday press conference that appeared to have technical issues of its own. “What we’re working to determine was whether we had incompetence — meaning, was it user error or [installer] error from L3Harris? Or did they install a faulty router?”\nThe FAA’s acting CTO Rebecca Guy added, “When we started to see the occurrence where our network was having instability, we started to look at what was the cause. And as such, we saw two places that were actually pinging our network at a very high rate — a thousand connections over every four to five seconds.”\nGuy said the FAA disabled “both of those, right away and were able to stabilize our network,” and is now performing a root cause analysis.\nDuffy has repeatedly insisted, at multiple press conferences, that Congress needs to provide more funding for aviation equipment upgrades. Asked why the upgrades were made at 3pm on a Friday afternoon — rather than, say, at midnight on a Saturday — he responded that while “switchovers” at bigger airports will take place late at night, remote switchovers happen during the day.\n“There will be reevaluation of when we do this work,” he said.\nThis was only the third nationwide ground stop of the 21st century. More than 8,200 flights were canceled on Friday evening, according to aviation data firm Cirium. And the effects continued after the ground stop was lifted, with at least  another 3,000 flights canceled on Saturday.\nThe ground stop came just a day after the FAA announced the rollout of an AI tool for air traffic controllers; the air traffic controllers union said it was not consulted in designing the system. However, FAA administrator Bryan Bedford said the agency sees “zero evidence of anything nefarious and zero evidence of anything AI-related.”\n“We want to be clear that we have an equipment issue, and we’re working through what this defective piece of equipment was,” Bedford said.\nWhen Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin was asked earlier if the ground stop was caused by AI, he replied, “We call it SI [super intelligence] around here, remember? SI.”\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nAnthony Ha is TechCrunch’s weekend editor. Previously, he worked as a tech reporter at Adweek, a senior editor at VentureBeat, a local government reporter at the Hollister Free Lance, and vice president of content at a VC firm. He lives in New York City.\nYou can contact or verify outreach from Anthony by emailing [email protected].",
