      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-14-lovable-apps-expose-passport-details-and-more-than-200k-priv",
      "title": "Lovable apps expose passport details and more than 200K private messages",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.crissyfield.de/research/lovable-en/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50499017\nPoints: 876\n# Comments: 339",
      "body": "Lovable: Many Apps Built on the Platform Expose Sensitive DataAI makes it easier to build your own web applications, but handling sensitive data still requires special\ncare. We examined 159,041 Lovable projects and found many cases where personal and confidential information was\naccessible without logging in.Diesen Artikel auf Deutsch lesenAbout LovableLovable is a Swedish AI startup founded in Stockholm in 2023 by Anton Osika and Fabian Hedin. The platform turns\nplain-language descriptions into web applications and is designed to be accessible even to people with little\nor no programming experience.Lovable grew out of Osika’s open-source project “GPT Engineer”, released in 2023. The project evolved into the\nGPT Engineer App before being renamed Lovable in late 2024 and launching publicly that November.Visit the Lovable websiteThe PromiseLovable can build impressively sophisticated web projects with AI, from static websites to full applications\nwith user management and database connections. But as soon as an application handles confidential information,\none question becomes crucial: Is that data protected against unauthorized access?In its marketing, Lovable\nemphasizes that no technical knowledge is needed to build\nprojects. This can give the impression that the platform takes care of security\ntoo. Yet Lovable’s Terms & Conditions place responsibility for applications and data handling on the\ncustomers themselves.That leaves a troubling gap between the promise of an easy-to-use, all-in-one service and the responsibilities\ncustomers are expected to take on.Against this backdrop, we investigated how well sensitive data in Lovable applications is protected, in\ncollaboration with Handelsblatt. Lina Knees reported on the findings on\nGerman).Research QuestionWe wanted to find out whether sensitive data in Lovable applications was accessible without logging in. We\nlooked separately at data sent when a website first loaded and additional content we checked by making further\nread-only requests with the same API key.The result: We found many clear examples of sensitive data left unprotected.MethodologyWe focused on the database service Supabase. Its integration is relatively easy to\nanalyze, and we believe it is the most widely used database service among Lovable projects. Lovable’s own\ndatabase features, introduced in late 2025, are still less widely used. Similar misconfigurations may occur\nthere too, but we did not examine them in this analysis.Using datasets from the open web archive Common Crawl, we identified and analyzed\n159,041 Lovable projects from December 2025 to September 2026. Of these, 34,545 used Supabase.24,664 websites sent database records from Supabase as soon as they loaded, without requiring a login or any\nother interaction. That alone is not a security issue: the data may be intended for public display. But by\nmaking additional read-only requests, we were able to retrieve more database content. We found records\ncontaining email addresses in 10,560 tables across 7,403 of the 24,664 websites (30%).LimitationsEmail addresses can help identify records containing personal data, but they may also be public contact\naddresses or test data. At the same time, this approach misses personal records that contain no email address.\nThese effects partly offset one another, though we could not establish to what extent. The 30% figure should\ntherefore not be read as a confirmed data leak rate. It applies only to the 24,664 websites we examined more\nclosely. Our sample, drawn from Common Crawl, is also not representative of all Lovable applications.We retrieved only parts of the datasets rather than downloading them in full. The figures below reflect the\nrecords we actually downloaded; the affected datasets may contain more. We are not publishing details of how to\naccess them, as doing so could make further access easier.Data LeaksWe found many serious cases of exposed data, including:A German lifestyle forum exposed more than 80,000 profiles containing full names, email addresses, dates\nof birth, and gender information. More than 200,000 chat messages were also accessible.Several event organizers and restaurants exposed tens of thousands of records relating to ticket and food\nsales, with no login required. The records we inspected included email addresses, phone numbers, postal\naddresses, payment information, and ticket codes.A property management company exposed AI-generated summaries of internal emails containing sensitive\ninformation about tenants, property owners, and business partners.In a particularly serious case, a service provider exposed more than 1,000 records containing identity\ndocument details, including passport numbers, expiration dates, and nationalities. These highly\nsensitive identity details could be used for identity theft.Other accessible content included tens of thousands of contracts, invoices, personal photo albums,\ncase, 280,000 emails sent to a provider’s customers were accessible.Depending on what it contains, exposed data like this could be used for phishing, ticket fraud, or identity\ntheft. We could not establish whether any of it had been misused.Handelsblatt contacted Lovable with the findings of our investigation.ImplicationsOur analysis shows that many Lovable applications leave even highly sensitive data unprotected, with potentially\nserious consequences for the people affected.Access control mistakes can happen in any application, whether written by people or built with AI. With Lovable,\ndatabase integrations follow recurring patterns, making it easier for attackers to look for similar\nmisconfigurations across many applications. An approach that works against one app may then work against others\nwith little extra effort. Users without a technical background also face a harder task spotting and preventing\nthese flaws.An easy-to-use development platform does not remove the need for an expert review of access permissions before an\napplication handles confidential data.Automated checks are no substitute for a professional security assessment.Before an application handles real customer or business data, its access permissions, interfaces, and separation\nbetween user accounts should be reviewed. These checks should be repeated after significant changes. If the\nnecessary expertise is not available in-house, bring in qualified support.If you would like help securing your application, please get in touch.Note: This research report presents a more recent analysis and updated figures compared with the Handelsblatt\narticle published on November 2, 2026.Get in touch for an informal conversation. We'll look at your current situation and work with you to\nfind the right solutions.Contact an expert",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "armcat",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-14T17:43:10.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://www.crissyfield.de/images/research-lovable-opengraph.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.crissyfield.de/research/lovable-en/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 5,
