      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-14-unsloth-qwen3-8-27b-quantizations-dynamic-v3-1-update",
      "title": "Unsloth Qwen3.8-27B Quantizations – Dynamic v3.1 Update",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://unsloth.ai/docs/models/qwen3.8\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50491980\nPoints: 171\n# Comments: 50",
      "body": "Skip to contentFor the complete documentation index, see llms.txt. This page is also available as Markdown.ModelsQwen3.8 - How to Run LocallyGuide to running Qwen3.8 quants including Qwen3.8-27B on your local setup.Qwen3.8-27B is a 27B parameter dense model Qwen, featuring vision and reasoning capabilities, a 256K context window, and runs locally on 17GB RAM/VRAM setups. Qwen3.8 excels at agentic coding, vision and chat tasks, and can now run via Unsloth GGUFs, NVFP4 and Unsloth Desktop.Nov 11 Update: Qwen3.8-27B GGUFs now use Unsloth Dynamic V3.1 which improves average accuracy by 5-10% accuracy at comparable sizes including Terminal Bench, DeepSWE and more benchmarks. We're working on an even better version of Dynamic quants which will be applied to Qwen4 and future models.Run Qwen3.8 GuideDownload UnslothThank you Qwen for day zero access. Unsloth quants also include:Developer Role Support for agentic tools like CodexTool calling: Improved parsing nested objects to make tools succeed moreDynamic 4-bit Qwen3.8-27B in Unsloth DesktopDynamic V3.1 Update (November 11th 2026)We're excited to release new Qwen3.8-27B GGUFs using our updated Unsloth Dynamic V3.1 (preview) algorithm which improves Unsloth quants by 5-10% accuracy at the same size on most benchmarks and is the best in class for most tasks including coding, agentic workloads and general chat.Most quants from 2bit until 6bit are updated. We found 3bit UD3 quants to do on par with UD3.1, so we do not update them. We are actively working on making all quants better!We ran agentic benchmarks including Terminal Bench 2.1, DeepSWE v1.1, and general benchmarks like LiveCodeBench v6, GPQA Diamond, MMLU-Pro, AIME25 and many others. We used 256K context lengths for all agentic benchmarks, and 128K for the rest. We followed the same methodology for Qwen3.8-27B as DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash and Qwen3.8-Flash-Next which also utilized our V3.1 preview algorithm.Dynamic V3.1 Full BreakdownClick below to see full details of benchmarking and more:Click to expand Full details and breakdownExllama-v3 considerationsTo compare GSQ-RCO and Exllama-v3, we had to exclude the token embedding layer from the x axis, as Exllama-v3 uses BF16 for it.If you consider the full download size and total RAM usage for Exllama-v3, then adding the BF16 embedding table and including all embedding sizes changes the plots. Our main plots's x axis removes the embedding layer sizes from all quant providers. However on pure RAM usage and disk space usage vs accuracy, the below plot is the true reflection of actual usage.We have not investigated why Exllama-v3 keeps token embeddings in BF16, but as the lm_head is still quantized heavily, the embedding layer might have \"absorbed\" most of the quantization damage from it. We would be glad to benchmark new Exllama-v3 non BF16 token embedding quants like using 8-bit for example, and see if benchmarks change.GSQ-RCO results are misleadingFor LiveCodeBench, GSQ-RCO showed Unsloth UD3 quants doing consistently worse than ISTA's. However we found the opposite - UD3 quants (our OLD quants) do better than ISTA on LCB on all sizes except the smallest. UD3.1 further improves on LCB as well.We approximate that GSQ-RCO used a context budget of 96K, as that best fits the data for the runs we have. At 96K, GSQ-RCO does somewhat do better than UD3, but at 128K, UD3 does much better.On another benchmark, for ISTA GSQ-RCO's AIME25 benchmarks, their quants reach 100%, 100%, 100% and 96.7% accuracy recovery. And they said our quants do worse.Through out investigations, we estimate ISTA only did 1 trial - we did 14 trials, and we show on average the recovery rate is 93% to 96% - not 100% accuracy recovery. And in fact our UD3 old quants do BETTER on average than their quants on 14 runs vs 1 run of theirs, so we draw the opposite conclusion if you run the trials more.We also checked and graded our answers using Math Verify Dynamic 3.1 GGUFs and also LightEval and we find exactly the same conclusions. We also tried greedy decoding (temperature of 0), and this could not get the 100% accuracy recovery ISTA found across multiple greedy runs, as GPUs themselves are non deterministic. That is why you benchmarks should be run multiple times. Greedy decoding is also not representative of real world use cases.We also checked 96K (which is most likely ISTA's budget) and 128K, and our quants still do on average better.KLD of Unsloth v3.1 vs v3We did KLD for multiple domains for Unsloth UD3.1 quants and UD3 quants - we reduce KLD throughout most quants and most domains - we did not replace the 3-bit ones, as we found UD3 to do mostly great, and the accuracy recovery on benchmarks was not significant enough with UD3.1 to warrant an update, so we did not update them.Benchmark details and setupsWe follow the same procedure for all benchmarks as for Qwen3.8-Flash-Next, DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash as well as discussed in Dynamic 3.1 GGUFs. We used temperature 1.0, top_p 0.95, top_k 20, min_p 0, no presence or repetition penalty, preserve_thinking and xhigh reasoning for Qwen3.8-27B.⚙️ Usage GuideQwen3.8-27B Requirements:Qwen3.8-27B 4-bit quants work on 16-19GB VRAM like RTX 5080, 4090 or a Mac with 24GB RAM.\nTable: Hardware requirements (units = total memory: RAM + VRAM, or unified memory)If you want to use MTP for faster inference, prepare to have 1-2GB extra headroom.Recommended SettingsQwen3.8-27B is a hybrid thinking model with different default settings for thinking and non-thinking modes. Extra high is enabled by default so if you want shorter thinking traces, you can adjust the thinking effort:Instruct (non-thinking) ModeMaximum context window: 262,144 (can be extended to 1M via YaRN)Thinking Mode: temperature=1.0, top_p=0.95, top_k=20, min_p=0.0, presence_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0Instruct (or non-thinking) mode: temperature=0.7, top_p=0.80, top_k=20, min_p=0.0, presence_penalty=1.5, repetition_penalty=1.0💡 Thinking + Preserve ThinkingQwen3.8 has Preserve Thinking which leaves the thinking trace from the previous conversation. This increases the number of tokens you use, but could increase accuracy in continued conversations. Unsloth has 'Think' and Preserved Thinking toggles for Qwen3.8 (see right):Qwen3.8-27B comes with support for reasoning_effort, which can be used to adjust reasoning depth and control cost. These toggles are automatically enabled in Unsloth:xhigh (default): for complex tasks demanding thorough analysismedium: balancing accuracy and speedlow: efficient reasoning optimizing for speed and costTo change thinking / reasoning effort in unsloth run or llama-server, use --chat-template-kwargs '{\"reasoning_effort\":\"medium\"}'If you're on Windows Powershell, use: --chat-template-kwargs \"{\\\"reasoning_effort\\\":\\\"medium\\\"}\"Change medium to your desired reasoning level.Run Qwen3.8 GuideYou can now run Qwen3.8-27B in llama.cpp and Unsloth Desktop. Feel free to change quantization type.Run in Unsloth DesktopRun in llama.cppNVFP4 GuideQwen3.8-2.4T-A95B Guide🦥 Run Qwen3.8 in Unsloth DesktopQwen3.8 can run in Unsloth Desktop, an open-source UI app for local AI. Unsloth automatically offloads to RAM and detects multiGPU setups. With Unsloth Desktop, you can run models locally on MacOS, Windows, Linux and:Search, download, run GGUFs, MLX and safetensor modelsFast CPU + GPU inference via MLX and llama.cppSearch and download Qwen3.8Go to Unsloth Chat or Model hub and search for Qwen3.8 in the search bar and download your desired model and quant.Run Qwen3.8Inference parameters should be auto-set when using Unsloth, however you can still change it manually. You can also edit the context length, chat template and other settings.For example using Unsloth Desktop with the 397GB Qwen3.8 (-91% smaller) allows you to toggle thinking modes, allow inline canvas, web search and code execution and much more.Dynamic 1-bit 397GB 91% smaller GGUF of Qwen3.8 2.4T in Unsloth DesktopServe Qwen3.8 with Unsloth APIYou can use unsloth run command and serve Qwen3.8 via an API using llama-server runtime flags, including context sizing, GPU layers, threading, sampling, networking, and tool configuration. For more info see our API docs or unsloth start.Unsloth is now readyYou can also do many other things with Qwen3.8 via Unsloth Desktop like:Train models: Fine-tune text, diffusion, embedding, and moreGenerate media: Create and train images, video, TTS locally🦙 Run Qwen3.8 in llama.cppDownload the latest version of llama.cpp. You can follow the build instructions below as well. Change -DGGML_CUDA=ON to -DGGML_CUDA=OFF if you don't have a GPU or just want CPU inference. For Apple Mac / Metal devices, set -DGGML_CUDA=OFF then continue as usual - Metal support is on by default.To run the model in llama-cli, follow the code snippets below:\nRemember to change settings according to your use-case.⚡️NVFP4Like Qwen3.6, we’re also releasing new dynamic NVFP4 Qwen3.8-27B quants that run ~1.5× faster than BF16 checkpoints, with better performance and comparable file sizes. Run Qwen3.8-27B NVFP4 1.5x faster on 24GB VRAM. We also added FP8 KV cache calibration for 2x longer context lengths! NVFP4 requires NVIDIA's Blackwell GPUs like RTX 50X, DGX Spark (see Qwen3.8-27B), B200, B300 GPUs. For older GPUs, our GGUFs work well! You can run NVFP4 quants in vLLM only for now (SGLang is not supported).See below for previous benchmarks conducted for Qwen3.6 as well comparing to other NVFP4 implementations which use 16bit activations vs our NVFP4 activations:All benchmarks use 1x B200 128 concurrency. Higher concurrency can boost 35B to 17,561 tokens / s. For accuracy benchmarks we ran KLD and Top-1 agreement over Code, Chat and many domains. NVFP4 is consistency 92% to 97% accuracy recovery vs BF16For accuracy benchmarks For Qwen 3.6, we conducted MMLU-Pro, AIME 2025, GPQA for FP8, BF16, NVIDIA's NVFP4 and our NVFP4s - we show our faster quants do similarly on all:To run NVFP4 quants, see below for commands to run Qwen3.8-27B in vLLM or SGLang:vLLM:To install vLLM in a separate venv:Then to serve the 27B variant:To enable MTP / speculative decoding (faster decode but somewhat less throughput), use:If you get Torchcodec issues, be sure to do the below then relaunch vllm.SGLang:If using SGLang, you must use SGLang version v0.5.19 otherwise it will not work since we quantize the lm_head to FP8.vLLM has a CompressedTensorsW8A8Fp8 kernel which supports this, whilst SGLang v0.5.19 cannot load the FP8 lm_head. v0.5.19 works now!For sglang after installing the latest:🤯Quantization AnalysisNVFP4 quants are 1.5x faster than BF16 and retains 92 to 97% top-1 accuracy.Top-1 accuracy plot as shown in UD-3 fro Qwen3.8-27B:And mean KLD for Qwen3.8:📊 BenchmarksQwen3.8-27BSee further below for table benchmarks:Text PerformanceClick to expand full tableAgentic terminal codingTerminal Bench 2.1 (Terminus)Agentic codingSWE-bench ProRepo-level code generationNL2Repo-BenchAgentic codingDeepSWE 1.1Software engineeringQwenSWEBenchLong-horizon office workCoWorkBenchProfessional job tasksJobBenchFrontier agentic tasksAgents' Last ExamInstruction followingIFBenchScientific reasoningGPQA DiamondMultidisciplinary reasoningHLECompetitive codingLiveCodeBench v6Qwen3.8-2.4T-A95BQwen3.8-2.4T-A95B is a 2.4T parameter (95B active) model with rivaling GPT-5.6 Sol. Full-precision Qwen3.8-2.4T-A95B requires 4.9TB of storage and 1-bit Unsloth Dynamic GGUFs takes 397GB (91% smaller), and larger IQ1_S takes 508GB.⚙️ Usage GuideQwen3.8-2.4T Requirements:Recommended SettingsQwen3.8-2.4T is thinking-only, while Qwen3.8-Max is hybrid.Context length = up to 1,010,000temperature=1.0, top_p=0.95, top_k=20, min_p=0.0, presence_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0If the model fits, you will get ~20 tokens/s generation when using B200s and >120 tokens / s throughput. Best rule of thumb: RAM+VRAM ≈ the quant size; otherwise it’ll still work, just much slower due to disk offloading.Run Qwen3.8-2.4T GuideYou can now run Qwen3.8 in llama.cpp and Unsloth Desktop. For the large Qwen3.8-2.T model, we will be utilizing the 397GB IQ1_XXXS quant (named Q1_0) for best results in terms of accessibility and accuracy and it will require at least 450GB RAM. Feel free to change quantization type.Run in Unsloth DesktopRun in llama.cpp🦥 Run in Unsloth DesktopQwen3.8 can run in Unsloth Desktop, an open-source UI app for local AI. Unsloth automatically offloads to RAM and detects multiGPU setups. With Unsloth Desktop, you can run models locally on MacOS, Windows, Linux.\nGo to Unsloth Chat or Model hub and search for Qwen3.8 in the search bar and download your desired model and quant. Then run it.Qwen3.8-2.4T-A95B New 1-bit data-typesWe extended IQ1_S in llama.cpp which is 1.5625 bits per weight to 1.1875 bpw by reducing the number of entries in the codebook - we found this works well for large models, and can still retain a lot of accuracy - we also found these new data-types to be fine for post training quantization (PTQ) without the need for QAT or QAD (quantization aware training / distillation). Qwen3.8-2.4T-A95B-GGUFClick for detailed analysisDue to naming issues, we used TQ2_0, TQ1_0 and Q1_0 otherwise it won't pop up in the HF repo.We are still running benchmarks for the new data-types, but for other large models, we get good results without any QAT / QAD:🦙 Run Qwen3.8-2.4T in llama.cppWe need to use the specific IQ1_XXXS branch here. You can follow the build instructions below as well. Change -DGGML_CUDA=ON to -DGGML_CUDA=OFF if you don't have a GPU or just want CPU inference. For Apple Mac / Metal devices, set -DGGML_CUDA=OFF then continue as usual - Metal support is on by default.Download the model via (after installing pip install huggingface_hub). You can choose Q1_0 for IQ1_XXXS or other quantized versions like Q8_0 . If downloads get stuck, see: Hugging Face Hub, XET debuggingTo run the model in llama-cli, follow the code snippets below. Remember to change settings according to your use-case.To run the general UD-IQ1_S you can do:Qwen3.8-2.4T BenchmarksDynamic V3.1 Update (November 11th 2026)⚙️ Usage GuideRecommended Settings💡 Thinking + Preserve ThinkingRun Qwen3.8 Guide🦥 Run Qwen3.8 in Unsloth Desktop🦙 Run Qwen3.8 in llama.cpp⚡️NVFP4🤯Quantization Analysis📊 BenchmarksQwen3.8-2.4T-A95B⚙️ Usage GuideRecommended SettingsRun Qwen3.8-2.4T Guide🦥 Run in Unsloth DesktopQwen3.8-2.4T-A95B New 1-bit data-types🦙 Run Qwen3.8-2.4T in llama.cppcurl -fsSL https://unsloth.ai/install.sh | shirm https://unsloth.ai/install.ps1 | iexbrew install --cask unslothbrew upgrade --cask unslothwinget install --id UnslothAI.Unsloth -ewinget upgrade --id UnslothAI.Unsloth -eunsloth run --model unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-GGUF:UD-Q4_K_XL\napt-get install pciutils build-essential cmake curl libcurl4-openssl-dev -y\ncmake llama.cpp -B llama.cpp/build \\\ncmake --build llama.cpp/build --config Release -j --clean-first --target llama-cli llama-mtmd-cli llama-server llama-gguf-split\ncp llama.cpp/build/bin/llama-* llama.cpppip install -U \"huggingface_hub[cli]\"\n--include \"*UD-Q4_K_XL*\" # Use \"*UD-Q3_K_XL*\" for 3-bit./llama.cpp/llama-cli \\\n--min-p 0.0uv venv unsloth-nvfp4-env --python 3.13\nuv pip install \"vllm>=0.25.0\" \"flashinfer-python>=0.6.13\" \"nvidia-cutlass-dsl>=4.5.2\" \\\n--torch-backend=autovllm serve unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-NVFP4vllm serve unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-NVFP4\n--speculative-config '{\"method\": \"mtp\", \"num_speculative_tokens\": 2}'sudo apt-get update\nsudo apt-get install -y ffmpeguv pip install sglang sglang-kernel \\\n--index-strategy unsafe-best-matchsglang serve unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-NVFP4sglang serve unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-NVFP4 --speculative-algorithm EAGLE \\\n--speculative-num-steps 3 --speculative-eagle-topk 1  --speculative-num-draft-tokens 4apt-get update\napt-get install pciutils build-essential cmake curl libcurl4-openssl-dev -y\ngit clone --branch iq1-narrow https://github.com/unslothai/llama.cpp\ncmake llama.cpp -B llama.cpp/build \\\ncmake --build llama.cpp/build --config Release -j --clean-first --target llama-cli llama-mtmd-cli llama-server llama-gguf-split\ncp llama.cpp/build/bin/llama-* llama.cpppip install -U \"huggingface_hub[cli]\"\n--include \"*Q1_0*\" # Use \"*IQ2_XXS*\" for 2-bit./llama.cpp/llama-cli \\\n--min-p 0.0pip install -U \"huggingface_hub[cli]\"\n--include \"*IQ1_S*\" # Use \"*IQ2_XXS*\" for 2-bit./llama.cpp/llama-cli \\",
