    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-13-home-assistant-the-password-is-not-the-key-token-secrets-in-",
      "title": "Home Assistant: the password is not the key – token secrets in cleartext",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://seclists.org/fulldisclosure/2026/Nov/34\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50490967\nPoints: 3\n# Comments: 0",
      "body": "From: theknell--- via Fulldisclosure <fulldisclosure () seclists org>\nDate: Sun, 08 Nov 2026 11:02:24 -0500\nHome Assistant's documentation tells users: \"Your password is the key\nto your home, so choose a strong, unique one.\" Home Assistant is\ninstalled in two million homes by the vendor's own published count,\nand in those homes it holds the locks, the alarm, the garage, the\ncameras, and the record of who is home.\nThe password is not the key. Tested against home-assistant/core\n2026.11.0 (frontend 20261028.4, hass-nabucasa 2.8.1):\n1. Every refresh token's secret and its per-token JWT signing key,\nfor every user, is serialized in cleartext in one file:\nconfig/.storage/auth. Passwords in the neighbouring store are\nbcrypt-hashed; token secrets are not hashed at all — validation\nis a direct string comparison against the stored value.\n2. Two of the three token classes never expire. Long-lived and\nsystem refresh tokens are created with expire_at = None; the\nlong-lived access tokens the documentation recommends for scripts\nare valid for ten years, and the core API accepts a caller-supplied\nlifespan in days with no upper bound.\n3. Changing the password revokes none of them. All four\npassword-change entry points — self-service, administrator,\nSupervisor reset, command line — rehash the password record and\n4. The automatic-backup password is persisted in plaintext in\n.storage/backup, in the same directory every backup archives —\nand every settings backup carries .storage/auth inside it, so a\nLive proof, against our own instance only: after a real, verified\npassword change, a refresh token copied from .storage/auth beforehand\nstill minted fresh access, and a ten-year long-lived token minted\nScope, stated plainly: this is not an unauthenticated network break.\nIt begins with a read of the config directory or a backup. What it\nshows is that any such read — a backup file shared for support, a\nconfig folder synced somewhere it should not be, one file disclosure\nanywhere in the stack — becomes durable, password-change-resistant\naccess to the home, and the one remediation every user reaches for\nfirst, changing the password, does nothing.\nEleven documented claims were checked: 3 hold, 5 hold with a bound,\n3 fall. What is sound is credited in the paper at the same weight:\nthe token generation entropy is impeccable, and access tokens live\nVerify: gpg --import knell-public.asc, then\nMore, signed and timestamped: https://github.com/the-knell\nSent through the Full Disclosure mailing list\nWeb Archives & RSS: https://seclists.org/fulldisclosure/\nHome Assistant: the password is not the key — token secrets in cleartext, two token classes never expire, password change revokes none (2026.11.0, live-proven) theknell--- via Fulldisclosure (Nov 12)",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "atriix",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T22:30:10.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://seclists.org/images/fulldisclosure-img.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://seclists.org/fulldisclosure/2026/Nov/34",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-13-national-weather-service-reports-nationwide-radar-website-ou",
      "title": "National Weather Service reports nationwide radar website outage amid FAA stop",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.news9.com/oklahoma-city-news/national-weather-service-reports-nationwide-radar-website-outage\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50490778\nPoints: 32\n# Comments: 4",
      "body": "OKLAHOMA -The National Weather Service reported nationwide internet and communication issues on Friday, Nov. 13, warning that some weather products may be unavailable or outdated on its websites.In a notice posted on its website, the agency said technicians are working to resolve the disruption as quickly as possible.“The NWS has been experiencing nation-wide internet and communications issues. You may not be able to access products on our webpages, or these products will be old. Technicians are actively working to resolve these issues as soon as possible,” the notice said.The agency said local National Weather Service offices continue to issue watches, warnings and forecasts, which remain available through some external websites.The NWS apologized for the inconvenience but did not provide a timeline for restoring normal website operations.\nWhat National Weather Service services are affected?\nThe nationwide disruption is affecting access to weather information on National Weather Service websites, including potentially outdated forecasts and other weather products.The agency previously acknowledged an outage affecting its online radar platform, radar.weather.gov.It remains unclear whether the problems are limited to online access and communications systems or are affecting other National Weather Service operations.Despite the disruption, the agency said local offices are continuing to issue weather alerts and forecasts.\nOutage reported alongside nationwide FAA ground stop\nThe National Weather Service issues were reported Friday as the Federal Aviation Administration separately ordered a nationwide ground stop affecting departing flights across the country.The FAA said communication issues affecting Air Route Traffic Control Centers prompted the ground stop, which took effect at 2:54 p.m. Central Time.READ MORE: FAA orders nationwide ground stop until 10 p.m. following communication issueThe FAA’s advisory listed an estimated end time of 10 p.m. Central Time, although officials had not confirmed when flights would resume.There is no confirmed connection between the National Weather Service’s communication issues and the FAA’s ground stop.\nWhen will National Weather Service websites be restored?\nThe National Weather Service has not announced when full access to its websites and weather products will be restored.Technicians are actively working to resolve the nationwide issues, according to the agency.Local National Weather Service offices continue to issue watches, warnings and forecasts during the disruption.Users can check weather.gov and radar.weather.gov for updates as service is restored.\nCassidy Mudd joined the News 9 team in June 2026 as a digital producer and is excited to tell the stories that matter most to Oklahomans.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "galleywest200",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T22:16:11.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://cdn.griffin.news/dims4/default/847843f/2147483647/strip/true/crop/1280x720+0+0/resize/1440x810!/format/webp/quality/90/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fgriffin-media-production-griffin.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Fkwtv%2Fproduction%2F2019%2FApril%2F10%2Fnational-weather-service-has-concerns-over-presidents-budget.1554910082000.jpeg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.news9.com/oklahoma-city-news/national-weather-service-reports-nationwide-radar-website-outage",
